const e=["The theatrical arts of the capital are renowned as the finest under heaven. Here, at the very foot of the celestial throne, singing pavilions and dancing terraces grace every corner; within the bustling multitudes, people spend their days intoxicated by moonlight and evaluating the beauty of the flowers. Truly, its prosperity defies description and its myriad sentiments exceed depiction. The bizarre and wondrous sights encountered here, though strange, do not stray beyond the bounds of reason, yet they entirely touch the depths of human feeling. Thus, with a playful brush, I trace the lives of playful souls. Yet the rarest among them are those few gentlemen who love deeply while holding fast to propriety, alongside a few performers who keep their bodies pure and their self-respect intact—perfectly embodying the spirit of the Airs of the States: 'fond of beauty yet not licentious.' Let me first classify the young lords of the gentry into ten kinds, all united by the single word: feeling.","The first is feeling rooted in righteousness; the second, feeling lifted in eminence; the third, feeling elevated in loftiness; the fourth, feeling wandering in ease; the fifth, feeling shining in elegance; the sixth, feeling soaring in boldness; the seventh, feeling unfettered in wildness; the eighth, feeling sparkling in wit; the ninth, feeling settled in harmony; and the tenth, feeling steeped in joy. Let me then classify the celebrated dan performers of the pear orchard into ten kinds, similarly united by the single word: feeling.","The first is feeling at its most profound; the second, feeling touched by wisdom; the third, feeling woven with grace; the fourth, feeling distilled in purity; the fifth, feeling adorned in virtue; the sixth, feeling burning with ardor; the seventh, feeling tempered by integrity; the eighth, feeling flowing in abandon; the ninth, feeling glowing in radiance; and the tenth, feeling softening into charm. These are all figures of the highest caliber. There are, however, those of lower character to whom the word 'feeling' cannot be applied; they too may be classified into several types. The first is lewdness; the second, depravity; the third, cunning; the fourth, licentiousness; the fifth, greed; the sixth, the demonic; the seventh, the malevolent; and the eighth, the parasitic. Broadly speaking, from ancient times to the present, those who have invested their feelings in the quarters of pleasure have invariably tread one of two paths: the crooked or the straight. What my eyes have seen and my ears have heard, I have committed to writing, comprising sixty volumes in total. I title it 'Pinhua Baojian,' or alternatively, 'Yiqing Yishi.' Within this book, there are hosts and guests, neither clinging too closely nor drifting too far apart—like lotus roots severed yet joined by unseen threads, or like rich blossoms gathering beneath rolling clouds. Trite expressions have been rigorously expunged, betraying the author's untold labor; while fresh vistas are unfurled at every turn, offering the reader moments of spontaneous delight. As the proverb says: 'I embroider the mandarin ducks for you to admire, but I will not silently pass you the golden needle.'","The author of this book remains nameless, leaving the era, year, place, and identity forever unknown. The narrative begins by telling of a man utterly detached from feeling, who sires a son profoundly devoted to feeling. The father's surname is Mei, his personal name Shixie, and his courtesy name Tie'an, a native of Jinling in Jiangnan. Born into an illustrious lineage, he currently holds the post of Reader-in-Waiting at the Hanlin Academy, residing in Mingke Lane in the southern sector of the city. His grandfather, Ding, once served as Minister of Personnel, while his father, Gengdiao, was a Grand Secretary of the Wenhua Hall; for three generations, the family line has passed to a single heir. Shixie attained his jinshi degree at the age of seventeen and entered the Hanlin Academy; twenty-nine years have elapsed since then, making him forty-six this year. His family pedigree rivals the legendary Jin and Zhang clans, and his mastery of the classics honors the traditions of Ma and Zheng. Though of noble birth, he reveres the simplicity of the scholar; despite his high office at court, he shuns ostentatious display. His inner heart radiates the gentle warmth of spring, yet his outward demeanor commands the stern awe of autumn. Contemporaries liken him to the great figures Sima Guang and Zhao Bian. His wife, Lady Yan, also hails from a prominent Jinling family; she is the daughter of Yan Yaochen, Left Censor-in-Chief, and the younger sister of Yan Zhuang, a Hanlin Compiler, though both her father and brother have since passed away. Lady Yan, now forty-four, embodies a rare union of virtue and beauty, peerless in her gracious wisdom. She and Academician Mei have lived in harmonious partnership for over two decades. At the age of twenty-nine, she dreamed that a divine being bestowed upon her a piece of jade, and subsequently gave birth to a jade-like son, named Ziyu and styled Gengxiang. This Mei Ziyu is now seventeen, his countenance as flawless as fine jade, his essence as pure as refined gold—precious as a luminous pearl taking shape, radiant as the glorious moon cresting the peaks. Furthermore, endowed with heavenly intellect, his mind encompasses the constellations; though he has studied for merely ten years, he has already mastered ten thousand volumes. Two years ago, when Shixie took leave to sweep the ancestral tombs in the south, Ziyu accompanied him; he swiftly passed the local examinations to enter the county school. Though he did not succeed in the subsequent provincial examination, he returned to the capital with his father. Finding a journey south inconvenient, he enrolled as a senior student in the Imperial Academy and currently studies under Li Xingquan, a renowned scholar from Zhejiang. This Li Xingquan, a former examinee passed by Shixie in the provincial exams, is a strict neo-Confucian teacher of meticulous words and rigorous conduct. Lady Yan treasures her son beyond measure, keeping him close by her side whenever he is not at his books. Although the household employs numerous maids and serving women, she permits neither women under thirty nor maids over fifteen to wait upon Ziyu, fearing they might lead him astray. Yet Ziyu himself guards his purity like unblemished jade; though surrounded by rustling silks, he harbors no foppish habits. He adorns himself with no scented pouches yet remains naturally splendid, applies no powder yet shines with inherent brilliance. He devotes himself solely to honoring his teachers and choosing worthy friends, laboring bitterly over his studies; debating the present and investigating the past, his ambitions soar to the clouds. Even now, he enjoys the reputation of an auspicious star and a propitious cloud, with people deeming a single glimpse of him a profound joy.","One day, the teacher dismissed his students early to attend to some affairs, leaving Ziyu sitting in solitude, when two close companions arrived to visit him. One was surnamed Yan, named Zhongqing, and styled Jiantan; currently twenty-three years of age, he was the son of the late Compiler Yan Zhuang, making him Lady Yan's nephew.","When Yan Zhuang was alive, he shared not only the intimate bond of a brother-in-law with Shixie but also a profound friendship akin to that of Lei and Chen. Tragically, he was summoned to the Jade Pavilion at the mere age of thirty, and his wife, Lady Zheng, starved herself to death to preserve her chastity and follow him. At that time, Zhongqing was barely three years old; Shixie brought him into his home to raise him and petitioned the court to erect a memorial arch honoring Lady Zheng's fierce devotion. Zhongqing lived under Shixie's roof until the age of nineteen, when he passed the examinations as a supplementary candidate. That same year, Shixie acted as matchmaker, arranging for him to marry into the family of Wang Wenhui, the current Commissioner of the Transmission Office, who shared both a hometown and examination cohort with Shixie. As Wang Wenhui was Lady Yan's cousin, this union layered family ties upon family ties, and father-in-law and son-in-law found great harmony together. The other visitor was surnamed Shi, named Nanxiang, and styled Zhujun. Hailing from Hanyang in Huguang, he was twenty-four years old and had already claimed the title of Jieyuan—the top scholar in his provincial examination. His father, Shi Zengwang, currently served as a Supervising Secretary in the Ministry of Personnel. Both young men possessed towering intellects and learning profound enough to fill five carts, yet their temperaments could not have been more distinct. Zhongqing was solitary, lofty, and self-purifying, bearing a heart of open candor. If one compared his scholarship with Ziyu's, Ziyu followed the path of absolute purity, whereas Zhongqing pursued the path of expansive unorthodoxy. In matters of human sentiment and physical principles, Zhongqing grasped the broad outlines without seeking exhausting detail, while Ziyu probed the hidden depths, constantly refining his understanding to greater perfection.","Often, a subject that Zhongqing would dismiss with disdainful neglect, once meticulously expounded by Ziyu, would suddenly bloom with endless, subtle meaning. Conversely, a principle that eluded Ziyu's deepest searching could be illuminated by a single word from Zhongqing, instantly flooding the barren ground with brilliant light.","These two had spent over ten years in each other's company; the profound depth of their connection surpassed even the intimacy of blood brothers. As for Nanxiang, he was given to roaring with laughter and transcending mere form—a man of pure madness who stood utterly apart from the vulgar world. He looked down upon his entire generation, capable of writing ten thousand words while leaning against a horse, and deigned to appreciate only Ziyu and Zhongqing.","On this day, they came together to visit Ziyu. The gatekeepers, recognizing them as frequent guests and the young master's closest friends, ushered them straight into the study to meet him. Zhongqing first accompanied Ziyu into the inner chambers to pay his respects to his aunt, then returned to sit with Nanxiang. After the three had exchanged pleasantries, a pageboy served fragrant tea. Nanxiang noted the room's transcendent elegance, its furnishings exquisite and touched with antiquity, fully aware of Ziyu's extraordinary inner mind. Observing his noble and luminous bearing, he thought that even the figures recently celebrated in the 'The Opera Stage Flower Manual,' despite their physical beauty, lacked this divine spiritual bone. Yet, seeing Ziyu so modest, self-effacing, and seemingly reserved, Nanxiang wondered what true passions lay hidden within him. If he proved rigid, inflexible, and unyielding in his views, he could not be considered a complete talent. Resolving to test him, Nanxiang asked: 'Yuxiang, tell me, what in this world is most capable of pleasing the ear, delighting the eye, and stirring the soul?' Taken aback by the abrupt question, Ziyu gazed at Nanxiang and thought to himself: 'He is a man of pure, unrestrained elegance; his views will surely defy worldly conventions, and there must be a deeper reasoning behind his words.' He replied: 'Your question casts too wide a net. Though all men share the same ears and eyes, their temperaments diverge wildly. Some adore bustling prosperity, while others despise it; some seek cold tranquility, while others loathe it. For instance, Xie An found his spirit lifted by strings and woodwinds on the Eastern Mountain, yet another might find the same music a chaotic disturbance in his humble dwelling. One might banish moth-eyebrowed beauties and refuse their service, while another travels nowhere without concubines in tow. If the pleasures of the ear and eye are so varied, the things that stir the soul must be even harder to align. How can one person's senses and temperament dictate the senses and temperament of all?' Nanxiang shook his head. 'That is not my meaning. I am speaking of a specific kind of person.","The capital today is an endless sea of humanity. Suppose you meet a man of exalted rank and heavy prestige; to converse with him means exchanging official jargon and mouthing bureaucratic maxims, every inch from head to toe bound by official decorum—such a man inspires only dread. Suppose you meet a sour, pedantic scholar, his hands cramped and his feet bound, hunching his back and shrugging his shoulders as he groans in the pretense of deep contemplation—such a man is merely laughable. Suppose you meet a market vulgarity chasing after foul scents, haggling over silver and gold, debating market prices, exuding a stench of commonness that assaults the senses—such a man is simply detestable.","Suppose you encounter vulgar actors and indiscriminate prostitutes, with greased hair and powdered faces, utterly devoid of shame—such people are thoroughly hateful. Think about it: of all the figures that cross your vision, once you strip away these types, what kind of person remains?' Unable to guess his friend's true intent, Ziyu could only offer: 'Since true pleasure is not to be found in superficial sounds and sights, does it not reside solely in the day-and-night companionship of two or three kindred spirits with pure hearts?' Zhongqing burst into loud laughter. Nanxiang retorted: 'Absurd! How can one describe a friend as something that merely pleases the ear and delights the eye? Yuxiang, your heart harbors ill intentions.' With that, he laughed uproariously. Disconcerted by their laughter, Ziyu felt a faint blush touch his cheeks and protested: 'Cease your mockery. My meaning was this: engaged in pure conversation while waving a deer-tail whisk, admiring the graceful bearing of those in dark robes—does this not please the ear and delight the eye?","Does not rich, heart-intoxicating wine, or an ancient sword reflecting a hero's courage—do these not stir the soul within the bonds of friendship?' Nanxiang smiled and said: 'Your words never leave the confines of this single room. Your vision is not broad, and your wanderings have yet to transform you.' With that, he drew a book from his boot and handed it to Ziyu, saying: 'This is my recent printing. Those capable of pleasing the ear, delighting the eye, and stirring the soul are, I daresay, to be found among these few gentlemen.' Zhongqing chuckled: 'Presenting this book to Yuxiang for his judgment is rather like Su Qin's first audience with the King of Qin; I guarantee you could submit it ten times and he would still remain unconvinced. Not only has he failed to grasp the flavor of these sentiments, he has never even laid eyes on such people. How can you expect him to instantly comprehend it?' Seeing their earnestness, Ziyu wondered what extraordinary book this could be. He opened it and saw the title: 'Qutai Huaxuan.' It began with several prefaces, all written in ornate, parallel prose. Nanxiang urged him to skip the prefaces and proceed directly to the selected individuals. Ziyu noted that the first entry read:","Yuan Baozhu: A Pearl Tree of the Jade Pavilion. Baozhu's surname is Yuan, his courtesy name Yaoqing, and he is sixteen years of age. A native of Suzhou, he belongs to the Lianjin troupe. He is highly skilled in painting and fluent in poetry. When he performs pieces such as 'The Magpie Bridge,' 'The Secret Vow,' 'Waking from a Dream,' and 'Pursuing the Dream,' his radiant beauty eclipses the bright dawn clouds, shining with the clarity of immortal dew. Truly, he would make Yang Yuhuan lose her favor and render Du Liniang pale by comparison. His slender voice holds back the clouds; his tender feeling flows like water. A single melody of 'Nishang' resurrects the romantic splendor of the Tianbao era; a return to his spring dream composes the melancholy yearning of a fragrant boudoir. In daily life, he exudes a pure, shimmering aura, speaking in soft, gentle whispers; he cultivates a spirit as clear as ice and guards his body as pure as jade. Such a one deserves to dwell in a jadeite tower built behind a hall of curcuma flowers. Thus, I present him with this poem:",`Your dancing sleeves drift light and frail, too delicate to bear;
It is hard to liken such clarity even to the moon upon the water.
Since the character for 'Pearl' became the name that graces you,
It has multiplied the luster of pearls a hundredfold.
Your slender, supple waist is desperately pitiful;
Your lifelong love of elegance springs from nature alone.
Your romantic grace holds a separate, soul-melting charm;
Only now do I believe an exiled immortal walks the mortal realm.

Ziyu smiled and said, 'Are these not descriptions of young dan actors in the theatrical troupes? Which troupe boasts such an actor, that you praise him so highly?' Zhongqing replied, 'He is right here in the capital; did you not read that he belongs to the Lianjin troupe?' Ziyu countered, 'I don't believe it; Zhujun is making this up. I spent a day watching plays this year, and when did I ever see someone so extraordinary among the young dan?' Nanxiang retorted, 'Who knows what miserable troupe you watched that day; naturally, you saw no one worthwhile.' Ziyu then looked at the second entry, which read: Su Huifang: A Jade Moon over the Jasper Terrace. Huifang's surname is Su, his courtesy name Meixiang, and he is seventeen years of age. A native of Suzhou.`,"Originally the son of an official family, he drifted into the pear orchard through the misfortunes of wandering, and now belongs to the Lianjin troupe. Autumn waters form his spirit; jade blossoms form his bones. He is accomplished in poetry, reveres moral integrity, and excels in tactful adaptability. Possessing a uniquely inventive intellect, his art rivals the work of heaven, and his beauty and skill reign supreme in his generation.","In his performances of plays like 'The Jade Terrace,' 'Autumn at the Tray,' and 'The Pavilion Meeting,' one truly witnesses a fragrant heart seeming to pour out its grievances, a delicate grace on the verge of flowing over. The feast-worthy loveliness of Wu Jiangxian and the jade-immersing cold springs of Zhao Hede—young Su possesses both simultaneously. He once remarked to someone: 'It was my misfortune to fall into the pear orchard, but since I have taken up this profession, I must find peace within it. Who decrees that in this realm one cannot guard his purity and uphold his chastity, but must instead drift with the current and torment himself?' Such is his resolve. And his sweeping sentiments and transcendent bearing find scarcely an equal anywhere. Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`Your romantic grace amidst the woods has long been widely sung,
Young Su, by nature, monopolizes this singular art.
A single melody of pure song leaves its resonance winding round the rafters;
Heavenly blossoms tumble wildly, scenting your dancing robes.
The flutes and pipes upon the stage seem almost to blush with shame,
As you temporarily exchange your immortal bones for tender grace.
A swirling mass of crimson snow scatters upon the wind,
Scattering to become the sorrow of lovers for a thousand autumns.

He then looked at the third entry, which read:`,"Lu Sulan: A Coral Branch in the Azure Sea. Sulan's surname is Lu, his courtesy name Xiangwan, and he is sixteen years of age. A native of Suzhou.","He belongs to the Lianjin troupe. He possesses jade bones and ice-pure skin, an embroidered heart and a brocaded mouth. He excels at calligraphy; even a scrap of paper or a foot of silk bearing his writing is fought over by scholar-officials, treasured as highly as a large jade disc. His benevolent heart is profoundly deep, his essence more fragrant than sunken aloeswood; his exemplary virtue is refined, his beauty eclipsing the moon above a forest of jade. He frequently performs plays such as 'Composing the Score,' 'Dancing on the Tray,' 'The Small Banquet,' and 'The Pavilion of Gossamer,' appearing for all the world as another Yang Guifei reincarnate. Even if Chen Hong were to write his biography, he could not fully depict his voice and countenance; even if Bai Juyi were to compose a song, it would barely capture his likeness. He thirsts for righteousness and shuns evil as his mortal enemy. He is truly one who guards the flawless purity of a white jade scepter and maintains the austere chastity of un-dyed silk. The captivating charm of his graceful bearing is merely his lingering resonance. Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`A lotus rises from the water, revealing a rosy face;
Both plump and slender suit him well, blending Feiyan and Yuhuan.
If only the men of today had enacted the deeds of the past,
Surely no barbarian steeds would have breached the mountain pass.
This melody should exist only in the heavens above;
I know not where it fell to grace the mortal dust.
If I were the Emperor of Tang in the days of Tianbao,
I would gladly trade my rivers and mountains for such a beauty.

He then looked at the fourth entry, which read:`,"Jin Shufang: Luminous Snow on Mount Qian. Shufang's surname is Jin, his courtesy name Shouxiang, and he is fifteen years of age. A native of Suzhou, he belongs to the Lianzhu troupe. His elegant bones are delicate and translucent; his tender feelings flow in continuous pulses. He is accomplished in poetry and playing the flute, skilled at chess, and possesses the distinct, refined air of a scholar in seclusion. He performs the greatest number of plays, but the one for which he is most celebrated is 'Inscribing the Song.'","Truly, his sandalwood lips exude fragrance, and his unadorned waist sways like a willow. Compared to a newly opened begonia or a jasmine just beginning to bloom, his realm of color and fragrance is so profound it could almost force an immortal to fall into worldly tribulation. His performances in other plays, such as 'Flirting with a Zither' and 'The Autumn River,' bring emotional resonance vividly to life, an achievement beyond the reach of others. His fragrant heart is profoundly gentle; he is beautiful without and wise within. He is truly an immortal in charge of the books in the heavenly Langhuan library; how could one expect to encounter him within the chrysanthemum ranks of the pear orchard? Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`A slender, fragile slip of iridescent cloud takes flight;
Flowing snow and swirling wind—where will they find their rest?
With abundant fragrant golden threads, your dancing robes are heavy;
You ought always to wear the weightless garments of the gods.
The lotus yields to your face, the willow to your waist;
You perfectly suit the golden step-sway hairpin on your floral crown.
Even if you were entirely devoid of feeling and offered not a word,
Merely pacing your narrow steps upon the stage is enough to melt the soul.

He then looked at the fifth entry, which read:`,"Li Yulin: A Jade Tree Facing the Wind. Yulin's surname is Li, his courtesy name Peixian, and he is fifteen years of age. A native of Yangzhou.","He belongs to the Lianzhu troupe. He is like a lotus greeting the morning sun, or a willow swaying in the dawn breeze. Fluent in poetry, skilled in strings and woodwinds, his mastery of Go and the card game Madiao are both absolutely unparalleled in his time. Su Dongpo's poem 'Crabapple' reads: 'With a captivating smile amidst the bamboo fence, the peaches and plums covering the mountains are entirely vulgar.' Within his gentle and enchanting demeanor, he naturally possesses an unyielding resolve; he is truly an exceptional beauty. His performance of the scene 'Willow at Yang Pass' brought him immense fame throughout the capital. Seeing his graceful tenderness and his sorrowful, beautiful yearning is like witnessing the very life of Huo Xiaoyu; there is no need to even mention plays like 'Selling the Hairpin' or 'Dividing the Shoes'—one already hates the swordsman in the yellow shirt for failing to slay that heartless lover. When he performs 'Hiding the Boat,' 'Grass Ground,' and 'Sending the Fan,' his deep emotions are utterly moving. Truly, he is like a jade tree morning after morning, with golden lotus steps at every turn, possessing the lingering charm of the pavilions of Linchun and Jieqi. Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`Your long, trailing dancing sleeves are as breathtaking as the dawn clouds;
Your makeup complete, your clouded hair coiled in sweeping curves.
Attendants assist you up into the Pavilion of Approaching Spring;
You mean to rival Zhang Lihua of the Southern Dynasties.
With absolute brilliance and a fragrant heart, when the wine is half-drunk,
Your subtle charm mirrors the slender moon just past its third night.
Most moving of all is the melody of 'Yang Pass';
It leaves the departing traveler utterly unable to bear his grief.

He then looked at the sixth entry, which read:`,"Wang Lanbao: Silver Flowers on a Fiery Tree. Lanbao's surname is Wang, his courtesy name Jingfang, and he is seventeen years of age. A native of Yangzhou. He belongs to the Lianjin troupe. He glides like a startled swan and curves like a swimming dragon. Well-versed in literature and highly skilled in martial arts, his temperament is exceptionally fierce; he refuses to bow to the wealthy and powerful. He is truly a resonant voice among jade.","When he performs plays like 'Double Red Record,' 'Stealing the Token,' and 'The Green Gate,' he combs his hair into a dark tribal topknot pierced with a golden sparrow hairpin. He wears a purple robe trimmed with melted gold, cinched by a red embroidered sash, and small brocade boots. Bearing twin dragon-patterned swords on his back, he blazes like fire and blooming reeds, dazzling like brocade and clouds—truly the reincarnation of Hongxian. In plays such as 'Slaying the Tiger,' 'Stealing the Token,' and 'Killing in the Boat,' his chivalrous spirit surges forth, as if one is witnessing a heroine taking the fate of the world upon her own shoulders. Yet, when he senses the fragrant powder of his lover and whispers in soft intimacy, he becomes a truly infatuated maiden. Amidst his gentle enchantment and radiant splendor, to encounter this gentleman's sudden intensity is like listening to Emperor Minghuang striking the Jie drum, playing the 'Three Beats of Yuyang'—a profound resonance that instantly causes ten thousand flowers to burst into bloom. Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`Chivalrous bones and a tender heart are rare in this world,
Yet he is willing to lean against the balustrade with red sleeves.
A lifelong confidant requires no explicit instructions;
Just carefully examine the dragon patterns on his blade.
A riot of five colors rises like the morning clouds;
The incessant beating of the war drums redoubles his spirit.
When the play is done and his makeup removed, he stands with hands at his sides,
Standing tall and graceful—a single tree of verdant peach blossoms.

He then looked at the seventh entry, which read:`,"Wang Guibao: Lotus in the Autumn Waters. Guibao is the younger brother of Lanbao; his courtesy name is Ruixiang, and he is fifteen years of age, belonging to the same troupe as his brother. He exudes the fragrance of an orchid and is like a flower that understands human speech. His bright eyes are adept at casting enchanting glances; his gleaming teeth breathe out fragrance. His playfulness springs from natural innocence, and his coy naivety generates endless charm. He is skilled with brush and ink, proficient with the ivory clappers, and delights in playing drinking games and various board games. He is deeply empathetic to the feelings of others; even the lonely and melancholic find their hearts fully opened upon seeing him. His demeanor and posture are alluring, yet he sets his own boundaries. When he performs the scene 'Feigning Jealousy,' his fragrant sweat and flushed cheeks are truly enough to make Pan An die of infatuation. When he performs small scenes like 'The Vow,' 'The Rendezvous,' 'Demanding the Hairpin,' and 'Interrogating the Beauty,' he is like a delicate bird teasing the clear sky, his sidelong glances deeply captivating. Spectators stand packed several layers deep, leaving no room even to sit in the towering galleries. Contemporaries comment that while Yuan and Su are like the 'Nishang' dance, he is the 'Purple Cloud Return'—their charm is different, but their wonder is one and the same. Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`At a graceful fifteen, he is already a master of romance;
With a clever smile and sidelong glances, he knows not how to be shy.
I love most his coy naivety, utterly without pretense;
Retreating to an empty place, he mimics the melancholy of spring.
I wish to stand before the feast and beg for the Purple Cloud;
For a time, his price and fame are broadcast far and wide.
When the red ivory clappers strike the soul-melting note,
Sandalwood lips sing a clear song above a white silk skirt.

He then looked at the eighth entry, which read:`,"Lin Chunxi: A Jade Qilin from the Heavens. Chunxi's surname is Lin, his courtesy name Xiaomei, and he is fourteen years of age. A native of Suzhou.","He belongs to the Lianjin troupe. Like a fine flower still within its calyx, or a luminous pearl just emerging from the womb. He entered the troupe at twelve; though a mere two years have passed, he is already highly accomplished in music and rhythm, while also proficient in literature, performing both sheng (male) and dan (female) roles. The plays he performs—such as 'Entrusting the Son,' 'Stored Admonitions,' 'Returning from the Hunt,' 'Breaking the Loom,' 'Barbarian Child,' 'Underworld Inquest,' and 'The Female Musician'—display his long eyebrows and elegant cheeks. He looks like a young noble of the Wuyi Lane, wearing a purple silk fragrant pouch; truly a child of powder and fragrance, inspiring intense admiration. His very eating and drinking are a sight to behold. In a few years' time, he will surely rise above all others, his worth equal to multiple cities. Thus, I composed for him this poem:",`There exists a different kind of powdered youth in the mortal realm;
Melted gold forms his ornaments, and jade forms his adornment.
The stone Qilin from the heavens is intrinsically priceless;
He should be holding the incense burner, waiting upon the Jade Emperor.
He has barely trilled his singing throat, yet praise flows without end;
Gold is eagerly thrown to form his silken headband.
When young lord Wang occasionally rides out in his goat-drawn cart,
Ten miles of pearl curtains are rolled up all at once.

Ziyu read it and merely smiled, offering not a single word. Nanxiang asked, 'Why do you withhold your judgment?' Ziyu replied, 'Generally speaking, when evaluating people, though some embellishment is unavoidable, one should not stray too far from the truth. As for evaluating this lot, it is truly a waste of your brush and ink.`,"I believe that among such people, there absolutely cannot be a flawless piece of jade. Since they serve others with their looks, if they do not seek to charm, they must seek to flatter. Furthermore, shifting their allegiances from morning to night, amusing themselves with wine and food, forcing smiles and feigning joy, their only true love is the money thrown at them. Since their bodies can hardly remain pure, their aspirations are correspondingly far too base. Add to this that they are born in poverty and raised in filth; their exposure is narrow, and their minds grow smaller by the day. What they study is how to bend the knee and wear a slavish face; what they excel at is frivolous banter and arrogant jesting. Even if their makeup is expertly applied and their mimicry captures the correct posture, they are merely donning the hide of a Qilin or acting as a wordless stele when they take the stage.","How could there be one who emerges from the mud without being stained, or drifts along the wild current without being pulled under? Moreover, even if they possess a tolerable appearance or a single specialized skill, it is like unraveling the threads of a brocade sock—useless for sewing a garment. It is like forging a knife from leaden water—unfit for chopping wood. Their internal organs are foul and turbid; their speech lacks order and refinement.","Their bones lack culture; their every movement is vulgar. Thus, though their coloring may be beautiful, it is not radiant; though their skin may be white, it is not pure; though their spirit may be pretty, it is not clear; though their aura may be soft, it is not elegant. Afflicted by these several flaws, how can they be considered excellent? As for the delicate maidens of the red boudoirs and the beautiful women hidden in golden houses, their nature is rooted in pure yin, and their bodies contain ultimate stillness. Therefore, their bones are soft, their skin smooth, their flesh pure, and their blood vibrant. Their spirits are tranquil; their demeanor graceful and refined. Their eyebrows and eyes naturally reveal a clear brightness, and their voices naturally form a delicate sweetness. Their posture and movements are wondrously natural. The shadow of their hair and the fragrance of their garments require no artificial fabrication—only then can they be called beauties, called exquisite women. Now, to place them on the red carpet to enact the peerless beauties of antiquity is truly, as the saying goes, trying to paint Wuyan to look like Xishi, deeply insulting the true beauty of the past.",`That is why I am unwilling to watch plays featuring young dan; I would rather watch the painted-face, secondary male, old man, and clown roles, which can instead ease the mind and provoke hearty laughter. My elder brother, you spare no ink and spare no effort in your extravagant exaggerations to add glory to this lot, while leaving the ancients to harbor regrets. I secretly feel there is something lacking in your approach.' This speech infuriated Shi Nanxiang. Zhongqing laughed and said, 'Yuxiang's argument is not without merit, yet Zhujun's selections are also quite fair. It is just that Yuxiang does not realize that such individuals exist in the universe. It is like reading "In Search of the Supernatural" or books of "Hidden Marvels";`,'if one insists on making people truly believe they exist, who would readily accept it? It is impossible unless one sees them in person. Tomorrow, we will take him out and point out one or two of them for him to see; only then will he believe that your "Selection of Flowers" has made no mistake.',`I believe that once Yuxiang sees these people, he too will be able to appreciate them. The spiritual essence of heaven and earth—where does it not gather? To say it gathers only in women and not in men is an untenable generalization. Yuxiang just claimed that men are foul and turbid, incapable of matching the spiritual elegance of women. When it comes to those called beauties and exquisite individuals, I believe there have been quite a few beautiful men since ancient times, and several instances of men being called beauties or exquisite individuals. For example, in the Mao Shi: "That beautiful person!"; in Du Fu's poetry: "Why is the beauty separated by autumn waters?"; in the "Rhapsody on the Red Cliff": "Gazing at the beauty at the edge of the sky." As for men being called exquisite individuals, consider the "Songs of Chu": "Thinking of the exquisite person's long capital," where the commentary states: "Exquisite person refers to King Huai." In the "Book of Later Han," the Director of the Secretariat, Lu Hong, possessed a countenance like jade. Emperor Guangwu sighed and said, "The South has many exquisite people." In the "History of Jin," when Tao Kan attacked Du Tao, he said to Du's subordinate Wang Gong, "You are originally an exquisite person; why do you follow rebels?" Furthermore, there are instances of women calling men exquisite individuals. For example, during the Former Qin dynasty, Dou Tao's wife Su Hui created the "Revolving Brocade Picture" (Xuanji Tu), which readers could not fully decipher. Su sighed and said, "Unless he is my exquisite person, no one can understand it." It is evident that exquisite beauty does not belong exclusively to women. It is not necessarily true that there are no peerless beauties among men; consider Li Gu scratching his head and striking a pose during the reign of Emperor Chong of Han, or Zhang Yizhi applying rouge and powder during the reign of Empress Wu of Tang. It is clear that Pan An and Wei Jie were not the only ones to dazzle their respective eras.' Hearing this, Ziyu felt his heart slightly moved. Nanxiang added, 'And it does not stop there. Vegetation facing the sun flourishes, while that in the shade withers. The southern branches of the plum blossom bloom first, the northern branches later. Consider the phoenix, the mandarin duck, the peacock, the wild pheasant, and the domestic rooster—the birds with brilliant plumage are always male. It is clear that the creative energy of nature gathers first in the male, and only later in the female. While women are certainly beautiful, they inevitably rely somewhat on makeup and powder. How can they compare to men who, without applying lead powder, possess a natural radiance? There is an even simpler way to understand this. I will use you as a living example: would you really claim your own countenance is lacking? If you were to call the maids in your house right now and look together in a mirror, you would naturally see the difference. You would absolutely not say they are beautiful and feel ashamed of your own inadequacy. With just this one sentence, you should understand.' Ziyu unwittingly blushed, carefully thinking that there was indeed much reason in these words. Could there truly be such fine figures among the young dan? He then thought again: the universe is vast; what does it not contain? Must heaven rigidly select only certain people to endow with beautiful materials? Even Xishi once washed silk in poverty, and Yang Guifei served as a Daoist nun. Indeed, among the famous beauties of history, half emerged from brothels and winding alleys. Perhaps heaven naturally produces such individuals to delight the hearts and eyes of the mortal world; it is not impossible. But to boast that they protect their purity, hold extraordinary ambitions, strictly choose their associates, remain unmoved by the lure of profit, are well-versed in literature, and rarely engage in debauchery—that remains unbelievable. He seemed lost in thought, remaining entirely silent. Nanxiang laughed wildly for a moment and said, 'It is hard to count Yuxiang as a knowing friend right now. I shall go seek the judgment of others.'`,"He then dragged Zhongqing away. Ziyu saw his guests out and returned, silently pondering Nanxiang's 'Selection of Flowers' once more. He thought back to the plays he had seen in the past and the young dan he had encountered, finding that they did not match the descriptions in the slightest. He still concluded that Nanxiang was speaking wildly, using this merely for his own amusement, and so he put the matter out of his mind. Teacher Li returned; Ziyu studied in the study for a while longer before Lady Yan called him inside.","Two days later, Ziyu requested a half-day's leave after breakfast and went back to visit Nanxiang and Zhongqing.","Having informed his mother, Lady Yan noticed that the weather was freezing that day, with a sharp north wind blowing, and it being mid-winter, she had a servant woman bring out a grape-patterned lynx fur coat for him to wear. She also ordered the carriage interior changed to a warm white fox-fur lining. Two young pages, one named Yun'er and the other Jun'er, rode ahead on horseback. First, they went to the residence of his maternal uncle, the Commissioner Wang. As it happened, the Commissioner had gone out, but his eldest son came out to welcome him in. This young master of the Wang family had the single name Xun, was styled Yong'an, and was precisely twenty-two years old.","He possessed an extraordinary, handsome, and elegant appearance. His literary talents were profoundly accomplished, and his nature was exceptionally generous and sincere. He had purchased the title of a senior student at the Imperial Academy and was participating in the northern provincial examinations. He and Ziyu were cousins and sworn, inseparable friends.","He welcomed Ziyu inside. First, they went to the inner quarters to pay respects to his maternal aunt, Lady Lu. This lady was Wenhui's second wife and was only forty years old this year. He also greeted Wang Xun's wife, Lady Sun, his cousin-in-law. Zhongqing's wife, Ronghua, was his older female cousin. There was also a Miss Qionghua who did not come out to meet him; having heard her father praise Ziyu's qualities a few days prior, and sensing his tone suggested arranging a marriage between them, she felt shy and refused to come out to meet this cousin. Lady Lu felt her affection grow every time she saw Ziyu; she kept him seated, asked a few everyday questions, and then Ziyu took his leave.",`Afterward, he accompanied Wang Xun to the study and asked after Zhongqing, learning that he had been invited out by Gao Pin and Nanxiang. Ziyu mentioned that the 'Selection of Flowers' he had seen Nanxiang carrying the other day was excessively exaggerated. Wang Xun replied, 'As for Zhujun's "Selection of Flowers," practically speaking, one should rather fear it falls short; how could it be considered an exaggeration? Now that those treasures have received this evaluation, their prestige has grown even further. You really ought to have seen these people.' Hearing this, Ziyu realized that Wang Xun also had a fondness for young dan and was prone to following the crowd, so he dropped the subject.`,`Wang Xun continued, 'What do you think of Zhujun's "Selection of Flowers"? Was the selection unfair, or too narrow, leaving behind uncollected pearls? There are certainly other good ones out there. But they simply cannot compare to these eight; these are the flawless bronze coins of ten thousand selections. If one were to fully articulate their virtues, it would require writing a separate biographical chronology for each to make it clear. These few words barely outline the broad strokes.' Ziyu felt highly intrigued in his heart. 'Could such people truly exist now?' He thought again, 'These three men do not easily praise others. How is it that when speaking of these few young dan, their hearts and mouths are so aligned? I must see them with my own eyes to believe it; otherwise, it is merely their shared prejudice.' He then said, 'I happen not to listen to plays often, and so I am poor at seeking out such figures. Why don't you take me to hear a couple of plays and broaden my horizons?' Wang Xun agreed, 'Very well.' He ordered a carriage prepared and horses saddled, and they changed into casual attire. Ziyu also had Yun'er fetch a casual hat to change into. Wang Xun noted, 'The "Selection of Flowers" lists six from Lianjin and only two from Lianzhu, so naturally we shall go hear Lianjin.' They then headed to the theater together. Upon entering, Ziyu saw a sea of humanity, the theater packed to the brim, and felt a surge of regret, not wishing to go further. Wang Xun guided him, squeezing sideways through the gaps in the crowd to the edge of the stage. Looking around, Ziyu saw not a single decent-looking person sitting in the pit; only upstairs and downstairs were there a few who looked somewhat presentable. The usher, seeing two wealthy young masters arrive with servants carrying wolf-skin cushions behind them, quickly cleared a table, laid out the cushions, seated them, and served tea and lighting materials. At that moment, they were performing 'The Romance of the Three Kingdoms.' The gongs and drums deafened the heavens, creating a tremendous racket. Wang Xun looked carefully but could not spot a single one of those six young dan, nor even any of the middle-tier ones. The people milling about them were all dark-faced young male escorts, flowing endlessly in all directions looking for wealthy patrons to buy them meals.`,"Ziyu watched the tedious play for a while, only to see a man at a table over there calling out to Wang Xun. Wang Xun turned his body to converse with the man. Then, Ziyu saw another man walking over. He wore a gray old fox-fur coat and a pair of loose black boots with mud-caked edges. Looking at his build, he was broad and flat, around thirty years old, with drooping shoulders and a dazed, sluggish expression. He squeezed past right beside Ziyu. A moment later, he squeezed back in the other direction. In a short span, he passed back and forth three or four times. Every time he drew near, he would inevitably shoot a glance at Ziyu, then look at Wang Xun, pausing his steps slightly, as if intending to greet Wang Xun. Wang Xun, deep in lively conversation with the other man, paid this fellow no mind, so the man had no choice but to walk on and squeeze his way elsewhere.","Ziyu felt incredibly annoyed, as if sitting on burning coals. Wang Xun finished his conversation and sat back properly. Ziyu wanted to suggest they return. Before he could speak, he saw a man leading an escort boy, approaching with a beaming smile. He offered two greetings, then squeezed into the middle of the table and sat down. Wang Xun didn't recognize him either. Ziyu looked at the boy, who seemed about fifteen or sixteen, with a dull, heavy head and two large, prominent cheekbones on his face. Though his face was white, his hands were dark. He actually reached out to touch Ziyu's hand and asked for his esteemed surname. Ziyu was highly reluctant to answer him.","He heard Wang Xun ask the man, 'What is this boy's name?' The man replied, 'He's called Baozhu.' Hearing this, Ziyu couldn't suppress a laugh. He then heard Wang Xun ask, 'Aren't you with Guibao?' The man answered, 'There are too many people at Guibao's place; he left there a few days ago. This Baozhu lives right next door to Guibao. Will the young master call for Baozhu to serve him today?' Wang Xun offered a noncommittal reply. That Baozhu then grabbed Wang Xun's hand and asked, 'Where are we going? It's about time.' Wang Xun said, 'Another day.' The boy then pestered Wang Xun, wanting him to take him out to eat. Ziyu truly could sit there no longer, and fearing Wang Xun might drag him along, decided it was best to leave first. He told Yun'er to go check the carriage. Yun'er came back a moment later and said, 'It's ready.' Ziyu immediately said to Wang Xun, 'I am going back.' Knowing Ziyu couldn't stand it, and feeling rather bored himself, Wang Xun said,","'We arrived too late today; let's rest a day and come earlier next time.' They then left together. Wang Xun's servant paid the theater fee. That escort boy still tugged at Wang Xun, walking a few steps with him, but seeing no prospect of being taken out to eat, eventually left on his own. Ziyu and Wang Xun boarded their carriages and went their separate ways home.","Ziyu couldn't stop laughing to himself all the way. 'Why do these people turn their minds so hazy and blind, failing to distinguish black from white, all for a few young dan? If a truly peerless beauty were to appear right now, I fear they would actually say he looked terrible.' Lost in these thoughts along the way, his carriage suddenly became wedged in a crowded bottleneck. Ziyu caught a waft of a pure, fresh fragrance—neither orchid nor musk—and peeked out through the glass window on the curtain. He saw a carriage opposite them. Inside sat an elderly man, and on the outside sat two wondrous young boys, both no more than fourteen or fifteen.","One was already like a crabapple blossom, incomparably delicate and radiant, his features perfectly shaped by nature. The other was truly a divine being from the heavens, a peerless beauty in the mortal world. He had jade for bones and the moon for a soul; flowers formed his emotions, and the glowing aura of pearls and jewels formed his spirit. Ziyu was stunned silly. Without realizing it, he lifted the curtain and stared intently. Those two wondrous boys also gazed back at him with clear, unblinking eyes. The one of peerless beauty seemed to stare even more intently, his gaze fixed on Ziyu, lost in thought. Ziyu felt his heart sway and his eyes dazzle. A layer of radiant light seemed to project from the peerless boy's face, scattering into an exotic fragrance that filled Ziyu's nose.","Just as he was absorbed in this beautiful sight, the carriage passed by. Behind it followed three or four more carriages, also carrying young children, though they were quite ordinary. Ziyu felt a sense of hazy confusion rising in his mind; it was as if he had seen this person's face before, as if he resembled someone, but he couldn't quite remember who.","He thought to himself, 'Who are these children? They look like they belong to a theater troupe, yet their clothes are not extravagant. That one could truly be called a rarity across all ages, unmatched under heaven. Since he possesses such beautiful features, why is his attire so unexceptional? Such circumstances truly wrong him. He ought to be housed in the Guanghan Palace of the moon; merely placing him in a hall of curcuma or a jadeite tower would hardly do him justice.",`Looking at it this way, the phrase "universally appreciated" has indeed proven to be an empty boast. To compare that Baozhu from earlier to this one—even using him as a footstool would be an insult.' Ziyu pondered this all the way home. To know what happens next, please listen to the explanation in the following chapter.`],l=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations1:e},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),a={10:["To resume our tale: after Ziyu and the other guests had dispersed, Xu Ziyun at last returned. Seeing that the night had already grown deep and the hour approached midnight, he made his way directly to his private residence.","Ever since Qin Yan first paid his respects to Ziyun the previous year, he had accompanied the other celebrated beauties in their daily visits to the Yiyuan garden, where Ziyun favored him no less than he did Baozhu. Yet Qin Yan was of an aloof and arrogant nature, preferring solitude to social pleasantries; though one might offer him a full measure of gold, it could not buy so much as a single smile. Whatever antiques, delicacies, or fine garments caught his fancy, Xu Ziyun provided without hesitation, treating him with the utmost devotion. Qin Yan was not ungrateful for this kindness, yet he regarded Ziyun as only half a kindred spirit. From the day of his arrival in the capital, when he had encountered Ziyu on the road and recognized him as the savior from his dream of the hidden pit, Ziyu's image had remained constantly in his thoughts. Then, on the day of the opera at the Gusu Huiguan, seeing Ziyu seated among a company of young gentlemen that included Wei Pincai and Li Yuanmao, he inquired about Ye Maolin and learned that this young master's surname was indeed Mei, fulfilling the omen beneath the plum tree. From that moment, a thread of tender feeling, like willow catkins clinging to the mud, had hopelessly entangled his heart.","Over these past few evenings, he had dreamed of Ziyu several times. In a haze of reverie, they would either be laughing together or shedding tears in each other's presence.",`In another dream, it seemed as though he were traveling far away, with Ziyu seeing him off, holding his sleeve and clasping his hand. Then the journey seemed to have already taken place, and he had returned, the two of them sitting close with knees touching, pouring out their hearts. The images were blurred and indistinct, and upon waking he could not recall them clearly. Although he knew Ziyu was a scion of a great family, he remained entirely ignorant of his temperament and predilections, or how he might compare to Duxiang. He harbored the fear that Ziyu might be one of those young men of wealth who were shallow, heartless, and fickle in their affections, or perhaps an arrogant lord of luxury who demanded constant fawning. Yet studying the warmth in his bearing, he seemed a man of genuine goodness who surely could not be so base. Then one day he dreamed that Baozhu had taken on Ziyu's appearance and performed the scene of the 'Waking from a Dream' with him, leaving him utterly bewildered. The following day, when Ziyun came to the garden, Cixian spoke at length of the young gentlemen who had come to view the lanterns the previous evening, praising Ziyu's talent and appearance with great effusiveness. Hearing this, Ziyun was inwardly quite pleased and remarked: "This Mei Yuxiang, though he does not know me, happened to cross my path last year. Our families share old ties, for his late paternal grandfather, the Grand Secretary, and my late great-uncle, the Censor-in-Chief, were classmates and the closest of friends. Mei Yuxiang's outward appearance leaves absolutely no room for criticism, but I know nothing of his inner character." Cixian replied: "He is as steadfast as gold, as smooth and warm as jade, devoid of the slightest inclination to flaunt his talents or assert himself. Though we were together only a brief time, I have already recognized his extraordinary quality." The two conversed for a long while, and Ziyun did not go out that day. By the hour of You, Baozhu arrived at the garden accompanied by Qin Yan. Seeing them, Ziyun laughed and said: "Yu Nong, things have turned out wonderfully for you this time. I have found your perfect match, so do not forget me when the time comes." This unexpected remark so startled Qin Yan that he immediately flared up. Unable to hold back the tears streaming from his eyes, he declared: "Duxiang, you have treated me with such immense kindness, never looking upon me as a lowly creature, and I feel your generous grace deeply. Even if I have failed in some service to you, and you are angry with me, hate me, scold me, or drive me away, I would not dare to harbor resentment. But there is no need to bring someone in to humiliate me. May I ask what this talk of 'finding a match' is supposed to mean? I demand a clear explanation." Ziyun, realizing he had spoken too rashly, felt thoroughly embarrassed and had no reply. He had no choice but to ask Baozhu to accompany and comfort Qin Yan with gentle words, before using the pretext of viewing paintings to slip away to Cixian's room.`,`Here, Yuan Baozhu used a handkerchief to wipe away the tear-stains on Qin Yan's face. He then described Shi Nanxiang's drunken antics and his adorned appearance in such amusing detail that Qin Yan's spirits lifted, and they sat together on the daybed. Baozhu remarked: "Among those who solved the lantern riddles yesterday, there was one named Mei Yuxiang, no more than seventeen or eighteen years of age, who possessed the most exceptional appearance of them all." Qin Yan asked: "Is his surname also Mei?" Baozhu replied: "He even asked after you." Pondering for a moment, Qin Yan said: "This Mei person, did he mention having met me before?"`,`Baozhu replied: "That is precisely what was so strange about it. Because he asked after you alone, I naturally assumed you must have shared drinks together at some point. When I asked if he was on intimate terms with you, he hedged a bit, saying something along the lines of 'not previously acquainted, though I have long admired him by reputation,' which hardly sounded like someone who had actually met you. He also mentioned that he found your performance of the 'Waking from a Dream' scene exceedingly fine." Qin Yan mused: "Could this Mei-surnamed gentleman be the very Mei young master who watched the opera that day?" Consequently, he asked Baozhu: "This Mei young master, was he the one sitting in the upper eastern gallery of the Gusu Huiguan on the sixth day of the first month?" Baozhu laughed: "I was not performing that day, so how could I possibly know whether it was him or not?" Qin Yan sat lost in thought for a long while before asking Baozhu again: "Does his appearance roughly resemble that of Lu Xiangwan from our troupe? Except that the eyes are somewhat longer and seem to radiate a brilliant light, and the nose is a little straighter, lending the whole face an air of elegant refinement. Is that accurate?" Baozhu laughed: "From the way you describe him, you must know him quite well. Why try to hide it?" Qin Yan had no answer to offer. Recalling the dream, he asked: "How many years have you been close with this Mei gentleman?" Baozhu answered: "We only met yesterday." Qin Yan stated: "I do not believe it. If you only met yesterday, how could he have taken your form the evening before?" Having spoken, Qin Yan covered his mouth with his sleeve and laughed. Baozhu was thoroughly bewildered: "Yu Nong, what kind of nonsense are you talking?" Qin Yan insisted: "It is not nonsense at all. You had taken on his appearance and were even singing the role of Liu Mengmei." Baozhu grew more baffled than ever: "Are you still play-acting, or were you merely dreaming?" Smiling sweetly, Qin Yan gave a detailed account of the day the Mei young master watched the opera and of the dream in which Baozhu had transformed into him. Baozhu observed: "This person is indeed remarkably good-looking. If you were truly to pair up with him and perform this 'Waking from a Dream' scene, you would make a magnificent match. It is just a pity I cannot transform." Qin Yan fell silent for a long while before sighing: "What a pity I was out yesterday and missed the chance to see him." Baozhu, having gauged that Qin Yan's affections were fixed on Ziyu, then asked: "Do you realize now that you wronged Duxiang today?" Qin Yan asked: "How so?" Baozhu explained: "The match he said he had found for you, who do you think it is?" Qin Yan asked softly: "Could it really be the Mei young master?" Baozhu confirmed: "If not him, then who?" Qin Yan admitted: "I thought Duxiang meant to humiliate me. Little did I know that the one who solved the lantern riddle was the very same person." Baozhu suggested: "From my perspective, you and this Mei young master share a great deal of predestined affinity. Let me go and tell Duxiang to invite him tomorrow so you can meet. What do you say?" As she spoke, she rose and made to leave; however, Qin Yan caught hold of her sleeve and urged: "You are being reckless again. For one thing, I have never actually met the Mei young master, and I cannot be absolutely certain he is who I think he is. If it turns out he is not, what then? And even if it is him, I have no idea what his inner nature is like. For another, I just quarreled with Duxiang a moment ago, so how could I suddenly turn around and face him?"`,'While this lively conversation was unfolding, Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian had long since slipped around into the adjoining side room and overheard every word with absolute clarity. Pushing the door open, Ziyun and Cixian walked in together, their faces lit with smiles. The moment Qin Yan saw them, he flushed crimson with deep embarrassment and turned his back to sit facing away. Ziyun gently inquired: "Yu Nong, do you still blame me?" Qin Yan lowered his head and offered no reply. Ziyun continued: "Even if my words were poorly chosen, they were spoken without malice.',`Besides, you are not a girl, so what is there to fear in talk of a 'match'? Could I really be marrying you off to Mei Yuxiang?" This remark made Cixian and Baozhu both burst into laughter. Baozhu interjected: "Say no more, for he has already understood. Why don't we simply invite Yuxiang here to meet him?" Ziyun declared: "I have my own method of determining whether it is truly him or not." Baozhu and Qin Yan dined at the Yiyuan that evening, sitting together until the second night-watch before finally returning home.`,`The following day, Ziyun went to call upon Ziyu. They exchanged warm words of mutual admiration and longing, ultimately agreeing to meet for an intimate dinner on the evening of the nineteenth. On his way back, Ziyun stopped at the residences of Wang Xun, Liu Wenze, and Shi Nanxiang to pay courtesy visits, but found none of them at home. Returning to his own residence, he reflected: "This Mei Yuxiang is indeed everything his stellar reputation suggests. Yu Nong is already deeply drawn to him, and once they meet, it goes without saying that he will devote himself entirely to Yuxiang." He then considered further: "Yu Nong's temperament is something most people can never quite fathom. If a single word goes awry, a decisive break is entirely possible. Even if Mei Yuxiang proves to be a man of profound feeling, he may not possess the ability to understand and accommodate Yu Nong as completely as I do.`,"According to Yaoqing's account, Yuxiang knew nothing whatsoever of the name change arranged for Yu Nong, which clearly demonstrates that the two have never shared true intimacy.",`He has only watched a single opera performance, and I suspect his admiration is limited to an appreciation of Yu Nong's face and figure. There is no certainty that Qin Yan alone holds sway over his heart. I believe I shall put him to a test." His thoughts continued: "If on the nineteenth I simply arrange for Yu Nong to serve wine beside him, this being their very first meeting, even if both harbor deep feelings for each other it will be impossible to speak plainly, and no observer will be able to discern the truth. I shall therefore employ the stratagem of substituting one flower for another: first I will conceal Yu Nong, then find someone bearing a resemblance to him, and use carefully chosen words to probe Yuxiang. Observing his response will naturally reveal everything." With his mind firmly made up, he disclosed the intricacies of his plan to Cixian and Baozhu.`,"On the nineteenth, every arrangement was perfectly in order. At the hour of Shen, Mei Ziyu arrived at the Yiyuan garden, where the host stepped forward to welcome him. Together they walked to the Meiyan. This Meiyan was celebrated as one of the garden's most exquisite scenic spots. Just then the plum blossoms were in their full, glorious bloom. At the foot of the great rocky hill, nestled amid a dense grove of trees, stood several dozen chambers arranged elegantly in five distinct clusters. Buildings were surrounded by blossoms, and blossoms surrounded buildings, creating layer upon layer of breathtaking beauty. From a distance, the trees and architectural structures seemed seamlessly intertwined.","The antiques and furnishings housed within were too wonderfully numerous to describe in detail. One simply felt that to stand there was to be enveloped inside the flowers themselves. There was no need to invoke the immortal realm of Luofu, for this was truly an ocean of fragrant snow that mortals were privileged to know.",`The central pavilion served as the very heart of the plum grove. Five chambers had been unified into one expansive long room, then delicately subdivided into five distinct spaces. Suspended above was a plaque bearing the elegant characters 'Meiyan.' Flanking it on either side hung a poetic couplet:

'Ten thousand plum trees, the nose attains virtue;
One ancient house on the hill, the heart finds peace.'

In the center hung a serene portrait of Lin Hejing, the devoted hermit poet who so loved plum blossoms. Facing it stood a daybed crafted of zitan rosewood, intricately carved with plum-blossom designs. Upon it rested a guqin lovingly wrapped in antique brocaded silk. Ziyun invited Ziyu to take his seat inside. Ziyu expressed his gratitude: "The other day I had the boldness to come here and try my hand at the lantern riddles, already acting as an uninvited guest. Today I am once again graced with your summons and seated in your exquisite studio. Your kindness, my good host, vastly surpasses what I can properly receive." Ziyun graciously replied: "Master Yuxiang, you are as auspicious as a lucky star, as honored as a favored minister. I deeply regret not having received you sooner. That you now condescend to visit is a matter of profound and sincere delight to me." Ziyu then inquired: "Are there other guests expected at today's gathering? Why do I not see Master Jingyi?" Ziyun explained: "Jingyi has a small matter to attend to and will join us shortly." Then, gesturing gracefully toward the qin on the daybed, he added: "Today's banquet has been arranged expressly for the occasion of Yu Nong presenting this qin. It would not be fitting to invite other guests, lest the atmosphere for intimate conversation be spoiled." Ziyu pressed further: "I was just about to inquire: why, simply for solving a lantern riddle, was such a tremendously generous gift made? And who exactly is this Yu Nong, for whom you make so much of the occasion?" Ziyun then directed his page boy to untie the silk case and, with both hands, deliver the qin to Ziyu, instructing: "There is an inscription on the back of the qin, please take a moment to look." Ziyu received the qin and examined its masterful craftsmanship: jade tuning pegs, pearl fret-markers, plum-blossom grain, and serpentine crack patterns. It was a magnificent ancient qin of scorched paulownia wood. On the back were engraved two lines of Han-dynasty seal script that read:`,`The qin's heart runs deep and profound;
The qin's virtue is tranquil and serene.
Its owner is fair and pure as jade;
Together they appreciate the divine music.`,"Below the elegant four-line inscription flowed a further line in cursive running script: 'Xu Ziyun of Shanyin, on behalf of Yu Nong Du Qinyan, presents this as a gift for the refined appreciation of the celebrated scholar Yuxiang.' Below this dedication were neatly engraved two seals: one in intaglio reading 'Composed by Cixian,' and one in relief reading 'Carved by Jingyi.'",`Recalling what Baozhu had revealed about the change of name, Ziyu now fully understood that Yu Nong was none other than the qin-player, and a wave of joy washed over him beyond all expectation. He made a deep and respectful bow of thanks and then directed his page boy to carefully rewrap the qin. Ziyun, testing the waters, remarked: "I have heard that you and Yu Nong are on the most intimate of terms. Is there truth to this?" Ziyu earnestly replied: "My family is kept under such strict supervision by my father that ordinarily I am confined strictly to the house. It was only on the sixth day of the first month, when I ventured to the Gusu Huiguan, that I saw him perform one scene of the 'Waking from a Dream.' Someone there mentioned his name was Qin Guan, and I found his beauty and artistry to be both deeply admirable.`,'Not until the other day, here in your esteemed company, did I chance to learn that your honor had changed his name to Qin Yan. In truth, I have never actually met him in person. As for the rumors of intimacy, that is surely a misconception. You might ask Qin Yan himself when you next see him." Ziyun observed: "Your appreciation is certainly not misplaced. But are you aware that Qin Yan harbors rather deep feelings for you?"',`Ziyu smiled gently: "The matter of true feeling is never easily spoken of. Whether it is a literary friendship between peers where kindred spirits connect at first sight, or a bond forged in morality and righteousness spanning a thousand miles, it absolutely requires mutual devotion before one can truly speak of feeling. There is absolutely no reason to bestow such feeling upon a mere stranger. To me, Qin Yan is like a stranger on the road. Since I have completely forgotten any thought of him, how could he possibly direct such feeling toward me?" Ziyun pressed: "According to your own judgment, how does Qin Yan compare to the Baozhu you met the other day?" Ziyu, mindful that both Qin Yan and Baozhu enjoyed Ziyun's favor and unwilling to elevate one at the expense of the other, replied diplomatically: "Generally speaking, to truly evaluate a flower, one must observe its form and bearing after it has been fully adorned, and study its natural grace after the adornment has been removed. Furthermore, one must be intimately familiar with its character and thoroughly acquainted with its speech before truly recognizing its authentic nature. I have seen Baozhu and Qin Yan only once each: one fully adorned, the other unadorned. It is much like catching a fleeting glimpse of flowers while galloping past on horseback; it is impossible to discern any true depth." Ziyun challenged him: "Supposing someone were to offer Qin Yan to you as a gift, what would you do?" Ziyu replied earnestly: "To cherish the fragrant and treasure the beautiful is a feeling entirely natural to all men. Even if I possess no golden chamber in which to conceal such a treasure, I have you, Master Duxiang, to serve as the presiding host of this romantic world. I need not worry about a celebrated flower falling into tragic neglect."`,`Just as they were engaged in conversation, Baozhu arrived gracefully, adorned like a flowering branch, accompanied by another figure. When Ziyu looked, he saw it was none other than the very person for whom he had yearned day and night: Qin Yan. His heart gave a secret, powerful start of surprise. Then he heard Ziyun announce: "Yu Nong, your heart's beloved is here. Come and be properly introduced." Qin Yan smiled sweetly, walked gracefully forward, and made a formal greeting bow. This unexpected moment left Ziyu entirely uncertain whether to sit or to stand, and he simply stared blankly at Qin Yan. Qin Yan then offered a greeting bow to Ziyun as well. Baozhu declared: "Yuxiang, following Zhujun's wise instructions I shall dispense with tedious ceremony." Ziyu responded:`,'"It is best to transcend worldly conventions in this manner. Why should Yu Nong not do the same?" Qin Yan offered a faint smile but remained silent. Ziyu looked first at Qin Yan, then at Baozhu, and felt that Baozhu surpassed Qin Yan in luminous beauty, possessed a lighter and more charming manner of speech, and exhibited a far more leisurely elegance in her movements. He found himself pondering:',`"So this is the true extent of Qin Yan's charm. How is it that those two fleeting glimpses of him from the carriage were so extraordinarily remarkable, and that his stage performance held such immense richness of spirit and manner? And yet Wei Pincai praised him without end, and Xu Ziyun's affection for him runs so astonishingly deep that it truly defies all understanding." As these complex thoughts passed through his mind, a faint trace of genuine disappointment appeared in his expression. Ziyun had already keenly detected it and was secretly delighted that his clever stratagem was working so effectively. Baozhu remarked: "You have long yearned for each other, and today marks your very first meeting, so surely you ought to exchange a few heartfelt words and display some affection. Why are you both as cold as ice, refusing to speak a single word?" As she spoke, she gently took Qin Yan's hand and placed it firmly in Ziyu's. Ziyun encouraged: "She is quite right, so please don't let our presence here inhibit you in any way." This encouragement only served to make Ziyu feel all the more awkward, for taking the hand felt inappropriate, yet refusing it seemed equally wrong. Consequently, he made a polite pretense of needing to step outside, retreating to observe the plum blossoms through the window for a while. Only after Ziyun had urged him repeatedly did he return inside with slow, deeply hesitant steps. Ziyun suggested: "It is entirely too open here. Let us move to the more intimate inner room." Baozhu walked up to the mirrored screen and gave it a gentle press; the screen pivoted on its axis like a door, allowing the party to pass through before silently closing itself again. Ziyu observed the inner chamber carefully: its elegant layout resembled five plum-blossom petals, though it was not overly spacious. While he was attentively examining the room, a page appeared outside the glass window holding a red visiting card and reported: "Master Jia respectfully requests an audience." Ziyun instructed: "I am currently entertaining an honored guest. Tell him to come another time." The page replied: "This master insists he has a matter of urgency and has already stepped inside." Baozhu recognized the name: "Is that not Jia Ren, Old Master Jia?" Ziyun sighed: "Who else could it possibly be?" Baozhu offered: "I was just looking for him myself. Why don't we all go and greet him together?" Ziyun protested: "We have an honored guest here, so how can I possibly leave him unattended?" Ziyu insisted: "Since we are on such intimate terms, why stand on such rigid ceremony?" Ziyun begged Ziyu's pardon, while Baozhu reminded Qin Yan to keep their guest well accompanied, and together they departed the room.`,"The mirrored screen was once again drawn shut, leaving only Ziyu and Qin Yan together in the quiet room. Qin Yan invited Ziyu to sit on the daybed, while he himself remained standing beside him, gazing at Ziyu without once turning his eyes away. This intense scrutiny made Ziyu feel deeply bashful, and he lowered his head.",'Qin Yan leaned his body slightly, resting elegantly against the kang table, supporting his fragrant cheek with one hand, and spoke in a voice dripping with tender charm: "Master Mei, on the sixth day of the first month, you were in the gallery watching me perform, were you not?"',`Ziyu gave a single, slow nod. Qin Yan continued: "Are you aware of the deep longing I have felt for you?" Ziyu gently shook his head. Qin Yan pressed: "The lantern riddle about the jade qin, was it truly you who solved it?" Ziyu nodded once again. "What a wonderfully fine mind. And do you fully comprehend Duxiang's plan?" Ziyu shook his head once more. Qin Yan playfully raised Ziyu's chin with one delicate finger and said: "I heard from Baozhu that behind my back you inquired quite extensively about me, and I am profoundly touched by your feeling. Now that we have met today, and there is no third person present to disturb us, why remain so distant?" Ziyu, pressed to the point where he had no graceful retreat, forced himself to reply: "Yu Nong, I have heard your temperament is extremely proud and aloof, and for that I hold you in high respect. How is it that, just a few days after arriving in the capital, you appear to have entirely lost your true nature?" Qin Yan responded:`,`"So you are ignoring me simply because you look down upon me, which perfectly explains why you are so cold and indifferent. It is a terrible pity that I wasted my earnest feeling." As he spoke, a delicate flush of red spread across his face; the luminous waves of his eyes swept toward Ziyu, and their gazes locked. Ziyu hastily lowered his eyes, his own face reddening as he felt deeply ill at ease. Qin Yan let his half-drowsy eyes linger upon him, and then tilted his fragrant shoulder sideways so that his cheek pressed intimately against Ziyu's cheek. Ziyu shifted uncomfortably aside, and Qin Yan leaned directly into Ziyu's arms, laughing softly. Ziyu, now genuinely provoked, pushed him away and stood up abruptly, stating firmly: "True friendship between people lies in fundamentally knowing each other's hearts. By behaving in this manner, you are treating me as if I were a commoner who frequents the pleasure quarters. Though the company of beauties may serve to delightfully lighten the idle hours, it must always remain strictly within the bounds of a gentleman's honorable conduct. To stoop to the very lowest, to abandon all striving for what is noble, is something even those who merely wish to maintain respectability in their community would utterly disdain to do. I have long held this to be contemptible behavior, and I take it as a stern warning to myself. How could I possibly steel myself to act against my conscience and transgress all moral boundaries? Though your station is that of an actor, you can still forge a respected name through your beauty and art. What lasting pleasure can there possibly be in the vile and the dissolute? I truly cannot comprehend what kind of heart would seek it. At first I believed you were a person of elevated feeling and unconventional spirit, difficult to win over, and I held sincere aspirations of drawing near to you. Now that you behave like this, not only have you squandered your own feeling, but I too deeply regret having invested mine in such glaring error.`,`As for Wei Pincai's lavish praises, they were certainly not to be trusted. But what a tragic pity that Xu Duxiang, loving so widely with feeling spread far too thin, takes such pleasure in the shallow flattery and servility of others. Such is truly the deplorable habit of a pampered young lord. In absolute truth, how could it ever move me?" So saying, he angrily made to push the mirror screen open and depart. However, the screen gave no hint of yielding; push and press it as he fiercely might, it simply would not open.`,'Just as he was at an utter loss and hovering on the very edge of despair, a soft, distinct sound was heard at the mirror screen. Ziyun, Cixian, and Baozhu, their faces adorned with smiles, walked in gracefully from the other side of the screen. At that precise instant, the figure of Qin Yan vanished without a single trace, leaving Ziyu utterly dazed and bewildered. Then he heard Ziyun laughing brightly: "What an admirable Liu Xiahui, able to sit entirely unmoved in the presence of a breathtaking beauty. I offer you my absolute deepest respects. Though I must admit your severe censure of me, Xu Duxiang, was somewhat too cutting." Ziyu turned his head and realized with a jolt that the entire party had been present in the adjoining suite on the other side of the mirror screen all along. He exchanged formal greetings with Cixian, then immediately attempted to take his leave of Ziyun: "Today I tragically forgot an important matter before leaving home. I must return another day to properly impose upon your generous hospitality." Ziyun laughed warmly: "Brother Yuxiang, you must be vexed with your younger brother because of the trick played just now. The wine table is already splendidly laid in the inner room, so please have a cup, and allow me to present myself in sackcloth to beg your sincere forgiveness."',`Cixian added: "I have only just arrived, and was eagerly looking forward to a long and candid conversation, yet your honor fully intends to leave in displeasure. Am I to blame for arriving too late to properly escort you?" Baozhu gently took Ziyu's hand and led him into the inner suite, urging: "Come and have another look at your heart's true beloved; do not let him weep himself sick on your account." Ziyu saw a figure sitting quietly with his back turned, weeping softly. Thinking it was the same Qin Yan as before, he reflected that since Qin Yan possessed enough awareness to weep, he was entirely capable of regretting his conduct; he resolved to use the occasion of the banquet to reform him gradually through gentle, guiding words. Imagine his profound surprise, then, when the figure turned around, delicately wiped the tears away with a handkerchief, and looked at Ziyu with luminous eyes: "Yuxiang, I now truly know your heart." Ziyu noted that the voice was not quite the same. He looked more closely and saw that glowing with brilliant light, beautiful as a heavenly being, this was truly the very person he had glimpsed from the carriage that day and seen performing on the stage. At this breathtaking sight, Ziyu was so startled it was as if he had been caught doing something deeply shameful; his heart hammered in his chest without pause. He felt as if ten thousand tender emotions and an entire breast full of things to say were all pressing heavily upon him, yet he could not utter a single, solitary word. He stood there stupefied for a long, breathless moment. Then suddenly he heard someone announce: "The host is over there presenting wine." As if finally waking from a stupor, Ziyu stepped forward to return the courtesy but entirely forgot to offer a cup in return. Only when Baozhu handed him a cup of wine did he remember, and he sent it to the seat directly opposite his own. Cixian teased: "You are the guest, so how can you present wine on behalf of the host?" Ziyu then realized he had gone to the wrong seat, and could only make the best of the awkward mistake by sending a cup, composing himself, and then refilling the host's cup. After Ziyun had declined with repeated, gracious courtesies, he declared: "I will convey this cup on behalf of Brother Yuxiang to one special person." And he placed it at the seat beside Ziyu, instructing: "Yu Nong, come and sit here." Qin Yan had no choice but to gracefully comply. He filled a cup and placed it before Ziyun, then filled one for Baozhu as well, and finally took his seat. Evening had fully fallen and the warm lamps were lit. Ziyu mused: "What a truly strange day this has been. Was what happened earlier merely a dream of confusion?" This remark set the whole table laughing warmly. Qin Yan, who rarely permitted himself so much as an easy smile, heard these words and could not help letting his beautiful teeth gleam in laughter. Those beautiful eyes, sending mesmerizing ripples of light, had an effect that was truly enough to rob a man of his very soul. It was not only Ziyu, who had never witnessed it before; even Ziyun, who had spent nearly a month in Qin Yan's intimate company, was seeing this breathtaking sight for the very first time. Knowing that the laughter was entirely on Ziyu's account, he could not help but feel a sharp pang of jealousy. Yet fortunately Baozhu's luminous beauty and manner could hold their own beside Qin Yan's. Moreover, seeing Ziyu's refined and gentle elegance as he sat beside Qin Yan, forming a perfect, unmatched pair of jade figures, his admiration completely overcame his jealousy. Meanwhile, Ziyu looked Qin Yan over carefully once more and clearly noticed that the Qin Yan he had seen by day had brows that were shapely but not bewitching, eyes that were beautiful but not captivating, and a complexion that was fair but not lustrous. It was a face that bore some resemblance, yet in true spirit and manner was entirely, fundamentally different.`,"He could make no sense of whether they were one person or two, and the more he puzzled over it the more it became an insoluble, tangled knot, yet he found it utterly impossible to ask.",'After several rounds of fine wine, Cixian asked: "Brother Yuxiang, have you seen the inscription engraved on the jade qin today?" Ziyu replied warmly: "I nearly forgot to express my profound thanks. The brushwork is ancient and masterly, truly the work of a highly skilled hand. But as for the lantern riddle, it was not so very difficult to solve. As for what Duxiang said about it being arranged for Yu Nong, I confess I still do not quite understand his true meaning."',`Ziyun pointed to Qin Yan and explained: "This younger brother of mine set the riddle because he watched me compose it, and Qin Yan loved to recite the two poetic lines about 'fallen flowers' and 'gentle rain.' And because his name contains the character for qin, I used it as the prize, intending it as an augury of a true soulmate for him throughout his life. As it happened, it was you, elder brother, who solved it, brilliantly vindicating my small effort at making a match." Ziyu replied: "To refuse would be deeply discourteous, and to accept leaves me feeling entirely unworthy, yet I shall treasure it carefully on Yu Nong's behalf." A delicate shadow of pleasure crossed Qin Yan's face. Baozhu, observing this, recited a line of Tang poetry with one character cleverly changed:

'The same moonlight as always shines upon the qin;
Only with the plum blossom is it no longer ordinary.'

Ziyun and Cixian together praised the verse: "The change of 'qin' for the original word is wonderfully, perfectly apt."`,`Ziyu, noticing a slight flash of annoyance cross Qin Yan's face, understood that Baozhu was playfully using the character 'qin' in his name, and so remarked: "If Yaoqing did not possess brilliance like a luminous pearl within her breast, she could not have made such a quick and exquisitely clever substitution." The others took this for ordinary praise, but Qin Yan alone felt deeply, profoundly moved by Ziyu's care in avenging the slight of that character 'qin.' Cixian asked: "Why has Yu Nong not said a single word all evening?" Ziyun replied: "He is like the legendary Lady Xi, who steadfastly refused to speak. In the future, Jingyi, you might write him a couplet with those lines:

'If flowers could speak they would only cause trouble;
The stone that cannot speak is the most admirable.'

Ziyu kept nodding in absolute agreement. Baozhu teased: "He is only good at dreaming, so what could he possibly know of speech?" Qin Yan shot Baozhu a sharp, sidelong glance.`,'Ziyu thought to himself: "This is clearly someone entirely different from the one I saw earlier. Can there really, truly be two of them?"',`Ziyun, perceiving that Ziyu and Qin Yan were already mutually and deeply drawn to each other, suggested: "Did not Brother Yuxiang say he had something to attend to? Why not send someone home with word? We could then drink freely to our hearts' absolute content." Ziyu hedged politely: "The matter is a small one, and putting it off until tomorrow will cause no harm. You are a truly generous host, and it is only a pity the whole company of fine friends who came the other day is not here." Ziyun assured him: "They are often here, and there will be many other occasions for another gathering." Ziyu added: "Apart from them, there is still one of truly outstanding and uncommon character." Ziyun confirmed: "Him I know as well." Qin Yan remarked: "Now that is a name with a highly distinctive quality to it." Ziyun raised his cup toward Ziyu, declaring: "Rarely does Yu Nong open his golden lips, so we ought all to drain a full cup." After Ziyu had drained his, he raised one toward Cixian, who also drained his to the very dregs.`,`Baozhu proposed: "Why do we not make a forfeit rule for this evening based on Yu Nong's speaking? Every time he says a sentence, the whole table drinks a cup." Ziyun laughed warmly: "What a novel rule. Let it be so." Ziyu reasoned: "For every sentence spoken, the whole table must drink a cup; that would mean far too much wine. Would it not be better if each person he speaks to drinks a cup?" Qin Yan nodded in agreement. Baozhu cautioned: "That might lead to abuses." Ziyun assured them: "No matter. Even if anyone does get drunk, I have my sobering pills ready." Everyone agreed to the playful arrangement.`,'Qin Yan asked Ziyun: "What sobering pills are those? What are they called?" Ziyun explained each ingredient and preparation, which amounted to two cups for Qin Yan. Qin Yan then asked Cixian: "Why did you return so late today?" Cixian replied: "I was acting as a matchmaker and came back late." That was one more cup. Qin Yan looked steadily at Ziyu but said absolutely nothing; then he turned back and asked Ziyun: "How many plum trees does this garden have in total?" Baozhu gave a slight, knowing cough. Ziyun replied: "Approximately two thousand trees." That was another cup.',`Baozhu came over and filled Ziyun's cup, then leaned over and whispered something secretively in his ear. Qin Yan warned: "If you change the rules, the penalty is ten cups." Ziyu defended her: "No one changed anything." Baozhu came over to fill Ziyu's cup. Qin Yan took up Ziyu's cup and argued: "I did not speak to him, so why should he get wine?" Baozhu countered: "Him speaking to you is exactly the same thing." Qin Yan firmly stated: "This I do not accept." Ziyu, feeling slightly embarrassed, said: "I simply wanted a drink, so let me have one cup." Qin Yan insisted: "If you want to drink, use his cup." Baozhu reached to take the cup from Qin Yan's hands, but Qin Yan had already snatched it away and hidden it. Baozhu had no choice but to pour another cup and bring it to Ziyu. Just as Ziyu was about to reach out for it, Qin Yan covered the cup with his left hand, refusing to let him drink. Everyone gazed at that hand: full and beautifully rounded yet suggesting abundance, soft and pliable as if without bone, the very image of a perfect jade bamboo-shoot. Even a heart of pure iron or stone would have quickened at the breathtaking sight.`,"Ziyun, though he had once clasped that hand himself, could now only look on in deep, admiring envy. Ziyu thought of the hand of that daytime Qin Yan, rough and dark, and understood at long last that they must be two different people entirely. Baozhu thought of a clever scheme and asked:",`"Why is everyone staring at his slender, delicate hand?" At this, Qin Yan hastily drew back his hand, and Baozhu immediately seized the cup and placed it directly in Ziyu's hand. Qin Yan turned to Ziyu: "You must not drink this cup." Ziyu said nothing in reply. Ziyun instructed: "Yu Nong, you should represent me as host and offer a cup to our honored guest." Baozhu chimed in: "Besides, under this rule, that very first sentence was not addressed to Yuxiang either. Could it really have been said to someone else?" Qin Yan considered this for a moment, gracefully acknowledged the point, and allowed himself a beautiful smile.`,'When Ziyu had finished drinking, he turned and asked Baozhu: "Who exactly was that Yu Nong just now?"',`Baozhu laughed brightly: "That is something you must ask your own Yu Nong." Ziyun, still laughing, called out: "Yu Ling! Come and show yourself again for Master Mei." From the inner suite there emerged a person. It was the false Qin Yan from before, standing with arms at his sides, composed and upright, attending quietly beside Ziyun. This false Qin Yan was a member of the troupe of Master Hua's household, a boy named Yu Ling, who had originally been a page in Ziyun's household and later given to Master Hua. Because his face bore a certain resemblance to the real Qin Yan's, he had been called upon and cleverly used for the occasion. This, then, was the brilliant stratagem of substituting one flower for another.`,`At the sight of him, Ziyu felt distinctly awkward, and suddenly everything became wonderfully clear: the Qin Yan he had first seen was truly the false one. All his lingering doubts dissolved at once. Ziyun explained: "I wanted to test Brother Yuxiang's discernment, and so deliberately presented an inferior likeness to impose upon a true beauty. That you saw right through it proves your keen, exceptional perception." He then directed Yu Ling to bring two large jade cups and ordered: "Present a cup to Master Mei on my behalf." Yu Ling poured and brought the cup to Ziyu. Ziyu received it and said: "The wine has already been far too much, and the evening grows late, so let us have our rice." Ziyun insisted: "If Brother will not drink this cup, then he is truly angry with your younger brother. Yu Ling, drink a cup in my name and make my sincere apologies." Yu Ling duly poured that cup as well and took a large sip. Baozhu offered him a few sections of spring orange to go with it, and after two more sips he finished. Ziyu, with no way out, drained the first half of his cup in one breath, ate a little fruit, and then, while Qin Yan squeezed some spring orange juice into what remained, slowly finished the rest.`,`Today was Ziyu's very first meeting with Qin Yan, and the beauty of natural endowment and celestial grace had already deeply intoxicated his heart. Then to drink this large cup on top of all the rest, though joy poured into a joyful breast, meant the wine had passed its proper measure, and before his eyes the world dissolved into a swirling haze of colors. He could no longer hold himself steady. Ziyun dared not offer any more. They all ate their rice, washed, and then Ziyu prepared to take his leave. It was Qin Yan who, deeply concerned that he might be unwell from the wine, asked Ziyun for a dose of the Immortal Peach Longevity Pills, prepared them properly, blew on them until they were not too hot, and gave them to Ziyu to take. Before long, Ziyu felt his mind and spirits wonderfully clear. He then looked his absolute fill at Qin Yan once more; though neither could speak plainly of what filled his heart in the presence of others, there had already been a silent, profound mutual pledge, an unspoken understanding conveyed beautifully through glance and expression. Ziyu directed his page boy Yun Er to take the jade qin, and then said his fond farewells to Ziyun and Cixian and took his leave. Qin Yan quietly asked when they might meet again; Ziyu felt a great, heavy weight of pain in his heart, but forced himself to say: "As soon as a moment of leisure presents itself, we shall surely meet again." The whole party saw him off to the place where the carriage waited. There was a great, heavy air of reluctance to part; they watched his carriage until it passed completely through the garden gate, and then Baozhu and Qin Yan each mounted their own carriage and departed. What happened next, hear it told in the following chapter.`]},u=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations10:a},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t=["As the story goes, Ziyu rode home in his carriage, his mind lingering the entire way upon the exquisitely beautiful youths he had glimpsed. Arriving at the gate, he noticed a carriage and three horses; recognizing the servants of Commissioner Wang of the Office of Transmission, he knew the Commissioner was within. Proceeding to the study, he found his father engaged in conversation with Wang Wenhui. Ziyu stepped forward to offer his greetings, saying, 'I have just returned from paying respects to my uncle.' Wenhui, his face wreathed in smiles, replied, 'I departed early this morning and had not yet returned.' Ziyu stood respectfully aside as Wenhui continued, 'For the spring gathering to exchange New Year greetings, the Lianjin troupe has already been engaged to perform at the Gusu Guildhall. I fear attendance may be sparse this time, as quite a few have been appointed to provincial posts or dispatched on official duties, we will likely fill no more than three or four tables.' Academician Mei observed, 'Provincial Governor Yuan Hailou is in Yunnan, and Marquis Su has been ordered to Shanxi. Two others are serving as Education Commissioners, while three have departed the capital as Provincial Administration and Surveillance Commissioners. The remainder are merely the few of us present, hardly enough to occupy four tables.' Wang Wenhui then went into the inner chambers to greet Lady Yan. After exchanging familial pleasantries, he mentioned that his second daughter, Qionghua, was now sixteen and yet to be betrothed, entrusting Shixie to seek out a suitable match. Shixie agreed, yet added a caveat, 'Choosing a son-in-law is a daunting endeavor. There are those with handsome exteriors who prove unremarkable within, equally, some show early brilliance only to falter in adulthood.' Lady Yan chimed in, 'Such matters are ultimately governed by destiny. It is not merely finding a son-in-law that poses a challenge, arranging a fitting match for your own nephew is no simple task.' Wenhui sighed, 'Where indeed could one find someone as exceptional as my nephew?' As they conversed, a maidservant passed through the middle gate bearing two red calling cards. Shixie asked who had arrived. Presenting the cards, the maidservant replied, 'The gatekeeper reports they are visitors from our home province, waiting outside the middle gate for a response.' Shixie examined the cards, one read, 'Your humble nephew of the next generation, Wei Pincai,' while the other read, 'Your devoted later student, Li Yuanmao.'",`Shixie pondered, '"Your devoted later student", what a peculiar title. I wonder where he hails from? And who might this Wei Pincai be?' Wang Wenhui suggested, '"Your humble nephew of the next generation", could it be the son of Wei Laoren?' Shixie nodded, 'I suspect you are right. This past summer, I received a letter from Laoren mentioning his intent to send his son to the capital to secure a modest degree, entrusting him to my care. In truth, old Wei's character is utterly despicable, it is solely out of regard for our shared past that any shred of goodwill remains.' Wenhui replied, 'Old Wei possessed a first-rate intellect and could well have triumphed in the imperial examinations. Regrettably, he compromised his hidden virtues to such an extent that he devolved into a scoundrel of a scholar.'`,"'Since his son has traveled this great distance to seek refuge, you are bound by duty and cannot well turn him away.' Shixie summoned Mei Jin to verify their identities, and indeed, one was the son of Mr. Li, the resident tutor. He then instructed Mei Jin:","'Invite them to take a seat in the flower hall and inform them I shall be out shortly.' Wenhui then rose to take his leave. Shixie saw him to the door, turned back to the hanging flower gate of the hall, and instructed his attendant to summon the young master from the study before strolling inside.","There he beheld a young man standing at the place of honor, slight of build with bright, alert features. At the lower end stood another, heavily built and somewhat sallow, with thick brows and nearsighted eyes, appearing to be in his twenties. The young man at the upper end stepped forward, his face alight with a beaming smile, addressed him as 'Uncle,' and dropped to his knees in a deep kowtow. Shixie hastily returned the courtesy, studied him for a moment, and remarked, 'You carry the distinguished bearing of your family, you are the very image of your father.' Pincai was about to reply when Li Yuanmao executed a highly elaborate bow from his lofty height, slowly kneeling to prostrate himself four times as though worshipping at a temple altar. Shixie helped him up with both hands, saying, 'Your esteemed father has been eagerly awaiting your arrival. You must be exhausted from your journey.' Li Yuanmao moved his lips in a mumble that proved entirely unintelligible. Shixie invited them to sit, and Pincai offered, 'My father remains profoundly grateful for your immense kindness, which is forever etched in his heart. He specifically dispatched me to the capital to offer my respects to you and your lady, and to earnestly seek your guidance and patronage.'","Shixie courteously inquired after his parents' health. When Ziyu emerged and exchanged formal greetings, Shixie promptly instructed him to escort Yuanmao to meet his father. Ziyu then led both Yuanmao and Pincai toward the study. Meanwhile, Shixie ordered the servant Xu Shun to prepare two rooms in the secluded courtyard behind the study for their temporary lodging.","He further directed their attendant to retrieve their luggage before retiring to the main residence.",`In the study, Ziyu guided Li and Wei to meet Li Xingquan, who was already aware of his son's arrival. Once the son had offered his filial respects, introductions were made with Wei Pincai. Li Xingquan courteously offered Pincai the seat of honor, an offer Pincai repeatedly declined. Ziyu, sensing that father and son, newly reunited, would cherish a moment to converse in private, tactfully escorted Pincai to the boat-shaped annex across the courtyard, where Yuner and Junner soon served them tea. Pincai smiled warmly and asked, 'Does my elder brother still recognize me?' Ziyu replied apologetically, 'Your face is familiar, but I confess I cannot quite place you.' Pincai laughed, 'They say men of distinction have fleeting memories, and it seems to be true. The year your esteemed mother traveled to the capital and you accompanied her, I saw you off at the docks. You clung to my sash with both hands, insisting I join you as your companion. It took quite a bit of gentle coaxing from your mother to make you let go. Have you truly forgotten?' Ziyu smiled in realization, 'Now that you mention it, a vague memory returns. I was but five at the time. If I recall correctly, your name contained the character "zhen."' Pincai nodded, 'Exactly right. I knew that a man of your natural brilliance, having once shown such affection, could not entirely forget.' Ziyu inquired, 'Did you and Brother Li journey by water or by land?' Pincai answered, 'Partly by boat, though it was more a combination of both. It is a lengthy tale. Since I will be imposing on your hospitality for a time, allow me to recount it to you gradually.' Just then, Yuner arrived to summon them for dinner, and they returned to the study together. Li Xingquan eagerly offered Pincai the head seat. Pincai firmly refused, deferring to the teacher, and took a subordinate place, while Yuanmao and Ziyu sat below. Over the meal, Li Xingquan asked after their journey and expressed his gratitude to Pincai for looking after his son. Pincai responded with modest demurrers and offered effusive praise of Yuanmao. Li Xingquan was deeply gratified, hoping his son had perhaps matured. Throughout the meal, Li Yuanmao kept his head bowed, not daring to utter a single word. By the time they finished, their luggage had been delivered, and their rooms were fully prepared.`,"They lingered over tea for a while, discussing the state of affairs in the south. Pincai, aware that Yuanmao could not abide late hours, eventually rose to take his leave. Li Xingquan, understanding the exhaustion of their travels, sent Yuanmao along with him. Ziyu escorted them to their quarters, ensuring everything was appropriately arranged. Bidding them to rest early, he called for Junner to light a lantern and made his way back to the main house.","The following day, Pincai and Yuanmao visited the main residence to pay their respects to Lady Yan. They presented regional gifts brought from the south, alongside letters from their fathers, each brimming with earnest pleas for continued patronage and care. Among the missives was a letter addressed to Wang Wenhui, Shixie instructed Pincai to deliver it personally at a later date. Pincai was inherently clever and had been thoroughly molded by his father's worldly tutelage. His tongue possessed a thousand nimble tricks, endlessly adept at charming and cajoling. Consequently, within less than ten days, every member of the Mei household was singing his praises. Although Ziyu could clearly see through his artifice, he found Pincai to be far from disagreeable, serving as a pleasant enough companion to wile away the quiet hours.",`One evening, while Yuanmao slept, Ziyu and Pincai sat chatting together. Pincai inquired, 'The theater of the capital is renowned as the finest beneath the heavens. I have heard that the young performers of female roles, affectionately styled as "gongzi," carry themselves with an extraordinary air of distinction.`,`'It is said that even princes and high ministers share their tables as equals. As for mid-ranking officials, some actively court and flatter them, seemingly borrowing their luster to flaunt before wealthy and influential patrons. Retaining one's company for a day's drinking can cost tens of taels of silver, yet the performer may not utter a single word of gratitude. Is this truly the case?' Ziyu laughed softly, 'Perhaps in certain circles, but as I rarely venture out, I know little of such worldly affairs.' Pincai pressed, 'Surely you have attended performances. What are these young actors truly like?' Ziyu replied dismissively, 'I have never encountered a truly exceptional one. Such is the fashion in Beijing, the moment a young actor assumes the stage, the public extols him as a marvel. It has hardened into a convention that cannot be easily swayed.' Pincai nodded in agreement, 'That has always been my impression as well. The actors from Nanjing are utterly unexceptional, with the female roles played by men in their thirties or forties, I had never heard of gentlemen drinking with them. Yet, quite recently, two young performers have emerged who are akin to immortals banished to the mortal realm. They traveled with me on the very same boat as far as Zhangjiawan, and we entered the capital on the exact same day.' Ziyu asked, 'What do you mean by "immortals banished to the mortal realm"?' Pincai elaborated with dramatic flair, 'Even among the divine, these two would be chosen as unparalleled. The lesser deities of the lower eight caverns could not conjure such breathtaking visages. One of the four great troupes in Beijing employed a master who traveled to Suzhou and purchased ten youths, most fourteen or fifteen, some barely twelve or thirteen, bringing them north via the waterways in two grand vessels. When I departed my home, I initially boarded a cargo ship, and upon reaching Yangzhou, I chanced upon Brother Li at an inn, who was also bound for the capital. We joined forces, initially planning to journey overland, but finding the carriage fares exorbitant, we sought a passenger boat. It was then we encountered these theatrical vessels in Yangzhou. The master was a man named Ye Maolin, a native of Suzhou. He had previously taught singing at the Bian family's riverside estate on the Qinhuai, and I was acquainted with him. He graciously allowed us passage on his boat to the capital. Amidst the throngs of grain barges congesting the Grand Canal, the voyage stretched over four months. We observed them rehearsing their roles daily, absorbing many of their melodies by ear. Aboard our vessel were five youths, and the two most brilliant were a young dan named Qi Guan, fourteen years of age. His complexion was like rouge and fine powder masterfully blended, forming a flawless visage that seemed fragile enough to shatter at a touch. A singular aura of fresh purity lingered about his brows and the corners of his eyes. When he sang, his voice rang several degrees clearer and more resplendent than a thrush or an oriole.`,"'That alone would suffice to declare him a peerless beauty. Yet there was another, portraying the sequestered maiden, a youth named Qin Guan, merely fifteen years of age. His beauty transcends the boundaries of earthly language. To compare him to the beauties of this world is futile, for no such exquisite coloring exists in reality. Though one might liken him to the celebrated beauties of antiquity, I myself have never laid eyes upon a beauty of ancient times. Among all living beauties, nothing approaches his refinement. Not even the most accomplished painters of ethereal women could capture the spirit that resides within his eyes. His surname is Du, perhaps he is the very reincarnation of Du Liniang, returned from the grave. If not, he must surely be the fairy maiden Du Lanxiang descending to grace a mortal lover. Beyond these two illustrious ladies of the Du lineage, there could be no third equivalent.'","Ziyu could not suppress a laugh. Inwardly, he reasoned, 'Such extravagant praise cannot be entirely trusted. Yet, his description mirrors the two youths I beheld in the carriage the other day with uncanny precision, not a single detail is amiss. Since those two exist in reality, how could heaven possibly forge an identical pair? It is simply impossible, there is no need to lend full credence to his words.' Aloud, he remarked, 'From your description, Brother, they sound truly peerless. It is doubtful anyone else in this world could rival them.' Pincai declared, 'You shall see for yourself. They arrived in the capital on the exact same day as I did, and by now they must be formally bound to a troupe. Are they not destined to grace the stage one day? Once you set eyes upon them, you will trust my judgment, for I am not one to dispense praise lightly. There is, however, one caveat: their beauty is matched only by their forbidding temperaments. No matter how ardently one attempts to please them, not a single kind word can be coaxed from their lips.","'The other is even more peculiar, simply choosing to ignore people entirely. If pressed with a few extra questions, he grows so incensed he appears on the verge of tears. I fear such a disposition will win him few admirers in the capital. But in matters of appearance, even if the capital boasts fine young actors, none could ever outshine him, they would likely pale entirely by comparison.'","Ziyu reflected silently, 'He claims these two entered the capital on the exact same day as he did. They arrived precisely after I saw those two on that very day, could the ones he speaks of be the very ones I saw? That group did indeed appear to be travelers from the south.' He then asked, 'What did you say the most beautiful one was named?' Pincai replied, 'He is named Qin Guan. The other is Qi Guan.' Ziyu pressed on, 'What garments was Qin Guan wearing on the day he entered the city?' Pincai described, 'Both wore fur coats of blue crepe silk beneath outer riding jackets of dark brown wool.' Ziyu, noting the match in attire, probed further, 'Did each ride in his own carriage, or did they share one?' Pincai answered, 'He shared a carriage with Qi Guan and Ye Maolin. The carriage awning was of blue cloth, drawn by a white mule.' Ziyu asked, 'And how old is this Ye Maolin?' Pincai said, 'Past fifty.' Unable to contain himself, Ziyu clapped his hands and laughed, 'I have already seen these two! Your praise is not misplaced in the slightest, they are truly unparalleled beauties.' Pincai, overjoyed, exclaimed, 'You see? But when did you happen to see them?' Ziyu recounted how he had been caught in the throng that day and had observed four carriages filled with young boys, the foremost bearing those very three. Qi Guan was already exceptional, but Qin Guan was truly without equal beneath the heavens. Pincai was beside himself with delight and pressed further:","'How do they compare to the famed actors of the capital?' Ziyu smiled, 'The two I saw in the carriage the other day surpass anything my eyes have ever beheld, with Qin Guan being a rarity beyond measure. Yet, I wonder which troupe they have joined?' Pincai proposed, 'I shall make inquiries tomorrow. Once I have word, we shall go and hear them perform.' Ziyu nodded in agreement, but just as he was about to speak again, the light flared, and a young maidservant called from outside the door, 'The mistress requests the young master to retire early.' Ziyu had no choice but to rise and retreat to his chambers. Throughout the night, he pondered Pincai's words over and over, meticulously recalling every detail and expression he had witnessed in the carriage, before finally drifting off to sleep. From that day forth, Ziyu treated Pincai with ever greater warmth and familiarity.","The following morning, Pincai took his young servant, Si'er, to deliver Wang Wenhui's letter. As it happened, Wenhui had departed early and had not yet returned, and Wang Xun was likewise away from home, leaving Pincai no option but to request an audience with Yan Zhongqing. Upon meeting Zhongqing and observing his striking demeanor, Pincai engaged him in polite pleasantries. Learning that Zhongqing was Wenhui's son-in-law as well as Shixie's nephew by marriage, Pincai was generous with his flattery. He was just preparing to take his leave when a servant entered, bearing a bundle of clothes, and announced, 'The master has returned.' Pincai felt obliged to remain seated. After a brief pause, the sound of voices drifted from the courtyard, seemingly discussing arrangements for a theatrical troupe. This was followed by the heavy, rhythmic thud of boots, and a figure strode into the room. It was a man with a broad, square face and a long, pepper-and-salt beard, clad in the resplendent attire of a third-rank official. His imposing stature, brilliant furs, and thick-soled black boots cut an awe-inspiring figure. Recognizing him as the master of the house, Pincai hastily stepped forward to bow deeply in greeting. Wenhui caught him by both hands, insisting, 'Please, please! There is no need for such excessive formality. I was informed the very day you arrived in the capital. You are staying with my relative, Mei Tie'an, are you not?' Pincai respectfully assented.",`Wenhui invited Pincai to take a seat, settling himself comfortably with legs crossed, while Zhongqing occupied a stool by the window. Observing Wenhui's grandiose, self-important posture, Pincai carefully calculated his approach before rising to his feet and addressing him, 'I am entirely dependent upon the patronage and shelter of esteemed elders like yourself. My father once confided to me that with your distinguished countenance, you were destined to attain the highest pinnacles of office. Taking this opportunity to pay my respects, I harbor hopes that you might deign to uplift this humble scholar in the future.' With that, he drew forth the letter and presented it respectfully with both hands. Wenhui accepted it with one hand, glanced at the envelope, and tossed it aside, erupting into a booming laugh. 'Why does your esteemed father treat me with such distant formality, addressing me as "Your Excellency"?' He let out a sigh, 'It is a pity about your father's masterful eight-legged essays. The year we passed the district examinations together, the very session in which I succeeded, your father was slated to place first. The decision was final, but the chief examiner suddenly noticed a drawing on the cover of his exam booklet, a blade and a brush. Beneath the brush was a pool of ink that had soaked straight through to the back page. Peeling the pages apart, the stain resembled a human head, and with each subsequent page, the image grew clearer, until even the facial features were distinctly visible. Interpreting this as a sign that he had severely damaged his hidden virtues, the examiner replaced him. Heaven only knows what your father was thinking, forsaking literature to draft legal petitions with that fine brush of his. To this day, he remains an impoverished scholar, devoid of any great fortune. Whenever an education commissioner departs the capital, I make a point to put in a strong word on his behalf, otherwise, once word of his litigious activities spread, the consequences would be dire.' Pincai shifted uncomfortably, mortified by the recounting. Wenhui stroked his long beard and continued, 'The year before last, when Prefect Wei was appointed to Jiangning, he asked me to recommend a reliable assistant before he left the capital. I put forward your father's name, and he readily agreed to hire him. Yet, when I subsequently received no word from your father, I grew suspicious. Only later did Prefect Wei send a dispatch explaining that his superiors had forced so many recommendations upon him that he was compelled to hire them all.`,"'He also mentioned that Hou Shiyan had ruthlessly imposed two of his own relatives on him. He had no choice but to offer alternative arrangements or recommend him elsewhere.","'Tell me, did that alternative recommendation ever materialize?' Pincai, utterly bewildered and entirely unaware of the matter, could only bow deeply in feigned gratitude and reply, 'No such recommendation came to pass.' Wenhui mused, 'He must have caught wind of some unsavory gossip. Is your father still teaching, or is he still dabbling in those shady affairs?' Pincai answered:","'At present, my father serves as a clerk in a salt administration office, which offers slightly more latitude than teaching.' Wenhui nodded approvingly:","'That is much better. What is his annual remuneration?' Pincai replied, 'He earns around three hundred taels.' Wenhui remarked, 'That should suffice for daily expenses. Truth be told, as a third-rank metropolitan official, my own annual stipend scarcely amounts to more.' With that, he threw his head back and laughed heartily. Left with nothing further to say, Pincai was contemplating his departure when a strikingly handsome attendant, dressed in splendid attire, leaned in to whisper something in Wenhui's ear. Pincai, perceptive to a fault, recognized that a private matter required attention and promptly rose to take his leave. Wenhui made a gesture to escort him out, but Pincai demurred, 'I still have a few words for Brother Yan. Please, Your Excellency, attend to your affairs.' Wenhui halted, offered a perfunctory bow, and swaggered into the inner chambers with an exaggerated sway. Zhongqing escorted Pincai to the door, while Pincai thought to himself, 'This old man is incredibly arrogant, a far cry from the graciousness of Uncle Mei.' He then made his way back to the Mei residence, which needs no further recounting.","Meanwhile, Zhongqing had returned to his own quarters for a meal and conversed briefly with his wife, Ronghua, before heading to Wang Xun's study, arriving just as Wang Xun returned. They had barely exchanged a few words when two of Wang Xun's brothers-in-law arrived to pay a visit. One was named Sun Sihui, the other Sun Siyuan, both sons of Ministry Director Sun Lianggong, a fellow provincial and examination graduate of Wang Wenhui. These two brothers were truly a matched pair of ineptitude.","Compared to Wang Xun in both physical appearance and intellectual capacity, the difference was as vast as that between heaven and the abyss. Sihui possessed a hunched neck and bulging cheeks, his complexion passably fair were it not marred by a severe excess of internal heat. Year-round, his face was blanketed in a dense crop of red pustules, leaving virtually no skin untouched, with the affliction clustering most aggressively upon his nose, transforming it into a bright, inflamed bulb.","Though twenty-six years of age, he had yet to complete his study of the Five Classics. His grasp of literature was woefully deficient, yet he possessed an insufferable penchant for flaunting obscure terminology, peppering his speech with archaic particles in a manner that reeked of pedantry. A certain Gao Pin, an imperial student from Suzhou well-acquainted with him, had bestowed upon him two derogatory monikers: one was 'Moth-Eaten Thousand-Character Classic.' Because of his inflamed nose, which sometimes shone with a greasy luster, he was also dubbed 'The Aroused Dog Penis.' His younger brother, Siyuan, was afflicted with an owl-like beak and protruding teeth, coupled with drooping eyelids, his right eye permanently slanted upwards as if half-circled by a vermilion brush. His intellectual prowess mirrored his brother's, yet he delighted in critiquing Sihui's grammatical blunders. Further compounding his flaws, Siyuan suffered from a severe stutter, when embroiled in debate, he would stammer incessantly, his speech devolving into incomprehensibility the more agitated he became. Gao Pin had likewise graced him with a nickname, 'The Stuttering Dictionary.' Truly, these two useless individuals were a perfect pair.",`On this particular day, they arrived at the Wang residence precisely as Wenhui was hosting a banquet, the guests on the verge of assembling. Wang Xun promptly ushered them into the study. Unable to avoid them, Zhongqing politely exchanged greetings and sat down beside Wang Xun to keep them company. Sihui initiated the conversation, addressing Zhongqing pompously, 'Today the heavens are clear and the air is crisp, thus my brother and I have straightened our garments and drifted hither to pay our respects.' Wang Xun and Zhongqing struggled to suppress their amusement. Sihui then turned to Wang Xun, 'Just as your esteemed self had ventured out to parts unknown, had it not been that "the weary bird knows to return," then even if one drew a bow and shot an arrow at it, it would merely arouse the envy of the fowler.' Zhongqing was on the verge of formulating a reply when Siyuan interjected:`,`'B-b-brother, your phrase is s-s-spoken in error. How can you c-c-compare a bird to a human, and furthermore, you w-w-wish to shoot an arrow at him? That is simply p-p-preposterous.' Sihui retorted disdainfully, 'Number Two, your mind is a hollow void, utterly devoid of the subtle alchemy of scholarship. This flows effortlessly from my profound erudition, tumbling spontaneously from my lips. This phrase, "the weary bird knows to return," originates from the Finest of Ancient Prose. If you argue a bird cannot be compared to a human, then why does the Great Learning state, "Can a human be inferior to a bird?"' Zhongqing chuckled inwardly at the sheer magnitude of such stupidity and remarked aloud, 'Brother's avian discourse is profoundly logical. Not only is Brother akin to a bird, but I fear the bird is scarcely a match for Brother. One must understand that Master Jingjie originally employed this phrase as a metaphor for himself.' Sihui tilted his head, listening intently, before countering, 'I fear the edition of the Finest of Ancient Prose my esteemed brother has perused is a pirated copy. My memory is flawless; the author of this ancient text was surnamed Tao, not Qin.' Wang Xun, unable to contain himself any longer, feigned the need to use the washroom and hurried out, his lips pressed tightly together to stifle his laughter. Zhongqing replied with a smile, 'Brother is truly a wellspring of profound knowledge, even knowing the author's surname.' Sihui, assuming Zhongqing genuinely admired him, swelled with pride. The red pustules on his face seemed to engorge like ripening berries, glistening brightly. He turned to Siyuan, 'Number Two, for us scholars, natural talent and a robust memory must advance in tandem, neither can be lacking.' Siyuan scoffed, 'B-b-brilliant! If your memory were not so exceptional, you w-w-wouldn't have mistaken a dog for a human. And if your talent were not so formidable, you w-w-wouldn't have mistaken an ox for a teacher.' As he laughed uproariously, a tear trickled from his slanted eye. Sihui flared with indignation, his cheeks puffing out like a toad's. Zhongqing deliberately inquired, 'It seems your esteemed brother was once again citing the classics, I should very much like to hear this tale.' Siyuan elaborated, 'In t-t-terms of literary skill, my brother has studied two years l-l-longer than I, and I confess I c-c-cannot match him. However, his errors are legion. One day, our t-t-teacher set us a couplet exercise, requiring us to match a classical text with another. The first line was: "Man can enlarge the Way." My b-b-brother answered swiftly, writing down: "The d-d-dog has no constant heart." The teacher said, "This is not from the classics." My b-b-brother insisted, "It is from Mencius." The teacher retorted,`,`'"P-p-preposterous!" My brother assumed the teacher had forgotten, and was d-d-delighted beyond measure, hastily flipping through the text to show him. It turned out to be the character "gou" (if/careless) with the grass radical, not the character "gou" (dog) with the dog radical.' Zhongqing let out a soft laugh and commented, 'If not for the canine confusion, it would have been an excellent couplet.' Siyuan continued, 'On another occasion, the teacher set a theme for the opening argument of an essay: "Where is the master going?" My brother immediately b-b-began his essay with "Where is the ox going?" The t-t-teacher aggressively crossed it out with his brush and delivered a scathing reprimand.' This recounting left Sihui so overwhelmed with shame and fury that he began pacing wildly around the room, sputtering, 'Nonsense, sheer nonsense!' before storming out to take his leave. Wang Xun, fearing their sibling rivalry might escalate unpleasantly, deemed it unwise to insist they stay, and together with Zhongqing, escorted them out.`,"Just as they reached the inner gate, it happened that Sun Lianggong was making his entrance, and the Sun brothers respectfully stood aside.","Wang Xun and Zhongqing stepped forward to exchange greetings. Lianggong inquired, 'Have all the guests assembled?' Wang Xun replied, 'Not yet.' Zhongqing observed Lianggong closely, noting his purplish-brown complexion and flat nose. Yet, his features were properly aligned, and accompanied by a modest beard, he presented a significantly more dignified appearance than his two illustrious sons.","Lianggong was on the verge of addressing his sons when Wang Guibao entered the courtyard. Spotting Lianggong, along with Wang Xun and Zhongqing, he also stepped respectfully aside. Lianggong glanced at Guibao before instructing his sons, 'Return home immediately, and utter not a single word.' The Sihui brothers, understanding the implication, voiced their assent. Lianggong then took Guibao by the arm and led him inside.","Zhongqing and Wang Xun saw the two brothers to the door before returning inside. They changed their garments and settled in the study for an evening meal, sharing wine and casual conversation. Wang Xun mused, 'These two brothers-in-law of ours are truly worthy of inclusion in the Peerless Register.' Zhongqing remarked, 'Why do the brothers and your wife share so little resemblance? Had your esteemed lady possessed such a visage, you would surely be suffering a bitter fate.' Wang Xun laughed, 'Fortunately, my wife was borne by my current mother-in-law. You are unaware that we have an older sister-in-law still residing at home. She is practically ancient, a head full of white hair, and hopelessly unmarriageable, being nearly thirty years of age.' Zhongqing inquired, 'I have heard rumors that your mother-in-law is exceptionally fierce and jealous. Is this true?' Wang Xun confirmed:","'That jealous temperament is truly rare.' But let us set that aside for now.","Meanwhile, Sun Lianggong entered with Guibao and formally greeted the host. Within a short time, all the guests had arrived, and they arranged themselves for the banquet. These guests were all Wang Wenhui's fellow graduates. As Sun Lianggong was the eldest and a relative by marriage, they offered the seat of honor to Yang Fangyou, a Vice Director in the Ministry of War. Seated opposite him was Zhou Xijue, Vice Minister of the Court of Imperial Entertainments. Investigating Censor Lu Zongyuan took the third seat, Sun Lianggong the fourth, while Wenhui presided as the host. Guibao poured a round of wine, after which Yang Fangyou invited him to join the table, seating him opposite Wang Wenhui. Wenhui inquired why his brother Lanbao had not attended. Guibao explained, 'Today we spent the entire day touring the Yi Garden. Master Xu only dispatched me here upon learning you were hosting a banquet. Lanbao, Baozhu, Huifang, Shufang, and Yulin have not yet dispersed; I suspect they will linger until the fourth or fifth watch.' Wenhui remarked, 'This Xu Duchang is truly the most carefree and joyous man in the mortal realm.' Lu Zongyuan added, 'I have heard that constructing his Yi Garden cost over half a million taels of silver.' Yang Fangyou noted, 'The grounds are vast, and the craftsmanship is exquisitely refined.' Wenhui shared, 'I spent a full day touring it last month and still did not see half of it.' Guibao chimed in, 'Today we explored the Plum Blossom Retreat and the East Wind Last Night Pavilion. Those two sections alone boast a full hundred rooms. The layout is astonishingly complex, you step inside and can scarcely find your way out.' Sun Lianggong quipped, 'You ought to dig a tunnel and secrete yourself within.' The table erupted in laughter. Guibao retorted, 'You are too fond of insulting people,' and promptly poured a large cup of wine as a penalty. Lianggong initially refused, but Guibao threatened to force it down his throat, leaving him no choice but to drink.",`After several dishes were served, Wenhui proposed, 'Drinking plainly like this lacks flavor. Ruixiang, why don't you initiate a drinking game?' Guibao suggested, 'The "擂" (lei/challenge) game is best, it accommodates any rule.' Sun Lianggong groaned, 'We are doomed! We have invited the very patriarch of drinking games.' Wenhui instructed the servants to fetch six coins. Zhou Xijue advised, 'This cup should be divided into larger and smaller portions.' Yang Fangyou proposed, 'Let the two of us split a cup three ways.' Lu Zongyuan countered, 'That seems too meager. Why don't you two divide a cup in half, while the rest of us take one full cup each? How does that sound?' Everyone nodded in agreement. Guibao extended a fist and asked Wenhui how many cups he wagered.`,"Wenhui declared, 'Let us not overcomplicate matters. The six of us will strictly adhere to six cups each, without addition or subtraction. Whoever wins a cup may divide it into as many smaller portions as they wish. Furthermore, let us dispense with complex rules regarding sequential drinking, whoever guesses correctly simply executes the game's designated action. That is the most straightforward method.' Guibao then turned to Yang Fangyou, 'What is the rule for the first cup?' Yang Fangyou replied, 'One cup becomes three, finger-guessing with your partner.' He then asked Sun Lianggong, 'What about the second and third cups?' Lianggong decreed, 'Both cups entail impersonating a young female performer presenting wine.' Zhou Xijue interjected, 'With our prominent beards, that will prove somewhat challenging to act out.' Lianggong reassured him, 'So long as the performance is convincing, a beard poses no obstacle.' Guibao then asked Lu Zongyuan, 'And the fourth cup?' Lu Zongyuan instructed, 'Grab a handful of melon seeds, count them out, and whoever the count lands on must drink.' Guibao commented:","'That cup got off lightly.' He then asked Zhou Xijue, 'What are the rules for the fifth and sixth cups?'",`Zhou Xijue proposed, 'Two cups become six, utilizing a "flying toast" featuring the character for "flower" (hua).' Guibao first addressed Wang Wenhui, 'How many?' Wenhui answered, 'One.' Without pausing, he asked Lianggong, 'How many?' Lianggong raised two fingers, 'Exactly two.' Guibao laughed, 'An excellent guesser, correct on the very first try.' Opening his hand, he revealed precisely two coins. He then arranged three cups, filled them brimming with wine, and placed them before Lianggong. Lianggong declared, 'This follows Brother Yang's rule, I challenge you to finger-guessing.' Yang Fangyou reminded him, 'I specifically wagered half a cup.' Lianggong dismissed it, 'We will settle the portions after the match.' They played three rapid rounds, and Lianggong lost all three. Resigned, he said, 'My luck at finger-guessing is dismal today, I concede to you.'`,`The second and third cups fell under Lianggong's own rule. He sighed, 'This female impersonation has truly backfired on me. Very well, allow me to toast two gentlemen.' Rising to his feet, he clasped a cup of wine in his left hand, obscured his beard with his right, tilted his head coquettishly twice, and, wearing a beguiling smile, minced delicately toward Yang Fangyou with a swaying gait. Executing a demure curtsy, he cooed in a sickeningly sweet voice, 'I present Master Yang with a cup of wine, please do me the honor of accepting.' As he spoke, his eyes darted flirtatiously around the table, perfectly mimicking the grotesque antics of the famous comic actor Tan Ba from the Lianjin troupe. The entire table convulsed with laughter, Guibao shaking like a branch of blossoms in the wind. Yang Fangyou had no choice but to down the cup. Sun Lianggong then plucked a sprig of plum blossom, tucked it jauntily behind his ear, seized a large cup, and crept stealthily toward Lu Zongyuan. Filling the cup, he crooned, 'The Metropolitan Censor Lu has always showered me with affection, I dedicate this cup to you.' Lu Zongyuan protested, 'How am I to manage such a massive cup?' Sun Lianggong teased, 'I assume the Censor prefers a "skin cup" (a kiss).' With that, he took a sip and pressed the rim against Zongyuan's lips. Zongyuan leaped up, laughing, 'Spare me the personal service.' The guests roared with amusement. Lianggong, unable to suppress his own laughter and failing to swallow the wine in time, sprayed it directly into Lu Zongyuan's face. The room erupted into sheer pandemonium. Lu Zongyuan scrambled for water to wash his face. The fourth cup involved the melon seed counting rule. Lianggong grabbed a handful and counted them out, twenty-five seeds, landing precisely on himself. Lu Zongyuan remarked, 'That is exceptionally fitting.' The fifth and sixth cups were the flying flower toast. Sun Lianggong gazed at Guibao and quoted, '"How can one bear to ask again of the rear courtyard flowers?"' He counted it out, and once more, the penalty fell upon him. Lianggong grumbled, 'Such wretched luck. Let me change a single word.'`,`The group protested unanimously, 'Absolutely not! Changing a single word incurs a ten-cup penalty.' Guibao poured a cup and announced, 'I invite Master Sun to savor the "Rear Courtyard Flowers" wine.' The table erupted into laughter once more. Lianggong pinched Guibao playfully and drank the wine. Next, it was Wang Wenhui's turn to issue a flying toast. Guibao directed a conspicuous pout toward Sun Lianggong, and Wenhui, catching the hint, recited, '"Peach blossoms drift lazily, following the falling willow flowers."' The count dictated that Lu Zongyuan and Sun Lianggong each drink a cup. Lu Zongyuan, still vexed by the wine sprayed in his face, quoted, '"Helplessly, the flowers fall and drift away."' Yang Fangyou quickly followed, 'Let us combine them and drink two cups together.' Lianggong agreed enthusiastically, 'Excellent, proceed.' Yang Fangyou recited, '"Laughing across the lotus flowers, sharing words with another."' Guibao poured two cups, and Sun Lianggong dutifully drained them. When it was Guibao's turn for the flying flower, he pondered for a moment before declaring, '"How lovely to catch the golden pollen beneath the flowers."' The count predictably landed on Lianggong again. The guests cheered, 'Splendid!' Lianggong protested vehemently, 'Foul, foul! This line is utterly fabricated, it belongs to no ancient poet.' Guibao retorted, 'Fabricated? It is a genuine line from the poetry of Lu Guimeng.' Zhou Xijue confirmed, 'He is quite correct, you cannot escape this cup.' Lianggong grumbled, 'He pondered for an age, deliberately engineering it to land on me. If he can recite two more lines off the cuff that land on me, I will drink.' Guibao challenged, 'Truly? No retreating?' Lianggong affirmed, 'No retreating.' Guibao rattled off three lines in rapid succession: '"Do you recall the fragrant flowers beneath the full moon?"', '"Rinsing the teeth before the flowers, the wine half-consumed"', and '"In the upper chamber, the flower branch smiles, sleeping alone."' The guests applauded wildly, praising his ingenuity. Left with no avenue of escape, Lianggong downed three and a half cups in succession. The final cup fell to Zhou Xijue, who recited, '"The flying flowers settle silently, a pair of swallows."' Lianggong lamented, 'Look at you all, conspiring together to force one man to drink all the wine.' He pleaded with Zhou Xijue to drink on his behalf, but Zhou steadfastly refused. Lianggong threatened, 'Shall I impersonate a female performer to present it to you again?' Zhou Xijue laughed heartily, 'Spare me, I shall drink it for you.' This sparked another round of laughter. Guibao smirked, 'This flying flower game is inherently unfair. I possess a flying flower game of absolute impartiality.' He crushed several plum blossoms, placing the petals in his palm, and announced, 'I shall blow on them, whoever they land upon must drink.' Lianggong, grinning broadly, watched Guibao and said, 'Excellent. Try blowing once, let us see who falls victim.' Guibao deliberately cast his gaze toward the door and said, 'Master Sun, a messenger from your household has arrived.' As Lianggong reflexively turned his head, Guibao blew forcefully, plastering Lianggong's face with petals. Flushed and sweating from the copious wine, the petals adhered stubbornly to his skin, with one drifting into his nostril, provoking a massive sneeze that sent the table into hysterics. Lu Zongyuan quipped, 'What a magnificent painted face, you scarcely need theatrical makeup.' Sun Lianggong hurriedly brushed the petals away. Unrelenting, Guibao flew one final line: '"The idle flowers effortlessly manifest the visage of spring."' The guests laughed and offered renewed praise. Lianggong was preparing to lunge at Guibao in mock outrage when an actual servant entered and whispered two words in Lianggong's ear. All color drained from Lianggong's face as he instructed the servant, 'Return first, I shall follow immediately.' He turned to Wang Wenhui, 'I have consumed too much wine, please, let us hasten the rice.' Wenhui and the guests instantly grasped the situation, exchanging knowing smiles and nods. Guibao teased, 'I wager the mistress has sent for you. Tarry any longer, and you shall be punished by having to balance an oil lamp on your head.' The guests roared with laughter once more. Wenhui remarked, 'Splendid, now you have drawn the entire table into the jest.' Lianggong penalized Guibao a cup, then, thoroughly flustered and anxious, frantically demanded the rice. Once they had finished eating and rinsed their mouths, the guests dispersed in unison.`,"Wenhui rewarded Guibao with twenty taels of silver. After expressing his gratitude, Guibao made his way to the study in search of Wang Xun and Zhongqing. They conversed for a time before Guibao revealed, 'Two new performers have recently joined our troupe, one named Qin Guan, the other Qi Guan. Both are exquisitely beautiful and remarkably talented. I fear Shi Zhujun's Selection of Flowers will require yet another printing.' He lingered a while longer before finally departing for home. As for what unfolded next, you must listen to the following chapter for the explanation."],g=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations2:t},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n={3:[`Wei Pincai returned to find that dinner had already been served in the study. Finding himself at a loss, he considered venturing out to a restaurant for a light repast. As he approached the accounts room, he caught sight of a young page bearing a large square tray—upon it rested a steaming hotpot and two freshly prepared dishes, all carried briskly inside. The head steward, Xu Shun, trailed closely behind. Catching sight of Pincai, Xu Shun paused to ask, "Has the young master dined?" Pincai replied, "I have only just returned from an errand." Xu Shun offered, "If you have yet to eat, why not join us here in the accounts room?" This Xu Shun and his wife were part of Lady Yan's dowry household, entrusted with managing all the family's financial affairs. Pincai stepped inside, where Xu Shun graciously urged him to eat first. Pincai politely declined, drawing Xu Shun to sit and share the meal together.`,"When the meal concluded, Xu Shun brewed a bowl of deep, fragrant tea, offering it to Pincai as they fell into idle conversation.",`Glancing at the wall clock, Pincai noted it was just past one in the afternoon. His gaze drifted to a purple-bamboo bookshelf, where a few worn volumes caught his eye. Pulling out two at random, he found them to be hand-copied opera librettos, containing nothing more than a few scattered arias from *The Peony Pavilion* and *The Palace of Eternal Life*. He then selected a slender, woodblock-printed book of twenty-odd pages titled *Qutai Huaxuan*. Leafing through it, he realized it was a critique and ranking of the capital's young *dan* actors. Picking up another incomplete volume, he found it to be *Pieced White Fur*. Pincai mused aloud, "Are these books yours? You must have a keen ear for music." Xu Shun laughed, "What do I know of music? I have no idea which of the masters left them here." When Pincai asked to borrow them, Xu Shun readily agreed. Tucking them into his sleeve, Pincai returned to his room and reclined on the heated brick bed. He studied the *Selection of Flowers* at length, committing the names of eight renowned performers to memory. "So the capital boasts such extraordinary talents," he reflected. "No wonder provincial folks always say, 'For true opera, one must go to the capital.' These actors are not only unparalleled in beauty but also possess consummate skills and literary refinement—truly living up to their names."`,'"By this measure," he mused further, "though Qinguan possesses an otherworldly beauty, even he might not surpass this echelon of performers."',`Yet another thought gently took root: "Perhaps the praises sung in these pages are somewhat embellished. In sheer physical grace, I doubt anyone in this world could eclipse Qinguan." He closed his eyes, vividly summoning the images of the eight performers described, etching them into his mind. Just then, Si'er came running in, breathless: "Master Ye, who traveled with you on the boat, is looking for the young master—he is waiting in the accounts room." Pincai remarked, "How curious—why would he come here?" He slid the *Catalogue of Flowers* beneath his dressing box, latched the door behind him, and made his way out.`,`Upon entering the accounts room, he found Ye Maolin seated beside an unfamiliar, pale, and stout gentleman. At Pincai's entrance, both men rose to greet him warmly. Pincai asked, "Master Ye, what brings you to our humble residence?" Maolin beamed, "Knowing Your Excellency was here, I came especially to pay my respects." Sensing the polite fiction, Pincai replied, "I have yet to call upon you, and here you have troubled yourself to visit me first." He then gestured to the stranger, "And your honorable companion?" Ye Maolin introduced him: "This is Master Jin the Second, the head manager of our troupe. He has come to invite Lord Mei to arrange a performance." Before Pincai could inquire further, Xu Shun descended the stairs, announcing: "The Master has decreed that since the first five days of the New Year are already booked, the sixth or seventh will suffice—provided there is no splitting of contracts." Master Jin sighed, "'No splitting' is a promise I dare not make. During the first month of the year, let alone our Lianjin Troupe, any decent company must split its performances two or three ways a day. Master Xu, I must trouble you to relay this once more." Xu Shun shook his head: "The word has been given; returning to press the matter would only invite a rebuff. Better to negotiate directly with Lord Wang." Jin asked, "And the date?" Xu Shun replied, "Settle all matters with Lord Wang. The sixth or the seventh—simply fix a day and be done with it." Ye Maolin added, "On your way back from Lord Wang's residence, you must pass by here. I shall wait; you and Master Xu can finalize the terms, and we can depart together." Master Jin agreed, departing with Xu Shun. Turning to Pincai, Ye Maolin asked, "Have you yet attended the theater in the capital?" Pincai confessed he had not. Maolin launched into an effusive description—of the dazzling costumes, the masterful casts, the exquisite attire of the *dan* actors, the vibrant atmosphere of the playhouses, and the opulent grandeur of private banquets. Pincai listened, thoroughly enchanted. Inquiring after their fellow passengers from the boat, he learned that Qinguan was residing with Cao Changqing; having suffered a brief illness, he was now on the mend.`,`Qiguan was slated to make his stage debut on the tenth day of the twelfth month. The others had followed their respective masters—some joining the Lianjin Troupe, others scattering to different companies. Pincai asked for Ye’s residence and was told he resided at the Lianjin Troupe's main quarters in Yangliu Lane. "I shall come to pay my respects soon," Pincai promised. "I would not dare presume," Maolin replied humbly, "only drop by if you are passing through." Presently, Xu Shun and Master Jin returned, having struck an agreement: the performance was set for the sixth day of the first month at the Suzhou Guild Hall. Whether the contract was split mattered little, so long as the requested actors performed without omission. Xu Shun ascended to report the finalized terms, the deposit was paid, and the party dispersed. That evening was reserved for Ziyu's studies, leaving no time for idle chatter with Pincai.`,"The following morning, with Ye Maolin's vivid descriptions lingering in his mind, Pincai finished his breakfast, resolved to attend a play. He instructed Si'er to carry some coin and changed into fresh garments. Knowing Yuanmao was immersed in his studies, Pincai departed alone on foot. The theater district was a short distance away, a bustling thoroughfare lined with five distinct playhouses, choked with carriages and horses, making passage arduous. Scanning the playbills for the Lianjin Troupe, he found they had no public performance that day, having been summoned for a private engagement. Mildly disappointed, he sought out another company. The resonant clash of gongs and drums soon drew him past several storefronts to a theater named the Sanyue Garden, home to the Lianzhu Troupe. Stepping inside, he beheld a sea of patrons—the upper galleries, the lower boxes, and the central pit were filled to bursting. The performance was on the verge of commencing. An usher swiftly approached, guiding Pincai to a table near the stage entrance, set against the wall. Seeing Pincai had brought no cushion, the usher provided one, arranged his seat, and served a pot of tea alongside a glowing incense coil. Before long, the curtains rose. The opening acts, however, were entirely unremarkable.","Casting his gaze to the upper gallery, Pincai noticed a party of men bearing the distinguished air of capital officials, flanked by a retinue of attendants. By the curtained entrance to the dressing rooms, several young *dan* actors lingered, their snow-white profiles partially visible as they smiled toward the officials. Soon, they gathered in small clusters of three and five, ascending the stairs to offer their greetings. Observing them from afar, Pincai noted their varied charms—some possessed a tranquil elegance, others a spirited vivacity, and some a playful mischief. Their garments were breathtakingly opulent: sea-dragon fur, fox-leg trim, otter, and dyed sable. Each face was carved of jade and dusted with snow, radiating a floral grace and willowy allure. They leaned intimately against patrons, sat closely by their sides, or rested gentle hands upon their shoulders, laughing and chatting in a delightful flurry of attention. Pincai was utterly spellbound.","His attention then drifted to a previously empty private box, now occupied by a newly arrived patron. The man was powerfully built, with a swarthy, darkened complexion. Draped in a silver-needle sea-dragon fur cloak, he exuded an arrogant, overwhelming presence. Though barely thirty, he was trailed by three or four impeccably dressed servants. He had brought his own heavy pewter teapot, covered teacups, and a water-pipe, covering the table in an extravagant display before taking his seat. Almost instantly, a flock of young *dan* actors swarmed the box, packing the grand official’s seating to the brim. The man’s demeanor was insufferably haughty; he cast his gaze about with self-satisfied pride, barking orders at his servants to purchase an endless stream of delicacies. When a dish displeased him, he hurled it to the floor, cursing his attendants ruthlessly. Pincai, listening to his strange, grating accent, could not decipher which province the man hailed from.","While lost in observation, Pincai realized two gentlemen had arrived at his own table. Turning to look, he took their measure.","One was a man of considerable girth; the other, dark, lean, and sporting a sparse beard. Both were clad in sumptuous finery, appearing to be in their thirties, and were accompanied by two young *dan* actors engaged in idle chatter. A servant unrolled their seat cushions, and they squeezed together at the table. Pincai eavesdropped briefly, appraising the two accompanying actors—they seemed rather ordinary, possessing no transcendent beauty. Suddenly, a young *dan* from the raucous official’s box cast a glance in their direction, gracefully excused himself, and approached to greet the stout man and his companion. With Pincai present, the table now seated five, and the stout man’s sheer bulk left little room for the new arrival. Assuming Pincai was part of their company, the young actor offered him a slight, courteous bow. Pincai, ever attuned to social graces, promptly shifted his weight, creating a sliver of space. As the actor settled in and inquired after Pincai’s surname, Pincai eagerly obliged and prepared to ask for his in return. Before a word could be spoken, however, the stout man reached over and firmly grasped the actor's arm.",`The actor bristled slightly. "Why must you grip so fiercely?" he murmured, turning to sit facing the stout man, delicately resting one hand on his shoulder. The two actors who had arrived earlier promptly stood, flicked their sleeves in mild disdain, and departed. The stout man's voice boomed: "Rongguan, why has neither hide nor hair been seen of you these past two or three months? You never venture into the city to visit me, you sought-after darling. I waited half the day for you at Sixiang Hall, and you never showed. What is the meaning of this?" Rongguan’s cheeks flushed a delicate pink. Taking the stout man’s hand, he replied softly, "Third Master is in a foul temper today. When you summoned me to Sixiang Hall, I fully intended to come, but I simply could not slip away. By the time I could, it was late—once inside the city walls, one cannot easily leave. Residing in your study is a joy, and the Third Mistress dotes upon me. Yet, I cannot bear the clamor of Concubine Qing scolding the boys, beating the maids, and shattering porcelain, only to brawl with Concubine Bai. You are left caught in the middle, utterly helpless. And come morning, I am left stranded in the study while you hide away, refusing to show your face!"`,'Before Rongguan could finish, the stout man erupted into laughter, his eyes crinkling until they vanished into mere slits. He affectionately pinched Rongguan’s lips, scolding gently, "You sharp-tongued little imp. That was but a rare occurrence—it is not as if they brawl every day." Pincai, listening intently, found the banter delightfully sharp. He took the opportunity to study Rongguan’s features more closely: the youth was exquisitely captivating, no more than fifteen or sixteen, possessing a flawless melon-seed face. His elegantly swept brows were dark as painted ink, his luminous eyes rippled like autumn waters, and deep dimples graced his cheeks. A tiny gold ring glinted in one ear. Clad in resplendent silks, he emanated a heady, intoxicating fragrance. Rongguan cast a knowing sidelong glance at the stout man, teasing, "Third Master, you play the victim too well. Word has it you frequent the Quanfu Troupe, squandering three thousand strings of cash to buy out Xiaofu’s indenture. Look there—Xiaofu sits in the opposite gallery, yet he does not even deign to cross the room to greet you." The stout man protested, "What absolute drivel! I have seen Xiaofu barely twice. Who claims I bought his contract? You are nothing but a peddler of rumors." Rongguan smiled slyly, "I merely repeat the whispers of others." Turning his attention to the lean man, Rongguan noted, "The Great Lord has no ear for *Kunqu*, I see. He prefers the high-pitched arias and variety acts." The lean man nodded, "It is not a lack of fondness, but a lack of comprehension. I simply cannot grasp the lyrics. The high-pitched operas possess a certain raw flavor, and the clapper-operas, at the very least, sing a tale one can follow clearly." Pincai absorbed their exchange while keeping one eye on the stage.','The third act was *The Southern Shore*, a universally beloved scene. Pincai tapped his foot gently against the bench in time with the rhythm, poured himself a fresh cup of tea, and sipped it slowly. Just then, the stout man moved to stand. Seeking balance, he pressed his hand heavily upon Rongguan’s shoulder. Rongguan swayed, inadvertently colliding with Pincai’s arm. The teacup tipped, spilling its contents and leaving a large, dark stain across Pincai’s robe. The stout man and Rongguan were instantly mortified, offering profuse apologies. Pincai, feeling a touch of embarrassment himself, smiled warmly, "It is of no consequence at all. It will dry in a moment." With that, he drew his handkerchief and calmly dabbed at the silk.',`As the opera continued, an elderly, stooped man approached. A grotesque, sack-like tumor hung heavily from his neck. In his hands, he carried a small, yellow-lacquered wooden tray brimming with jade trinkets and vividly colored curios. "Buy a piece of jade," he murmured softly. "Have a look at the jade." Threading through the dense crowd, he stopped beside Pincai, pinching a yellow snuff bottle between his fingers. "Care for a snuff bottle, sir?" Finding the color exceptionally rich, Pincai accepted the piece, turning it over in his hands before inquiring the price. The jade peddler replied, "This amber bottle is a genuine antique, sir. If it pleases you, take it. To anyone else, the price is twelve taels of silver—not a copper less. For you, I will part with it for ten." Pincai had assumed the trinket was worth no more than a few hundred cash; stunned by the exorbitant demand, he hastily offered it back. The peddler refused to take it, insisting, "Sir, having asked the price, you are bound by custom to make an offer. Look at this piece—it is aged, possesses a wide chamber, rests warmly in the palm, and has a pleasing heft. It suits a gentleman perfectly. You must, at the very least, offer a price." Cornered, Pincai threw out a careless sum: "Two taels of silver." The peddler snatched the bottle back, lamenting the offer was impossibly low—insufficient to buy even a crude imitation. After a calculated pause, he sighed, "Very well. You are my first customer of the day. If your intent is true, call it six taels." Pincai shook his head firmly, "No, thank you." The old man let out a mournful sigh, "Business is a bitter struggle these days, and the southern gentlemen are far too shrewd. Six taels for an antique amber bottle... fine, take it for two. I shall rely entirely upon your future patronage." He thrust the bottle back into Pincai's hands. Pincai, having brought no silver, had intentionally lowballed the exorbitant ten-tael asking price, certain the man would refuse. Caught entirely off guard by the acceptance, he found himself paralyzed, staring blankly at the stage, his face flushing crimson.`,`The peddler, a seasoned trickster adept at reading faces, instantly recognized Pincai as a newcomer to the capital and seized the advantage. "I have peddled jade for forty years, traversing dozens of playhouses, and never once have I met a gentleman who strikes a bargain only to rescind it! Do not hoard your two taels, sir. Do not play me false." He pressed aggressively against Pincai, keeping the bottle firmly in the young man's grasp. Left with no recourse, Pincai confessed, "I carry no silver upon my person today. Bring it tomorrow, and I shall pay you in full." The peddler sneered in disbelief, "No silver? Then pay with a bank note." Pincai shook his head, "I possess no notes either." The peddler pressed closer, "Then I shall accompany Your Excellency to your residence to collect." Pincai replied, "I live far from here." The old man acted deaf to the excuse, leaning heavily against Pincai, the bottle practically trapped between them. On stage, the melody shifted to a *Erhuang* aria. A young *dan* had barely stepped into the light, lips parted to sing, when a solitary voice in the crowd roared, "Bravo!" Instantly, the theater erupted—dozens of voices from the pit to the rafters joined in a deafening, thunderous cheer, startling as a fire gong. Pincai flinched violently. His arm jerked, striking the peddler's hand. With a sharp crack, the amber-colored pine-resin bottle shattered on the floor. Pincai froze in dismay. The peddler, unhurried and unnervingly calm, knelt to gather the shards, placing them methodically beside Pincai. "It seems the gentleman has a temper," he drawled. "He rejects the whole, preferring the broken. Let us settle this amicably: the intact bottle was six taels; the fragments are six large strings of cash—twelve strings of capital cash in total." Pincai’s embarrassment flared into anger. "You are entirely devoid of reason! A moment ago, you demanded two taels; now you demand six. Is this not blatant extortion?" The surrounding patrons murmured in sympathetic outrage on Pincai’s behalf.`,`Just as Pincai was about to lose his temper, the stout man reached across the table, seizing the peddler by the arm. Pointing a thick finger, he warned, "Old Wang, enough of your tricks." Pincai offered a grateful nod, but the stout man’s anger was genuine. "Old Wang, do not try your swindles here. A lump of pine-resin is barely worth a hundred cash, yet you tried to fleece him for two taels. Now that it is broken, you demand six? You mark a southerner unfamiliar with your cons and move in for the kill. Not while I, Fu, sit at this table." The peddler’s bravado vanished instantly. "Third Master, whatever you decree, so it shall be." The stout man motioned to his servant to hand the peddler four hundred cash. The old man opened his mouth to protest, but the lean man cut him off. "Third Master Fu patronizes you constantly. Why squabble over such a trifle? He acts on behalf of a friend. Next time you procure decent jade, they will offer you their custom. Leave it at that." Rongguan chimed in with a delicate sneer, "This old relic is an absolute plague. He hobbles through the aisles, thrusting that wretched tray at everyone, without a single piece of merit to his name. If one truly desires jade, does the Degu Zhai shop not exist?" Swallowing his bile, the peddler scooped up the broken shards and shuffled away, muttering venomously under his breath, "Striking poses, playing the noble friend—'I will offer you my custom,' he says! If he has coin, he wastes it all on pretty boys." He melted back into the throng. Overwhelmed with gratitude, Pincai clasped Fu the Third’s hand warmly. "My clumsiness has caused Third Master undue aggravation." He offered a respectful bow to the lean man as well, then instructed Si'er to present two hundred large cash to the stout man with both hands. Fu the Third laughed heartily, "What nonsense is this?" Taking the coins, he tossed them directly back to Si'er. "Consider it accepted. Now take it back." Si'er hesitated, terrified to comply. Pincai smiled, "I cannot possibly allow Third Master to suffer a loss on my account. Please, accept it." He tried handing the money to Fu's servant, but Fu snatched it and tossed it onto the table with a sigh. "You are far too pedantic! What is a handful of copper? Stop this endless pushing back and forth." Relenting, Pincai ordered Si'er to pocket the money and offer a deep bow of thanks. Having heard the crowd refer to his savior as 'Fu the Third,' Pincai asked the lean man's name. The man introduced himself as Gui Fen, a seventh-rank minor official serving in one of the Ministries. Fu the Third’s full name was Fu Lun, a second-rank hereditary degree holder, currently serving as a secretary in the Ministry of Revenue. The men exchanged formal courtesies.`,`Fu and Gui returned the inquiry, asking Pincai’s origins and current posting. Pincai replied humbly, "I hail from Jiangning Prefecture. Having only recently arrived in the capital, I have not yet secured a position. I currently reside as a guest in the Mingke Lane estate of my family's old friend, Lord Mei." Fu the Third’s eyes lit up. "Jiangning is a magnificent place. In my youth, I accompanied my late father there when he served as Provincial Treasurer. I was but twelve years old. He was later promoted to Governor-General of Guangdong. The Lord Mei you speak of—he served as Education Commissioner in Guangdong and shared a deep bond with my father. Later, they both returned to the capital, where my father was appointed Vice Minister. Alas, within the year, he passed away."`,`"Lacking a scholar's discipline, I am unfit to mingle with such venerable elders, so I have let those ties fade over the years. I hear the Mei family's young master, Yuge, is exceedingly bright and strikingly handsome, perhaps sixteen or seventeen now. Has he taken a bride?" Pincai answered the inquiries gracefully, engaging Master Gui in polite conversation. He quickly discerned the nature of his companions: Fu the Third was a man of fiery generosity and deep sentiment, while Master Gui was cautious, proper, and anchored in convention. The three men conversed effortlessly. Meanwhile, Rongguan had drifted back to the opposite gallery to converse with the dark-faced behemoth.`,"From his seat, Pincai watched the jade peddler squeeze his way up to the upper gallery, clutching his remaining trinkets, attempting to hawk his wares among the actors and patrons. His persistence drew the ire of the dark-faced man, who barked a sharp reprimand. When the peddler lingered, muttering a faint retort, the towering man exploded in rage, ordering his servants to throw the old man out. The attendants fell upon the peddler without hesitation, shoving and kicking him violently. Realizing the peril of his situation, the old man abandoned his usual theatrics. Hunching his back, he scrambled toward the exit. In his haste to protect his tray, a cascade of glass snuff bottles and nozzles clattered to the floor, shattering instantly. As he bent to retrieve a piece, two more tumbled from the tray. For a fleeting second, he considered risking his life to extort this new assailant, but one glance at the man's terrifying visage convinced him otherwise—this was no reasonable soul like Fu the Third. Eyes brimming with humiliated tears, the old man stumbled down the stairs and fled the theater, cursing into the wind. The actors in the gallery erupted into peals of laughter, leaning over the railings to watch his undignified retreat before settling back into their seats. Down below, Fu the Third clapped his hands in grim satisfaction, while Pincai felt a profound sense of vindication. Yet, Pincai could not help but wonder about the identity of the fearsome, wealthy patron upstairs, resolving to ask Rongguan upon his return. Shortly after, the dark-faced man rose, gathered four young actors, and strode out with imperious arrogance. The remaining actors swiftly dispersed, seeking out other patrons.",`Moments later, Rongguan glided back to their table. Fu the Third teased him, "A waste of flattery, it seems. He didn't even take you with him. You hovered around him for half the day and couldn't even secure a dinner invitation." Rongguan nodded coolly. "Indeed, I hovered. But had he called for me, I would not have gone. That old patriarch is not a man one wishes to befriend."`,`Fu asked, "Where is he from, and what is his name?" Rongguan replied softly, "He hails from Guangdong. I only hear him called Great Lord Xi, and I have only just made his acquaintance. He is new to the capital, yet he treats Chunlan with obsessive favor. That black fox-leg coat Chunlan wore today? Slipped right off Lord Xi’s own back and sent straight to the furrier to be tailored down." Rongguan leaned in close, whispering something into Fu the Third's ear. Fu laughed, "How is it you find yourself with free time today?" Rongguan offered a radiant smile, resting both hands on Fu’s shoulders and giving them a gentle, affectionate knead.`,`Observing Pincai's elegant bearing and mindful of his connection to the Mei family, Fu the Third suggested, "Brother Wei, the opera has lost its flavor today. Shall we find a quiet place for a few cups of wine?" Recognizing Fu’s generous, open-hearted nature, Pincai sought to deepen their acquaintance. "It would be a great pleasure, provided you grant me the honor of playing host." Fu laughed heartily, "As you wish." He drew a small leather folio from his boot, extracted a banknote, and handed it to his servant to settle the bill—graciously covering Pincai's ticket as well. Pincai offered profound thanks. Together, with Rongguan in tow, they exited the theater.`,"They had arrived by carriage. Pincai shared a carriage with Rongguan, while Si'er rode the shaft, and Fu’s servant clung to the back. Within the carriage, Pincai conversed with effortless charm, drawing delighted laughter from Rongguan. He praised the youth's beauty, declaring him without equal in all the capital.",'Still immersed in lively conversation, they arrived at a refined restaurant, securing a secluded, elegant private room. A waiter appeared promptly to take their order, and Rongguan gracefully poured the wine. From the adjacent room drifted a chorus of voices, bright and musical as swallows and orioles. Peering through a slender crack in the wooden partition, Rongguan whispered, "It is that Great Lord Xi. He has Chunlan and three other actors with him." Turning back, Pincai respectfully addressed Fu the Third: "May I ask the honorable name of your late father?"',`Fu the Third looked mildly confused, but Master Gui understood perfectly. "He is asking for your father's official name," Gui clarified. Fu smiled, "You ask after my father? He was known as Fu Anshi." Pincai immediately rose from his seat, his expression filled with awe. "No wonder! Third Master is the descendant of a true sage. Your venerable father is revered as a living god in our Nanjing. Three years ago, the local populace gathered thousands of taels of silver to erect a shrine for distinguished officials, placing your father's tablet in the place of highest honor. His devotion to the people in life was legendary; after his passing, his spirit lingered to protect Nanjing. During times of plague, locusts, flood, or drought, he has manifested time and again—answering every prayer with miraculous efficacy. It is said the Governor-General intends to petition the throne to grant him posthumous titles. The people’s gratitude is boundless, and it is a universal belief that his descendants are destined for the highest echelons of power—to be named marquises and ministers. Seeing Third Master’s noble heart and distinguished bearing today, I have no doubt you will ascend to the very first rank." This eloquent homage left Fu the Third so profoundly moved and flattered that he found himself momentarily speechless.`,'Master Gui nodded thoughtfully. "The sentiment is entirely credible. His Excellency served in Jiangnan for many years, rising steadily from Prefect to Provincial Treasurer over more than a decade. It is only natural he forged a deep bond with the land and its people." Fu the Third recovered his voice, adding, "My father spent sixteen years in Jiangning, never once leaving the province as he climbed the ranks. He truly shared a destiny with the people of Nanjing. I was born within the walls of the Jiangning Prefectural compound, which is why I still carry a trace of the local dialect." Pincai then turned his silvery tongue toward Master Gui, offering a long string of elegant praises. Gui sighed modestly, "My path is already written in stone. Promotion relies entirely on selection—I shall end my days as a Subprefect or, at best, a Second-Class Subprefect. There is no other road for me." Pincai countered smoothly, "Surely a man of your caliber will not be halted at such ranks." Rongguan, eyes sparkling with amusement, leaned forward. "And what grand destiny do you foresee for me?" Pincai laughed warmly, "For you? You shall ascend to the Moon Palace and take your place among the immortals." Fu the Third and Master Gui erupted into laughter. Rongguan playfully poured Pincai a penalty cup of wine, teasing, "You have the tongue of a poet now! Where was this silver speech when that wretched jade peddler had you cornered?" Pincai chuckled, raising his cup. "Today, the heavens blessed me with the company of Third Master and Master Gui. Without you, I fear I would still be hopelessly entangled in his snare." Fu the Third shook his head, "Men of his ilk revere cruelty and prey on kindness. The softer your words, the harder they bite. Did you not see the brute upstairs cast him out, shattering half his livelihood? Yet the old swindler dared not utter a single breath of protest. Still, we are not tyrants; we do not revel in watching a poor man suffer. In truth, four hundred cash was far more than he deserved. That glass bauble was not worth a fraction of the price." Pincai praised his profound generosity, assuring him that such benevolence would surely summon immense cosmic blessings.',`Rongguan shuddered delicately. "Lord Xi’s men are utterly ruthless. The way they shoved and battered Old Wang—I feared he would fall and shatter that miserable tray of his entirely. It could have killed the old wretch! Luckily, only two glass nozzles and a snuff bottle were lost. One of the servants was particularly vicious; he struck Old Wang directly on that hideous tumor. The old man choked, his eyes rolling back until only the whites showed—it was truly horrifying. Lord Xi’s temper burns far too hot. Had his man struck a fatal blow, he would have had to pay with a life." As Rongguan spoke, a sudden, violent uproar erupted in the adjacent room. A harsh voice bellowed, "You put on airs again!" Rongguan lowered his voice to a terrified whisper. "Disaster. The Great Lord Xi has exploded again. Who is he cursing this time?" He pressed his eye to the crack in the partition. At their table, Pincai, Fu the Third, and Master Gui fell utterly silent, straining to listen. Through the thin wood, a young actor's trembling voice pleaded, "You open your mouth only to hurl insults." The deep voice roared back, "I despise nothing more than those who put on airs—wearing a new face for every day of the week!" The actor retorted, a hint of defiance in his tone, "Even if I do put on airs, you have no right to beat me to death for it!" The man snarled, "I won’t beat you to death—I will squeeze the very life from your bones!" A sharp *crack* echoed as a wine cup shattered against the wall. The man sneered, "That sound is too fragile. Let us smash something grander." The actor snapped back, "If you seek loud noises—" Another deafening crash followed as a heavy porcelain bowl was destroyed. The man raged, "You think only you can smash things?" He hurled another dish to the floor. The actor cried, "If you wish to destroy, who is stopping you?" A chaotic symphony of destruction ensued—*ding, ding, dang, dang*—as several more pieces met their end. Pushed beyond the brink of sanity, the man roared, "You smash things beautifully!" With a terrifying crash that dwarfed all the rest, he heaved the entire banquet table over. One of the three accompanying actors fled the room in sheer terror. The remaining two scrambled frantically to mediate, their voices a desperate chorus of "Great Lord!" "Godfather!" "Dear Father!" begging him to calm his wrath. The actor who had dared smash the bowls dashed into the courtyard, collapsing into violent, racking sobs. The restaurant proprietor and a fleet of waiters rushed in, paralyzed by fear, offering terrified, obsequious smiles and murmuring a litany of "Great Lord" over and over.`,`The proprietor hurried to the courtyard to console the weeping actor, his voice dripping with syrupy reassurance. "Chunlan, why must you war with the Great Lord? Look at this magnificent new black fox-leg coat—it is splattered with grease! Quickly, fetch some spirits to clean it." Servants rushed forward with alcohol, and the proprietor meticulously scrubbed the fur clean. He then guided the seething Lord Xi into a fresh, private chamber, dragging the reluctant actor forward to offer an apology. Chunlan merely wept, stubbornly refusing to bow. Seeing the proprietor’s frantic, groveling efforts, Lord Xi felt a flicker of embarrassment. "I have brought chaos to your establishment," he muttered. "This boy grows more insufferable by the day." The proprietor, acting as proxy, bowed deeply and repeatedly on the actor's behalf, successfully soothing the wealthy patron’s wounded pride. Slowly, the actor's tears subsided.`,`The proprietor corralled the remaining three actors into the new room, dispatching his staff to recreate the feast exactly as before, demanding the finest porcelain to pacify the Great Lord's residual ire. In the hallway, he furiously berated his head waiter. "Old Three, you are an absolute fool! The shattering of fine porcelain is the sweetest music there is. You should have served him on our thinnest, most exquisite bowls—they shatter with a crisp, crystalline ring that would have delighted the Great Lord immensely. This half-coarse rubbish merely clunks dullly against the floor. He intended to tip you fifty strings of cash, and now you shall receive only forty!" The junior staff erupted into muffled laughter as they scrambled to sweep the debris. The opulent feast scattered across the floor had already been rapidly devoured by four massive stray dogs that had slipped inside.`,"The dogs, having gorged themselves, fell into a vicious, snarling brawl amidst the wreckage. The waiters descended upon them with sticks and brooms, violently driving the hounds into the street before finishing their frantic cleanup.","Why, one might ask, did the proprietor debase himself so utterly for this Lord Xi? He knew the man was a colossal Cantonese plutocrat—a young heir of unimaginable wealth who had descended upon the capital bearing over a hundred thousand taels of silver, fully intending to purchase a prestigious official post. He had been in the city for merely a month.","Yet, he visited this restaurant almost daily, pouring over a thousand strings of cash into the proprietor's coffers. A banquet such as this, factoring in the shattered porcelain, would be billed at five or six times its actual cost; a fifty-string feast would effortlessly transform into a three-hundred-string invoice.","Lord Xi reveled in this extravagant cup of pleasure; inflate the bill as one might, he paid without a whisper of complaint, utterly refusing to appear stingy. He was a quintessential southern profligate, determined to carve out a reputation in the capital as a man of boundless, reckless wealth. Thus far, society knew only that he was the eleventh son of his clan, dubbing him 'Xi the Eleventh.' The defiant actor who had shattered the bowls alongside him was none other than Chunlan, the youth Rongguan had spoken of earlier.",`Fu the Third, Pincai, and Master Gui watched the spectacle from the doorway for a long while before returning to their seats. Rongguan stuck out his tongue playfully. "Terrifying! Now that is what I call a proper spectacle." Fu the Third laughed, "Such crude spectacles hold no true interest. But what exactly ignited the quarrel, I wonder?" Rongguan sighed, "Lord Xi's vulgar, venomous temper defies description." The men dismissed the drama, indulging in a few rounds of finger-guessing games. As evening draped its shadows over the city, they knew they must return within the walls. Master Gui moved to settle the bill, but Pincai insisted on hosting. Fu the Third waved them both down. "Let there be no competition. Let old Fu the Third handle this trivial sum. Tomorrow, you shall host; the day after, he shall. No arguments." Deferring to his generosity, Fu paid the tab. Fu and Gui rose to take their leave. Pincai offered his warmest compliments, his heart genuinely gladdened by the day's encounters. Asking for their addresses, he promised to call upon them the following day. Fu the Third instructed, "I reside in the East City, on the west side of Jinpai Tower Lane, directly across from the tea shop." He pointed a thumb at Master Gui, "He lives next door to the tea shop, his gates sealed with the official edicts of the Ministry of Revenue. Should you visit tomorrow, we will be waiting."`,`Pincai bowed deeply. "I shall certainly come. Let us count ourselves friends from this day forth. Yet I am but a commoner, without rank or title. I hardly dare aspire to your exalted company." Fu the Third and Master Gui objected in unison: "You must drink a penalty cup for such words! What do empty titles matter among brothers? So long as you do not find our company too crude, that is all that matters." Before departing, Fu the Third pressed eight strings of cash into Rongguan's hands, and two strings upon his young attendant. Rongguan offered profound thanks, bidding farewell to Master Gui and Pincai before taking his leave.`,"The sun had sunk low into the west; Fu and Gui hurried back toward the city gates. Pincai escorted them to their carriage, watching them depart before slowly walking the darkening streets home with Si'er. By the time they arrived, the estate was bathed in lamplight. Ziyu and Yuanmao were sequestered in the study, immersed in their evening lessons. Pincai changed out of his street clothes, slipped into comfortable shoes, drank several cups of hot tea, and sat in quiet contemplation.","Shortly thereafter, Ziyu and Yuanmao emerged from their studies, making their way to Pincai’s room.",`Pincai had untied his heavy waist-pouch, tucking one hand casually into the lapel of his robe, letting his empty sleeve sway rhythmically as he paced slow circles around the room. Seeing Ziyu and Yuanmao enter, his face broke into a radiant grin. Yuanmao asked, "What kept you abroad until this late hour?" Peering closer, he added, "You reek of wine! Did Ye Maolin invite you out? Did you happen to see any of those young actors?" Pincai laughed, "I did not seek out Ye Maolin. Instead, I attended a performance by the Lianzhu Troupe. That company boasts fifty or sixty young actors, all possessing exceptional grace. I happened upon an old acquaintance—the son of the former Jiangning Provincial Treasurer, a man bound to us by generational friendship. He invited me to dine and summoned a young *dan* of the very finest caliber." Ziyu, intrigued, asked, "Elder Brother, the other day you mentioned that Qinguan possessed a difficult temper and was prone to weeping. What manner of temperament is it, exactly?" Pincai paused, his eyes distant. "Qinguan’s nature is an extreme rarity. When he was cast into this world, I suspect the King of Hell embedded a shard of pure crystal in his chest—cold, unyielding, devoid of a single drop of mercy or tenderness. To speak to him of affection is a fool's errand. I was entirely captivated by his beauty; I trailed after him like a devoted slave, showering him with endless flattery. Yet, not only did I fail to coax a single smile from his lips, but on several occasions, my efforts drove him to tears! How does one even describe such a spirit? To put it plainly: in his eyes, there is simply no one in this world worthy of his regard." Ziyu pondered this deeply. *If his nature is truly so uncompromising,* he thought, *then he must be a soul of the highest, purest order—flawless in every conceivable way.* He fell into a profound reverie. *In this vast, churning sea of ordinary men, with their vulgar eyes and unrefined ears, everyone is desperate to flatter and possess. A beauty so pure, so fiercely self-respecting, must endure cold contempt and misunderstanding at every turn. Unless a fellowship of upright, noble gentlemen unites to shelter and nurture him—shielding him from the grasping hands of the scheming and the base—there is no hope for such a spirit to bloom. A soul whose voice rings clear as struck metal, as flawless and self-contained as winter ice... if such a rare miracle could emerge from that sordid world and truly come into its own, it would be a phenomenon rarely witnessed by the ancients, let alone the modern age.* As these thoughts swirled through Ziyu’s mind, his heart felt like a willow catkin released from the branch, caught upon the wind, drifting inexorably toward Qinguan. Suddenly, the tranquility was shattered as Li Yuanmao threw open the vestibule door, his voice ringing out:`,'"Disaster has struck!" To discover the nature of this calamity, one must listen to the following chapter.']},c=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations3:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),i={4:[`To return to our story: Ziyu was brooding over Qinguan's heavy heart when he heard Yuanmao fling open the vestibule door and cry out, "Something terrible has happened!" Startled, Ziyu and the others asked why he was making such a fuss. "Look at the ground," Yuanmao said, "it is already blanketed. With flakes falling as large as cotton bolls, we shall have over a foot of snow by morning." Ziyu and Pincai stepped to the door to look, and indeed, snowflakes were drifting and swirling down from the sky. Ziyu remarked, "This winter solstice snow is the finest kind. All season the wind has blown dry; now that it snows at last, it is surely because our speaking of Qinguan has moved Heaven to harmony, bringing auspicious signs on every side." Pincai replied, "If it snows the whole night through, tomorrow we may go out to admire it." By then, Yun'er had already fetched cloaks and wind-hoods, helping Ziyu put them on before leading him back inside.`,"That night, a full five inches of snow fell. By daybreak, sharp gusts of the north wind swept through in waves, bringing a bitter, piercing cold.","The snow had only just ceased. It was truly a world adorned in jade and glass, the heavens and earth carved in pure white—a scene of surpassing beauty. Master Li Xingquan had risen early and braved the chill, only to develop a dizzy head and a cough. Feeling out of sorts, he returned to bed and excused Ziyu and the others from their studies. Being well-versed in pharmacology, he had his page-boy fetch several doses of dispersing medicine, swallowed them down, and went to sleep with his head covered. Ziyu instructed the two page-boys to wait quietly outside the study, then retreated to a small three-room studio named the Twenty-Four-Qin Study. The plaque above the door bore the calligraphy of his grandfather, the late Duke Wenmu. Having nothing else to do, Ziyu took out Xie Huilian's Rhapsody on Snow and began to read.",`When he reached the lines, "The white crane loses its freshness, the silver pheasant its pallor," he paused to marvel at the ancients' skill in painting scenes with words. Just then, the sky darkened once more. Snow began to drift down anew, and the flakes resting on the rooftops and branches were swept up by the wind, scattering in a chaotic dance like falling pear blossoms. He immediately instructed Yun'er to have the kitchen prepare extra dishes, and invited the young gentlemen Wei and Li to come and admire the snow. Before long, a table of fine delicacies was laid out, and Pincai and Yuanmao arrived to enjoy the snowy vista together.`,"Unable to drink wine, Ziyu merely made a show of accompanying the others. He turned again to Pincai to ask after Qinguan. Seeing how entirely Ziyu's thoughts were captivated, Pincai shifted his tone, speaking of Qinguan's bearing and temperament with nothing but soaring praise, leaving Ziyu all the more ardently enamored. Ziyu thought to himself how, on such a snowy day, to behold one who stood like a jade branch amid ice blossoms would be no different from gazing upon the flowers of a fairy isle. He could only laugh at the brocade-tented banquets of warlords, where fine wine and roasted lamb could never mask the crude swagger of martial men. When the meal was finished, they melted snow to brew tea, conversed leisurely for a time, and then Pincai and Yuanmao returned to their own rooms.",`Suddenly, Juner arrived bearing a letter. The envelope was inscribed, "For the hand of Master Mei," with a line of smaller characters beside it. Inside lay a single sheet of note-paper and four sheets of verse-paper. Recognizing Zhongqing's calligraphy, Ziyu asked Juner who had delivered it. "It was brought by Young Master Yan's personal attendant," Juner replied. Ziyu asked him to wait a moment, then broke the seal. The note read: "Yesterday, Yong'an and I sat together in a desolate room. Only when our jade cups grew heavy with cold did we realize the six-pointed flowers had begun to fly; only when the silver candle-light guttered did we perceive the snow's full, radiant beauty. Ice mountains piled upon ice mountains, ranged like mica folding screens; pagoda upon pagoda lit from within, gleaming like glass lanterns. Beauties finished their toilette to toy with jade cats; arhats gathered in heaps to toss balls at lions. At dusk we chose rhymes, engaging in a white war of verse and setting topics. Ashamed at the poverty of our jeweled lines, we offer these common bricks in hopes of inviting your jade in return. Respectfully presented for your keen judgment; we eagerly await your pearled reply. To my worthy younger brother Yuxiang. Yong'an sends his regards as well. Penned in haste by Jiantan."`,'Ziyu read it and exclaimed, "What an exquisitely wrought letter!" He then turned to the verse sheets, which bore the title, Eight Odes from the Snow Window.',`Snow Mountain
This peak has surely flown here from afar,
one lotus of white jade, bursting into bloom.
In clogs, how fine to chant of catkins by the pavilion;
on a donkey, how hard to seek the plum along the ridge.
Look again: it seems to drip, yet holds no verdant green;
though much may melt, how could it be the ash of ruin?
Deep in the night of cloud and rain the cold locks shut,
how could the goddess now descend from her terrace?

Snow Pagoda
The flower-scattering maiden nears the Brahma palace;
these tiers of treasure, all this splendor, are Heaven’s own craft.
Four eaves, at times, will stand in the sun’s way;
seven storeys, at every turn, will startle the wind.
In the moon, the holy relic—how brilliantly it blazes;
on the water, the floating spire—its color emptier still.
Were I but granted the mood to climb its highest peak,
I’d carve my name up there, and put my question to the sky.

Snow Screen
Last night the Liang Garden gave word that spring had come;
jade tables, pearl blinds—all vie, brand-new and bright.
Mica would serve to screen the flower-loving censor;
crystal should be bestowed on the lady of the tiger’s house.
The silver candle, unstirred, sheds a light gone cold;
paint the golden geese—still the dream will not hold.
How strange—the ring of singers all in mourning white;
should the chancellor draw near, he would surely take offense.

Snow Lantern
Trimming the wick, how often I have chanted the sharp-rhymed snow;
tonight the burning oil outshines the nine-flower lamp.
Weaving white silk, one need no longer face the wall;
whose is the house that reads with never a lamp alight?
The pure cold has burned three candles to their ends;
lighting my sleep, I still can see the six-fold flowers.
Remember now the Lantern Feast draws near;
you festive moths and withered willows, boast not against this glow.

—first draft by Wang Xun of the Yong’an studio.

Ziyu read them and remarked, "Splendid poems. Of these four, Snow Pagoda must rank first, Snow Screen second, Snow Mountain next, and Snow Lantern last. Now let us see Jiantan’s:"`,`Snow Lion
Uncanny form, taking shape as a long-haired beast;
called by name, the Bai Ze claims lordship by chance.
Riding the air, can it truly leap beyond the seas?
Driven by wind, it only roars along the eastern river.
A high golden throne is not hard to make brilliant;
clad in red trees and new finery—the workmanship, I fear, falls short.
If the Recluse of Longqiu were but to see it,
he would surely cast aside his staff and speak again of emptiness.

Ziyu thought to himself: The topic of Snow Lion is not easily handled; yet look how he weaves his allusions with effortless grace, perfectly suited to the subject. Only a master's hand could manage this. He read on:`,`Snow Cat
Playing go at random, pillowed on two dressing-cases;
the jade-like creature crouches by the carved eaves.
Coaxed away—how could fish threaded on willow bring it back?
Led off—it is best lured by rice wrapped in salt.
Resembling a tiger's head—there truly was an original;
but those mouse-like things only bask in others' warmth.
Today the red of peony has all flown away;
cold-eyed, there is no need to add a single thread.

"This one is even better," Ziyu said. "The third couplet is delightfully pointed." He read further, finding the next topics to be Snow Arhat and Snow Beauty. Ziyu reflected that these two topics were even more barren and still than the preceding six, making them exceedingly difficult to write upon. He found:`,`Snow Arhat
Who at dawn pushed open the gates of Chan?
Gautama faces the wall, his staff-ring returned.
Deliverance—the mind is like still water;
wandering freely—the intention settles on Cold Mountain.
Turning sutras: in the misty dimness, palm-leaf texts;
forming a society: lotus flowers arise in an instant.
This very body is the same as a phantom image;
do not sigh and shake your head at the stubbornness of stone.

Snow Beauty
Jade bones, fine and flawless, without a single blemish;
is it she, in the end, or is it not she?
A spring heart already like floss clinging to mud;
my face should be like flowers blooming in the rain.
In the deep of night, pondering—it has become flowing water;
in a former life, its flavor I recall as brewed tea.
Where now is the pearl-selling maidservant who attended her?
Leaning against the bamboo without a word, the sun slopes once more.

—Jiantan Zhongqing, final draft.

Ziyu read through to the end, softly intoning the verses several times. He felt that of Zhongqing's four poems, Snow Lion was gilded and richly patterned; Snow Cat was carved of jade and finely chiseled; Snow Arhat exhaled a clean fragrance, like a courtyard full of lotus; Snow Beauty was deft in both strokes at once, graceful and free as jade camellias—surpassing even Wang Xun's work. He thought he would compose eight matching poems using the same rhymes, but feared they could not equal the seamless completeness of the originals. It would be better to choose four new topics and avoid the same grooves. Those eight topics of theirs were all conceived from the end backward, turning vacancy into substance, defining the host through the guests. He would instead conceive his from a moment before the snow had even fallen—and simply use four abstract words joined to the character for snow as his topics. He thought: before the snow falls, when heavy clouds mass and a misty indistinctness prevails, the feeling of impending snow is already present. He would make Snow Feeling his first topic. When snowflakes begin to drift, indistinct and shadowy, Snow Shadow emerges—that would be his second. When the snow first begins to fall, the granules pattering and rustling, it has a sound—Snow Sound would be his third. Once the snow has fully fallen, all is a brilliant expanse of white—that must be Snow Color, his fourth topic. Novel and original. With that, he set to composing. He had just completed two when Yuanmao and Pincai came in and saw him. Ziyu therefore invited them to compose a few verses as well. Yuanmao said:`,`"With a single abstract character appended to the word 'snow' as my topic, I simply cannot manage it. All I can do is write a plain poem on snow." Pincai added, "Even a plain snow poem—making it rhyme properly is hard work. I can only manage a quatrain." Yuanmao said, "Seven-character lines are too unwieldy; all I know is how to write a five-character regulated poem." Ziyu smiled. "Either will do perfectly well." They each went off to rack their brains.`,'Before long, Ziyu had completed all four of his poems and written them out on a sheet of cold-gold notepaper. He had just finished writing a reply and was about to seal it when Pincai came over smiling, carrying a sheet of verse: "It has not come out well. Please help me correct it." Ziyu took it and looked. The topic was Ode on Snow, and the poem read:',`Swirling toward the plum tips, petal by petal, slanting;
moths and powdered butterflies fill all the heavens and earth.
Clearly these are immortal products from the Jade Terrace above,
commanding alone the foremost flower of the human world.

Ziyu said in surprise, "I had no idea you possessed such skill. You must have put a great deal of effort into poetry." Pincai replied, "Where would I find the time for that? I just remembered a few arias and patched them together." Ziyu asked, "What arias?" Pincai explained, "The lines about swirling toward the plum tips, and moths and powdered butterflies, are from the Walking in Snow sequence in Snow on River and Sky." Ziyu asked, "And the last two lines?" Pincai said, "The third line is simply padding, and the final line borrows from the Exclusive Possession aria in the drama Winning the Flower Queen. I just commandeered it and used it as though it were a classical song." Ziyu said, "You have managed to patch it together without a single visible seam, which is no mean feat." By this time, Yuanmao had also finished, returning with his poem written out neatly and properly. Ziyu looked it over; it was rather difficult to follow, but he could only offer praise: "Very polished and well-balanced. Why not write both of them out and send them over so the others can have a look?" Yuanmao, hearing Ziyu's praise, was certain it must be very good indeed, and said, "Yes, let us invite their instruction." It was Pincai alone who knew his own worth, and he quickly interjected, "Mine had better not go—it would only make a spectacle of itself." Ziyu said, "What does it matter? I will write them out for you." He copied them onto a separate sheet of paper, added two more lines to the end of his reply letter, sealed the whole thing, and sent Yun'er to go with the attendant.`,`On the other side, Zhongqing received the reply note and read it together with Wang Xun. It read: "Your letter arrived like a drift of cloud; the words fell like fragrant snow. The lotus-lantern guttered in the Buddha-pagoda's delicate glow; the kingfisher screen grew cold as one traced the fairy mountain's dim silhouette. Upon the white ground, the golden body of an arhat manifests; a form robed in white approaches—jade bones of a beauty, a cat dozing by the chessboard at her side. New dew to wash the hands, ancient snow to cleanse the heart. The bright moon comes of its own accord; the melodies of the bright spring find few to echo them. My seven-character verses are ashamed before the jade and pearls that preceded them; these four vulgar stanzas are abashed that they cannot repay the fine jewels they have received. I write this in haste to acknowledge you; please regard it with a smile."`,`"To Jiantan and Yong'an, both brothers, for their joint perusal. Ziyu, with clasped hands. Attached are four rough compositions of mine, along with one seven-character quatrain and one five-character regulated poem; please kindly correct them."`,`Zhongqing and the others then looked at Ziyu's poem titles: Snow Feeling, Snow Shadow, Snow Sound, Snow Color. Zhongqing remarked to Wang Xun, "These four topics are entirely abstract—even harder to get a brush around than ours. Yuxiang is sure to have produced something fine." With that, they looked at the poems. They read:`,`Snow Feeling
Three thousand worlds gaze, brimming and full;
one knows there are jade flowers a-brewing, a-gathering.
Before becoming flower, it first cuts water into shapes;
already, cloud-like, it is about to take wing and fly.

Zhongqing said, "The opening couplet builds fine momentum before the topic; the second couplet carves out the essence of the word 'feeling'—truly a stroke of divine transformation." He read on:`,`People on earth await the planting of a flawless jade wall;
beyond the heavens a sleepless city is about to open wide.
Where is it now—the jade tower frozen solid?
The assembled immortals, imagining—rank on rank at Penglai.

Snow Shadow
Six-petalled, dimly drifting, the craft of embellishment complete;
beyond the jade balustrade, a delicate tracery is written.
Dim and low it mirrors the water, rippling with empty whiteness;
vaguely perched in the dust, it sways with soft redness.
Flying into plum blossoms—its trace grows faint;
dancing back among willow catkins—its color is all void.
In the clear cold it matches a dream of the Jade Pool;
the jade-tree branches glowing plainly in the moon.

Wang Xun applauded after every line. Zhongqing said, "This one has summoned the very soul of the topic." He read on:`,`Snow Sound
In the cold void, jade and jasper scatter;
entering the night, one burns incense to console the loneliness.
On the path the pellets gather, rustling first, collecting sleet;
at the window they are blown in a whisper, riding the gusting wind.
Threading through pine trees—in the stillness I sense pearls ricocheting apart;
sifting through bamboo—lightly, aptly, fine flakes drift.
Waiting until at dawn the clearing scene opens wide,
the dripping of the cold water-clock has nearly worn away its mark.

Snow Color
Who has been dazzled by that silver sea of jade radiance?
Alone at the summit of the Mountain of Assembled Jade he gazes outward.
The plantain leaf, without intent, somehow touched by green;
the pear-blossom cloud, lost in a dream, ends up piled in yellow.
Richly the pearly dew floats—three parts brilliant;
faintly it borrows from ice-plum—one thread of fragrance.
Dazzling the eyes, this pellucid emptiness is hard to examine closely;
white sands and pale moon—both fade into endless haze.

When they had finished reading, Zhongqing struck the table in admiration, and he and Wang Xun chanted the poems aloud several times. Zhongqing said:`,`"These poems surpass all of ours." They then looked at Pincai's quatrain. Wang Xun said, "This one is also quite good, but what made Yuxiang compose this extra one on his own?" Zhongqing replied, "This one is passable, but it is certainly not Yuxiang's work." They then looked at Yuanmao's five-character regulated poem. The opening couplet read:
In the sky, vermilion clouds spread;
my thoughts come—rain and snow fill to overflowing.
Wang Xun asked, "What do those two words 'my thoughts come' mean?" Zhongqing suddenly burst into laughter. "Read on." Wang Xun looked at the second couplet:
White people, both eyes draw near;
long horses, four hooves grow light.
He pondered, "'Horse hooves grow light'—I suppose this uses the image of light horse hooves when snow has thawed.`,`But why add the character for 'long'? And the first line is so profoundly obscure I simply cannot decipher it."`,`He looked at the third couplet:
Burrowing mayflies resemble—
digging out hollow lions, made complete.
Wang Xun said, "These two lines are very strange indeed. How do they come into play? The first line probably uses the Classic of Poetry—since there is the character for snow in 'linen garments white as snow,' the 'burrowing mayfly' passage gets dragged in. And this 'digging out hollow lions'—what allusion is buried there?" Zhongqing explained, "It is simply saying that the snow-sculpted lion has been hollowed out, that is all." They read the closing couplet:
Going out, one presents world-treasures;
six auspicious signs—the great steps are made smooth.
Wang Xun said, "Is such flattery really called for? This poem must be that old Wei's work. Seeing those slick and pat turns of phrase, naturally this sort of joke would come out." Zhongqing disagreed. "Not so. I look at old Wei and, though he is no conventional scholar, he seems a clever man; he would not be this inept. I suspect the seven-character quatrain is his, and this one must be the fine composition of that Young Brother Li—a poem very like the man." Wang Xun said, "Young Brother Li should not have produced this. Looking at him, so proper and literary, he still seems to have some scholarly spirit about him." Zhongqing replied, "Precisely because he has scholarly spirit, he lacks poetic spirit." Wang Xun asked, "Yuxiang asks us to comment on them. Shall we or not?" Zhongqing said, "You might as well give them a comment." Wang Xun said, "Why should I make enemies?" Zhongqing said, "Let me begin by marking Pincai's quatrain with a full circle." He wrote a critical note: Captures the spirit of Heaven's sport with jade.`,`For the first two couplets of Yuanmao's poem he drew single circles; for the last four lines he drew full circles. His critical note read: Couplets are polished and well-matched; classical allusions are dissolved into the new composition; fully worthy of carrying on the tradition of Yuan and Wei. Wang Xun used a needle to pin Ziyu's poems inside a translucent gauze cabinet, then read Zhongqing's comments and laughed. "The comments are well done, only slightly too barbed." Zhongqing said, "What a pity the day is not long, and this snow will not hold. Otherwise we could go and chat with Yuxiang." Wang Xun said, "Let us go tomorrow! If we went now there would be no time to talk properly." That day it snowed on for another full day and night, accumulating to about a foot's depth. The next morning it cleared. One blast of the north wind and the whole world was frozen solid as a jade box, dazzling and bright. After breakfast, Zhongqing and Wang Xun rode together in one carriage, their two attendants on horseback, and came to call on Ziyu. Halfway there, they met another carriage coming toward them. Both parties' attendants dismounted.`,`Wang Xun looked and saw it was Sun Sizhui. The two carriages drew up face to face. Wang Xun asked, "Where are you headed?" Sizhui replied, "Owing to the fact that my father and his wife have been at cross purposes—consuming and obliterating, with blood nearly flowing in rivers—a physician surnamed Wang has been treating man with man's own means to eradicate the old taint; looking askance at the situation, he says there is no injury. Now that the illness is somewhat better, one cannot but soothe and bring them around." Wang Xun smiled and said, "I will come by when I return." Sizhui agreed and hurried away. Zhongqing remarked, "This gentleman deploys his literary style in every conceivable context—truly absurd and laughable. This 'worm-eaten Thousand Character Classic' style is fit to be his name in life and his posthumous title in death; I doubt there is a second person in the world who resembles him in this." Wang Xun laughed, "As I see it, I fear that when it comes time for him to perform his conjugal duties, he will still have to compose a couplet or two for the occasion."`,'"One does feel sorry for that sister-in-law of ours. Though not especially beautiful, she is dignified and chaste, silent and unsmiling. Having married this sort of man, truly she will harbor regret for the rest of her life." Zhongqing laughed, "Perhaps he has one virtue that makes up for it; one never knows." Talking and laughing all the way, they arrived at the Mei residence.',`The gatekeeper announced them. Ziyu came out to welcome them inside, saying, "The two brothers have written excellent poems; my admiration is boundless. My own rough scribbles are inevitably a case of a minor shaman meeting a greater one." Zhongqing replied, "What my brother and the others have done—how can it compare with your fine compositions, which repay repeated murmured recitation? Refined and fitting in every respect." Wang Xun added, "What I love most are these two poems, Snow Feeling and Snow Color. Fresh and vigorous, combining the virtues of Geng Jing and Bao Zhao at once." Ziyu said, "Jiantan's four poems breathe ice and snow throughout, with pearls and jade in hand. The opening lines of Snow Mountain and Snow Pagoda arrive with the force of open sky—especially transcendent. As for Jiantan's finest individual couplets, 'but those mouse-like things only bask in others' warmth,' and 'in the deep of night, pondering—it has become flowing water,' the depth of feeling, the care of expression, a single-minded ardor going forward: as Dongpo put it, one who does not eat the food of this human world. Surely a work destined to endure." Zhongqing smiled, "One borrows from the scene to give expression to feeling on a whim; how lightly you say the word 'endure.'" Wang Xun asked, "That seven-character quatrain and that five-character regulated poem—whose hand are they?" Ziyu smiled. "Have you not tried to guess?" Wang Xun then reported what had been said the day before. Ziyu said, "Jiantan's eye is as sharp as ever. Have you added your comments?" Wang Xun took the poems from his sleeve. Ziyu read the comment on the five-character regulated poem but could not make out what the allusion meant. Who were Yuan and Wei?`,`Wang Xun then also told him about the Sun brothers. Ziyu laughed, and had someone summon Pincai and Yuanmao to come out. Everyone met. Ziyu returned each person's poems and said, "These assessments were made by Elder Brother Yan; he was most complimentary." Pincai read the comment and thought to himself: "This Yan Zhongqing is truly versed in everything ancient and modern; even the opera tunes I borrowed were spotted by him." He then went and thanked Zhongqing. Zhongqing said, "Brother Wei's poetic style is quite fine; both sound and rhythm are excellent. You must compose frequently, as though you have an aria perpetually on your lips." Pincai replied, "I have no foundation to speak of, and have neglected it for these several years. What can I call a poem at this point? So long as the tonal pattern is not violated, that is all." Ziyu said to Zhongqing, "Brother Pincai's poem is not too far off the mark." Zhongqing nodded. Yuanmao, meanwhile, pressed the circled couplets and the critical note up close to his face, reading them over and over again for quite some time. Then he put the verse sheet on the tea table, folded it twice with both hands, undid the buttons of his fur coat, and tucked it securely inside his lapel. Wang Xun noted, "Elder Brother Li, you are carrying a great deal more, aren't you." Li Yuanmao, thinking he was remarking on the moth-holes in his fur coat, answered without thinking, "The moth-holes are still manageable. Coming by the water route, it was shut away in the hold of the boat and absorbed the moisture; got a bit moth-eaten as a result. I have been wearing it through this one winter; next year I will have to have it seen to." Everyone heard this and had no idea what he was talking about. Pincai, realizing he had misheard, corrected him, "Elder Brother Wang is saying you have written a great many poems, not referring to your fur coat." Everyone then understood. Seeing Yuanmao's face flush deep red, unable to say a word, they all had to suppress their laughter. Zhongqing asked, "In your composition, 'long horses' and 'white people'—I imagine these use Mencius. But the three characters 'both eyes draw near'—is there a source for this?" Yuanmao gave Zhongqing two long looks and said, "I never rely on sources.`,`"I have seen in the poems of the ancients that they sometimes write themselves into the poem. It was that intention." Wang Xun suddenly understood. After more idle conversation, Zhongqing and Wang Xun said their farewells. Ziyu and the others escorted them to the door. Zhongqing suggested, "Why not come out with us to see the snow scenery?" Yuanmao, hearing this, was quite eager to go. Ziyu said, "The teacher has not fully recovered today. We must stay home to attend to him. We will accompany you another time." Yuanmao stuck out his lip but said nothing. Zhongqing and the others bid farewell and departed. Ziyu saw them out the front gate, came back inside, and chatted with Pincai and Yuanmao for a while about the poems, then suddenly again brought up Qinguan. Seeing that Pincai had developed a certain intent, he quietly threw out a hint: "Another day, why not steal a moment to come with me and make Qinguan's acquaintance?" Yuanmao said, "Let us go tomorrow. I shall just say I am going to see friends who came down the same route." He pointed at Ziyu. "And you say you are going to the Wang family to return a call. Once we are out of these two confining doors, what is there to fear?" Ziyu laughed, "In a few days' time." But let us leave this side of the story for now.`,`To return to Zhongqing and Wang Xun: they walked out of South Small Street to the Low Hollow to take in the view. All was a vast expanse of white; fields and paths were indistinguishable. Far off they could see Xu Ziyun's Garden of Ease, with its jadeite trees rising in uneven lines, mists and mountain vapors swirling and gathering, layer upon layer of gleaming multi-storied buildings, and the faint outline of high pavilions touching the clouds. They gazed for a while, then saw a carriage coming toward them from ahead. The person perched on the running-board spotted them first and immediately jumped down; then a young performer stepped down from the carriage as well. Zhongqing and the others quickly urged him not to bother. The young performer then leaned out of the carriage and settled himself—carved and powdered like jade, gilded and ornamented—it was none other than Lin Chunxi, the youngest of the performers listed in the Selection of Flowers. Wang Xun asked, "Where are you coming from?" Chunxi replied, "I am coming back from the Garden of Ease. Are you going there too?" Zhongqing said, "We are just looking at the snow scenery, and will be turning back soon." Wang Xun suggested, "Why do we not stop at that restaurant on Small Street and sit a while? One can also take in the distant scenery from there." Chunxi said, "If you are in the mood, I will be happy to accompany you." Zhongqing said, "Excellent." They turned their carriages about. Arriving at Small Street, they found a restaurant with two facing balconies, east and west. Zhongqing and the others went up to the east balcony. Though the day was cold, there was no wind up there.`,'Zhongqing had the windows thrown open, and the view extended a great distance. They ordered food and chatted idly for a while. Chunxi said, "This month the eight of us have been holding gatherings at the Garden of Ease every three days—a Winter Solstice club. Today is the fifth meeting. At each meeting there must be one entertainment, sometimes a drinking game, sometimes a parlor game. Today Duxiang wanted us to write poems. He set the topic Ice Bed, with each person writing one seven-character regulated poem, and Su Meixiang was judged first." Zhongqing asked, "Do you remember his poem?" Chunxi said, "I only remember the middle four lines." He then recited:',`Oars and punts have quite become a bed most stable;
storms and waves are no different from a level road.
Who says the Blue Sea is hard to fill to fullness?
I refuse to believe that Penglai's path cannot be passed.
"Everyone said his use of figures was wonderfully inspired, without a single flat line, which is why he surpassed the others." Wang Xun asked, "And yours?" Chunxi said, "Mine was not good; I cannot remember it." Zhongqing teased, "I imagine you came eighth." Chunxi laughed sheepishly, "You guessed it on the first try." Wang Xun reasoned, "No wonder; he is only fourteen years old. If he were to study for two years, do you think he could not catch up with them?" Chunxi said, "I was not going to compose at all, but they absolutely insisted I do. Today none of the poems were anything remarkable; it was only that Ruixiang and I got the tonal pattern wrong. As a result, Ruixiang was placed seventh and I was placed eighth. Never again shall I write such incompetent poetry. Let me study for a year and then come back and compete with them." Then he added, "Two new performers have come to our company. One is called Qinguan, one is called Qiguan. Have you met them?" Zhongqing said, "The other day Ruixiang mentioned the two of them, but just as he was starting to say something, someone interrupted and he never finished." Wang Xun asked, "What are these two like?" Chunxi said, "Excellent. That Qinguan is on a par with Yaoqing. That Qiguan—it is hard to say who ranks higher, him or Ruixiang. Neither has appeared on stage yet, but every day there are already three to five requests for them. The other day Duxiang saw them and praised them tremendously, immediately giving them fine gifts and having several complete new sets of costumes made for them.`,`"These two are going to make a great name for themselves. Only Qinguan's temperament is a bit cool; he is not very easy to talk to." Just as they were deep in conversation here, suddenly the window on the opposite west balcony opened with a sound. Zhongqing and the others looked up to see a beautiful young man, splendidly dressed. He wore a white heron-frost fur cloak and a violet sable cap on his head. With a face like polished jade and lips red as if painted, his gaze was bright with something of a cloud-piercing spirit, his bearing most refined, his manner extraordinary. To judge by his age, he appeared to be a little over twenty. He had four young performers in his company, and was leaning against the balcony window gazing out. Zhongqing and Wang Xun were privately astonished. Looking at his appearance and bearing, he was fully a match for Yuxiang—truly a phoenix among men. From his accent, he seemed to be from Jiangning, yet there was also something of a Yangzhou intonation mixed in. Looking at the four young performers, they were none of them celebrated names or proven talent, no more than common blossoms of the flower world. Wang Xun recognized one as Rongguan; the other three he did not know, and so asked Chunxi. Chunxi explained, "The one in tinted sable is Yu Mei; the one in Japanese-sword-patterned fur is Si Xi; the one in otter is Quanfu. All three are from the Jianchun company." Just then, the young man glanced over at this balcony once, then turned his back and sat down again. They could hear the young performers on that side chattering like orioles and swallows, with cups and counters flying about—quite lively. By comparison, the three on this side felt rather like the cold and lean poets of the Jiao and Jia school. They heard the beautiful young man say, "I hear people say that of the theater companies, Lianjin and Lianzhu are the finest. But listening to those two companies, all the performers are old; not a single fine-looking young dan in the Kunqu roles. What circulates through the gardens is nothing but defeated soldiers and broken remnants. I truly cannot understand why people say they are good."`,`Rongguan replied, "Our two Lian companies mostly do private banquet performances; the few good performers who sing Kunqu are always at banquets and rarely come to the public gardens. A refined person such as yourself—how is it that you do not appreciate Kunqu and instead prefer the clapper operas?" The young man laughed. "I pay attention to people, not to opera. I would rather have a vulgar opera with refined people than a refined opera with vulgar people." They also heard Yu Mei add, "It is all opera-singing; why distinguish between Kunqu and clapper style? Even granting that Kunqu has finer verse, it was still composed by the ancients and not by yourselves. The clapper style is merely rougher; in terms of conveying feeling and spirit it is fundamentally the same. What is most laughable is those people who talk only of Kunqu and do not like erhuang. In your two Lian companies, once those few people leave the company and stop performing, there will be no one left who can hold things together, and audiences will likely dwindle. The company might even disband." Si Xi said, "As I see it, it is all the same. Erhuang is opera, Kunqu is opera. Learn whichever you learn and sing whichever you sing." Rongguan laughed, "Enough, let us not talk about opera. Let us drink." They could then be heard playing finger-guessing games and singing drinking songs for a while. Then the young man spoke again: "When I listen to opera I do not listen to the words; I listen entirely to the tonal melody. It is not that I am unaware of Kunqu's moral dignity and musical elegance, but listening to it is like reading Song dynasty poetry: the rhythms are gentle and peaceful, yet the feeling lacks excitement and urgency. When I listen to the sounds of the zheng zither, pipa lute, and string instruments—with their dense and rapid notes and rhythms—there is a fullness and richness of feeling left over, capable of stirring a man to ardor and exaltation, to a vigor of all four limbs and an outpouring of all seven emotions. Even the clapper style, though it is certainly not orthodox music, has something of a quality of rises and falls, pauses and turns—something that moves one to contemplation, to dwell on the present and recall the past, like Ma Zhou passing through Xinfeng or Wei Su crossing the river south. With a chest full of sorrow and lament, feeling tangled and lingering, it is able to stir the deepest emotions of the lone traveler and the wandering scholar. People say that the sound of the huqin fiddle is the most licentious. But when I hear it I am overcome by sorrow, and it often moves me to tears. Just as Dongpo in his Ode on the Cave Flute said: 'like weeping, like complaining, like sobbing, like beseeching'—like the grief of a banished official ten thousand li from home, like the weeping of a widow in a lone boat on the river, each note entering the very depths of the heart. I cannot understand why people call it a licentious sound. Or is it that my ears differ from other ears, that my feeling is out of accord with ordinary feeling? As for the sounds of plucked strings and drums—I hear them and my heart and breath grow calm; there is nothing that moves me. That is how it is when I listen. I wonder whether others find it the same?" The four young performers could none of them answer.`,`Zhongqing said quietly to Wang Xun, "This man's views are partial, yet he has his own private understanding. His unwillingness to follow the common herd is already evident. Moreover his chest must hold much accumulated resentment, which is why he dislikes the peaceful and prefers the stirring. String music is inherently sorrowful; his saying that the huqin is not licentious—now that is indeed a view that breaks from convention, one that no one has ever perceived before. I look at this man and he seems precisely our sort, certainly not a mediocre person. Someday I would like to seek him out." Wang Xun agreed, "From what he has said and his manner and bearing, one can already form a general impression." Just then the young man asked one of the restaurant's people for brush and inkstone, and on the plastered wall he wrote a few lines; then, with his four young performers, he descended the stairs and left. Zhongqing and the others also stopped drinking, told the attendants to settle the bill, took Chunxi with them, and came over to the west balcony. There, in flowing strokes of dense ink, in a fine bold cursive hand, he had written a ci poem to the tune Langtaosha:
The red sun has already sloped to the west—
I smile, watching cloud and rosy haze.
The jade dragon's scales have scattered and spread across all the sky.
I yearn for the spring wind to come ten thousand li
and blow away every jade flower.
Do not boast of worldly things—
no thought of them is without error.
The immortal of Penglai in *Waves Scouring Sand* reaches down for the cloud carriage.
Drunk, I ask the guest dwelling in the Great Luo Heaven above—
the colored phoenix—whose home does it grace?
Zhongqing and Wang Xun read it and both nodded in admiration. Chunxi said, "This ci poem seems written by an immortal; there is a transcendent, unearthly quality to it." Zhongqing remarked, "This man is one of pure and unconstrained spirit, free and unrestrained. Yet the ones he appreciates are that company of young performers. Truly baffling." They then looked at the signature: "Xiangfan—written drunk." They could not tell his family name or given name, so they called the restaurant keeper and asked whether he recognized the man. The keeper answered, "This lord came here for the first time today. When they settled the bill, his second attendant paid in cash, and we never asked his name or address." Zhongqing said, "This ci poem is very fine, the brush of a man of exceptional talent. It illuminates your humble establishment; by all means preserve it and do not scrub it off." The keeper agreed and went off. Chunxi said, "As for this man's background, Rongguan ought to know. When I see him I will ask, and then we will know what manner of person this is." The three then talked a while more, descended the stairs, and each went their separate ways. As to what happened next, listen to the following chapter for the explanation.`]},m=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations4:i},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o={5:["The previous chapter told of Lin Chunxi and Zhongqing discussing the poetry-gathering at the Yiyuan garden. Now let us set the stage for that garden. There was a prince among young noblemen, a leader of literary men, by the name of Xu Ziyun, courtesy name Duxiang, a native of Shanyin County in Zhejiang. Speaking of his family lineage, it was truly among the foremost of the age, boasting seven generations of official distinction, with grandfathers and grandsons serving as prime ministers, fathers and sons as Ministers of State, and brothers as governors-general. Of Xu Ziyun's own line, his father, named Zhen, rose through the Hanlin Academy and now served as Grand Secretary and Governor-General of Guangdong. His elder brother, Zirong, also a Hanlin graduate, had served as a censor and been appointed Inspector of the Huai-Yang Circuit.","His mother had gone to Guangdong to accompany his father's posting; only Ziyun himself remained in the capital. This Ziyun was cultivated and elegant in appearance, distinguished and peerless in character, magnanimous beyond measure, and deeply versed in the classics and histories. He was twenty-five years of age, holding a sinecure post as a departmental secretary through hereditary privilege, and at twenty-two had also passed the provincial examinations as a juren. His wife, née Yuan, was twenty-three years old, daughter of Yuan Hao, the current Governor of Yunnan. She was surpassingly beautiful, supremely virtuous, and possessed literary gifts of the orchid and cassia. She and Ziyun were perfectly matched, a talented man and a beauty, loving and mutually respectful, as lute paired with jade se. They already had one son and one daughter.","Ziyun lived amid luxury and splendor. He had no trace of dissolution or extravagance. He disliked the constraints of official dress and decorum. He preferred the pleasures of hills and valleys as his enjoyment. Though still in his twenties, he already possessed the refined taste for music of Xie An of Dongshan, and the pleasures of lute and wine-cups of Kong Rong of Beihai. In front of his residence lay a large open plot of land, five or six li in circumference, with naturally rolling hills, natural hollows, ancient trees, and gnarled pines. It had originally been a ruined garden belonging to some former family.","Ziyun purchased this plot and expanded it, buying up the nearby residences at generous prices.","He had a close friend from Xiangtan County in southern Hunan, a man named Xiao Cixian, courtesy name Jingyi. Thirty-two years of age, he was a renowned man of letters who had come to the capital to sit for examinations as a distinguished tribute student. Yet he had no desire for trifling titles and no ambition to advance. In astronomy and geography, in the writings of all the philosophers and the hundred schools, there was nothing he had not mastered. He and Ziyun had sworn brotherhood, and over the course of three or four years spent enormous effort supervising the construction of the Yiyuan garden. The result was a place of driving clouds and shifting mountains, a sweep of soaring towers and layered pavilions, secluded recesses and winding depths. In the magnificence of its flowers, plants, and scenic walks it surpassed all others in the capital, comprising twenty-four scenic areas with over four hundred rooms. Great hills linked together, streams wound in curving courses; its wonders were beyond description. Ziyun's reputation and connections were vast, and scholars from all directions gathered like stars and massing clouds.","In character, Ziyun was noble and sincere, responsive to every request and sparing no effort. He cherished the choosing of friends, not by power or status, but by virtue and principle. Though every day his seats were full of guests and wine cups never empty, only a few true kindred spirits were his daily companions; the rest were merely received with proper courtesy, their wishes accommodated as best as possible. The eight famous dan performers from Shi Nanxiang's Selection of Flowers came and went daily. Ziyun treasured them all, but singled out Yuan Baozhu for particular affection. His feeling of love and appreciation for beautiful performers was quite different from others. He regarded these fine young opera players as he would rare treasures, fine birds, and celebrated flowers. He held only a caring and admiring heart, with no thought of vulgar intimacy. And so these famous performers all forgot the distances of social rank with him, treating him as a kind father or caring mother, able to speak openly of anything, free to follow their hearts. Xiao Cixian was even more pure and detached, taking no pleasure in worldly amusements.","Thus these two men not only sharpened each other in virtue and letters, but in temperament and spirit had no barriers between them whatsoever.","One day, at a theatrical gathering, Ziyun encountered two newly arrived performers, Qin Guan and Qi Guan, and was greatly impressed. He marveled that such talent was unprecedented, seeing that they were perfectly suited to stand alongside the eight famous dan performers. This kindled his desire to cultivate their acquaintance. He asked Yuan Baozhu to bring them to the garden another day. Seeing that their clothing was not yet fine enough, he immediately had several new outfits made overnight and presented them as gifts. The two performers were naturally grateful. But this Qin Guan was different from ordinary people. Let us first briefly describe his background.","Qin Guan's surname was Du. His father was known as Du Qinshi, Master Du the Qin-player, who made his living by crafting and performing on the qin zither. The sons of gentry throughout Jiangsu vied to invite him to give lessons, and so everyone called him Du Qinshi. When this son was born, he was named after the qin, and called Qin Guan.","Qin Guan bore auspicious markings on his palm and was clever from earliest childhood; his parents loved him as their most precious treasure. When he reached ten years of age, Du Qinshi was suddenly humiliated and beaten by a powerful lord, smashed his qin in rage and grief, and died. His mother, a year later, also fell ill with sorrow and died. Left without support, the orphaned Qin Guan was taken in by a clan uncle. When he was thirteen, this uncle too died. His widowed aunt could not maintain her chastity and remarried, so she sold Qin Guan into a theater troupe. Just then Ye Maolin happened to see him and bought him out of the troupe, taking him along to the capital. Qin Guan had begun to learn characters at six, was exceptionally gifted, and could memorize anything upon a single reading. By thirteen he had read many books, including poetry collections, ci-lyrics, miscellaneous essays, tales and popular fiction, and understood them all clearly. His heart aspired to lofty things and his nature was one of great purity; he possessed the ambition of a mountain pheasant dancing before a mirror, a vermilion phoenix perching on its proper plane-tree. When he was forced into the theater troupe, he had tried several times to hang himself, but failed each time to die, and so he used the troupe's disdain for him as a means to preserve his integrity. When Ye Maolin brought him to the capital and treated him with fresh care and regard as a rare treasure, who would not have found peace? Yet he had another worry: having climbed out of the bottom of a well, he feared he had only fallen into the bottom of the sea. He worried that the coral net would be hard to find, that bright pearls might be wasted in darkness, that his jade, like the fabled gem of Bian He, might go forever unrecognized, and that drawing his sword might only bring suspicion. So he was always melancholy. The night before entering the capital, he had a dream: he dreamed of a place where ten thousand plum trees bloomed, their fragrant snow filling the air like a sea. He was strolling in delight when suddenly his body sank into a pit.","The earth was about to reach the top of his head. He was in desperate peril. Suddenly a beautiful youth appeared, his face as luminous as jade and divine. With one hand he lifted Qin Guan out. Qin Guan was overwhelmed with gratitude and was about to bow in thanks. The young man turned gracefully, walked into the plum grove, and vanished. Qin Guan went in to search and found a large plum fruit growing on a plum tree; looking closely, he saw it was made of jade. Then he awoke. The next day upon entering the city, jostled in a traffic jam on the road, he saw Ziyu, who was the very person who had saved him in the dream. He was greatly astonished and could only stare at him dazedly for a while. But they were strangers on the road; he did not know the young man's name or address, and had no way to make inquiries. Whenever he attended theatrical gatherings or visited the theaters, he looked everywhere, but did not see him again. When he later met Xu Ziyun, who greatly appreciated him and gave him many clothes and objects, he became uncertain and wary. Knowing Ziyun was a nobleman of great wealth and rank, he feared such a man must carry the haughty airs of the powerful, and was very reluctant to approach him. But unable to escape his master's command, he had no choice but to go and pay his respects.","That day, Qi Guan had caught a cold and could not get up, so Yuan Baozhu came first to Qin Guan's lodgings. Baozhu's appearance had already been described in the Selection of Flowers: his nature gentle and yielding, his looks like a maiden's. He too was attracted to Qin Guan, whose appearance resembled his own. Though they had just met, it was as if they were long-standing friends. After several heart-to-heart conversations, Qin Guan had also come to value Baozhu's character, seeing him as someone who kept his own purity and self-respect. Baozhu then described Ziyun's fine qualities in detail, and Qin Guan felt somewhat reassured. The two of them rode in a carriage together, Qin Guan in front, Baozhu behind. By happy coincidence, they reached the South Small Street intersection. They met Ziyu returning from Shi Nanxiang's, in a carriage with two horses, coming directly toward them. Ziyu had not drawn his curtain; Qin Guan had drawn his, but through the glass window he had already seen clearly.",`Unable to help himself, he raised the curtain, revealing a face of unparalleled beauty. Ziyu caught a glimpse and recognized the very Qin Guan he had encountered before, the one Pincai had told him about, the one he had been thinking of day and night. Joy spread across his face and he smiled. He glimpsed Qin Guan's clear bright eyes and slightly parted crimson lips. Then the curtain fell, and in an instant both carriages had sped away from each other like wind and lightning. Ziyu saw that the carriage today was finer and more beautiful than before, and thought admiringly to himself: "Truly, a night-luminous gem cannot be concealed, a bright moon shines of its own accord. Naturally he has met a true connoisseur. But I wonder what sort of person that connoisseur is." He also thought: "Pincai said his temper is peculiar and he is extremely proud. He must be one who chooses whom to follow, and would certainly never drift with the current to seek a momentary meeting." On Qin Guan's side, he was thinking: "This young nobleman, his elegance is in his bones, his beauty in his spirit; his warmth and gentleness overflow naturally. He must be a righteous man of genuine feeling, with not a trace of selfish or improper intent. In my dream he lifted me from the mud, surely in the future he is the one meant to raise me up. Otherwise, why would I meet him so soon after dreaming of him? But in the dream, I saw him walk beneath the plum trees and disappear, and then I saw a jade plum. What does that signify?" He kept turning this over in his mind, lost in reverie.`,"Not long after, they entered the Yiyuan garden. Baozhu inquired and learned that Ziyun was that day at the Begonia Spring Court. This Begonia Spring Court had open terraces and winding covered galleries, intimate chambers and secluded rooms, all connecting in a continuous chain of over twenty bays. Baozhu led them inside to a suite of three rooms, where Ziyun and Cixian were sitting around a charcoal brazier drinking together. Seeing the two arrivals, both welcomed them with warm, smiling faces.",`Qin Guan timidly went forward, made two bows of greeting, expressed his thanks, and stood with bowed head. Ziyun and Cixian saw that today, in fine and colorful dress, his appearance was even more beautiful than before. But they saw his tentative, bashful, nervous bearing. Feelings of tender regard arose in them unbidden, and they invited him to sit. Qin Guan sat close beside Baozhu. Ziyun smiled warmly and said: "When we first met the other day, we could not speak at length. Please tell me in detail about your background, your family, and how you came to enter the theater troupe." When asked about his background, Qin Guan's accumulated sorrow was stirred; without realizing it, his face flushed rose-red and his eyes brimmed with pearly tears. He steadied himself, but could not refuse to answer, so adopting the Mandarin pronunciation and setting aside his Suzhou accent, he recounted his family history. When he came to his parents' deaths, his uncle's care, his uncle's death, and his aunt's remarriage and abandonment of him, his voice became like a gentle breeze playing a flute, softly lamenting. Ziyun and Cixian were quite moved and earnestly consoled him.`,"They then asked what operas he had studied, which pieces he knew, and Qin Guan answered them. Cixian said:",'"In what way does he resemble a performer at all? What a pity that such spiritual luminosity as heaven and earth has produced, instead of being bestowed upon a fragrant boudoir, is given to the singing stage. Wearing a cap instead of hairpins, shoes instead of skirts. It is truly a lamentable matter." Ziyun said: "He and Yaoqing truly could be called a matched pair soaring under auspicious clouds, racing a thousand li abreast. If their hats and shoes were exchanged for hairpins and skirts, I fear even the Two Beauties east of the River could scarcely be compared with them. It must be that the Creator intends to allow a few such extraordinary figures to emerge from this world, washing away all the commonplace and vulgar habits of former times, who can say for certain?" He then said to Qin Guan: "Here is different from other places, you need not feel like a stranger. Follow Yaoqing in everything; whatever he does, you do likewise. If you wish to know what we are like, simply ask him in detail.',`"Here Yaoqing is not treated as an ordinary performer. All forms of address are different from the outside world: everyone addresses each other by courtesy names, and formal salutations are not required. If you are inclined, please come here often in your free time. There is no need to stand on ceremony, for ceremony creates distance." Qin Guan could only agree. He looked at the two men again. Both were of extraordinary bearing, serene and approachable, and he knew they were not ordinary men. Cixian said to Ziyun: "What you have said is exactly right. At this moment he still does not know what our temperament is like; he may suppose that wealth and rank must bring with them proud and extravagant airs, not knowing that we most dislike such things. As for your natural gifts, nothing need be said. But a person's elegant demeanor and refined bearing all come from books; without having read and understood the principles of learning, one's speech and bearing will inevitably fall short of true refinement. Have you studied any books?" Before Qin Guan could answer, Baozhu laughed and said:`,`"His mind is far stronger than ours! If we were tested right now, I'm afraid even Meixiang could not surpass him." Ziyun was even more pleased and asked Qin Guan: "Tell me honestly, have you studied books?" Qin Guan replied: "I have studied for five or six years." Cixian asked: "What have you read?" Qin Guan replied: "Besides the Four Books, I read through the Rhapsodies on Categorized Affairs, a rhapsody on classes of things, and two volumes of Tang poetry." Ziyun said: "That is quite enough. Can you compose poetry?" Qin Guan said: "I cannot." Baozhu said: "That is only because he has never been taught; once he begins studying he will certainly be able to do it at once. The other day he told me about the operas, which pieces are fine, which are not, and he explained every point without error. With such innate gifts, how could he fail to learn?" Qin Guan lowered his head and said nothing. Ziyun said:`,`"His name is not suitable. Jingyi, please give him a new character, let us change this word 'guan'. And give him a courtesy name as well." Cixian thought for a moment and said: "What about changing it to Qin Yan, meaning Lute-Words, with the courtesy name Yunong, meaning Jade Youth. Would that do?" Ziyun said: "Very good. The two characters 'Qin Yan' are both fresh and elegant; and 'Yunong' as a courtesy name suits the person perfectly." Baozhu told Qin Guan to express his thanks; Qin Guan rose and made two bows. Cixian said: "We have already said all this, why are you bowing again?" Ziyun said: "Let us establish a rule: on festival days and occasions for congratulations you may bow; in all other ordinary meetings it is entirely dispensed with. The word 'Master' is forever forbidden from your lips. Address me simply as Duxiang, and him as Jingyi." Qin Yan stood and said: "How could I dare?" Ziyun said: "If you insist on refusing, it is as if we were the same as common men, that would not be honoring us but distancing yourself from us. Besides, what weight does 'Master' carry? Everyone outside, without exception, is called 'Master.' When you address others, naturally you must do the same; but here, such forms are unnecessary." Qin Guan still dared not agree, and Baozhu laughed: "Since Duxiang has already given his word, just call him Duxiang." Qin Yan saw that Baozhu called him by his courtesy name directly. Since this was still really their first meeting, he himself felt somewhat awkward, and so he gave a small smile. Ziyun, seeing this smile, lips like cherries, teeth like strung pearls, beauty blooming on fragrant cheeks, brightness rippling in clear eyes, felt dazzled and stirred to the depths of his heart, and knew a great blossoming of joy. He ordered a servant to set out wine, and the four of them sat together at the table. During the meal, Baozhu guided and instructed Qin Yan in all manner of things. Seeing that neither Xiao nor Xu showed any teasing or flirtatious words or intentions, that their speech was refined and their bearing proper, they were truly men one could approach and trust. Qin Yan gradually relaxed, his spirit settling and his breath becoming easy. Baozhu also went through several drinking games with him and told him about many famous scholars and men of letters of the day, and how he should conduct himself when he met them. Qin Yan listened to everything attentively. In his heart, he also thought again of the young nobleman he had glimpsed in the carriage, wondering whether Baozhu knew him, whether Duxiang was acquainted with him. He still did not know the man's name, and had no way to inquire. That day they lingered in the Yiyuan for half a day; after the wine was done, Ziyun and Cixian led him through a tour of the garden. The rooms and furnishings, antiques and curios were all things he had never seen in his life, and he took pleasure in looking at each one carefully. Ziyun gave him many more gifts and told him to come and ask for anything he desired. Qin Yan expressed his thanks and took his leave. From this point on, he came often to the Yiyuan together with Baozhu and the other famous performers; after a few visits he felt quite at home there. But let us set this aside for now.`,"Now let us return to Ziyu, who had again encountered Qin Guan that day and was in excellent spirits. Returning home, he immediately sought out Pincai to tell him what had happened. Pincai was somewhat pleased as well, and proceeded to describe to Ziyu the scene on the road. It turned out that when Pincai and Ye Maolin had been traveling together and reached Jining Prefecture, all the performers had gone ashore, but he found Qin Guan alone in the boat, in tears. He asked about his troubles, but Qin Guan would answer nothing. When Pincai suggested he might not want to go onstage for fear of disgracing his parents' memory, Qin Guan gave him a single glance. Pincai then tried to press further, not pausing to consider whether he was overstepping, and took out a handkerchief to wipe Qin Guan's tears. Just as he was about to do so, Qin Guan snatched it away with one hand and threw it into the river, then covered his face and wept. Pincai had resented him for this ever since. Now seeing Ziyu's delight, he carelessly let this episode slip out. Ziyu felt only deeper admiration and respect, admiring the chastity and reserve, the inviolable dignity. From respect grew love, from love grew longing; these two feelings turned like a waterwheel in his heart.","And so it is that among all the treasures in the world, true beauty is the foremost. If it is genuine beauty, there is no one in the world who does not love it. Ziyu's experience at the theater the other day had been such that Baozhu seemed to have made some impression on him; his heart ought to have been forever unmoved. Yet who would have known that after only two encounters with Qin Guan, it was like a flash of lightning or a spark of flint, passing in an instant without a trace, and yet his heart was now constantly thinking of him? How much more so for others, whose self-restraint was not as great as Ziyu's, and who could be with beautiful people day and night. Naturally their love and longing would be even more intense, transforming tempered steel into pliant gold thread. Pincai knew that he and Qin Guan had no karmic affinity and could get nowhere; though he admired Qin Guan's looks, he genuinely resented his temperament. Now seeing Ziyu's infatuation, viewing the inside situation as an outsider, he merely offered encouraging words and a flattering manner, but he himself was not particularly invested. Shortly after, Ziyu went out, and Pincai set the whole matter aside.","One day the weather turned clear and mild. The snow had melted, and Pincai thought of Fu Sanziye and wished to go into the city to visit him. He had his boy Sier hire a carriage and set off toward the Eastern City. Coming from the opposite direction was a crowd of horses and carriages rushing forward like the wind. First came a mounted outrider, then a pair of lead horses, then a carriage with its side-curtain hanging open. Pincai leaned out to look and caught, quick as a lightning flash, a glimpse of a beautiful young man with fine brows and bright eyes. His bearing was magnificent as a divinity, like the morning sun glorious among clouds and mist, like a crane in flight over the Isle of Penglai. He was clearly about twenty years of age.",`He wore an embroidered python-robe with sable cloak, a magnificent official hat, and court shoes. Behind him came twenty or thirty mounted attendants, all in freshly made uniforms of matching color. Then followed over ten cloth-curtained heated carriages, inside which sat some powder-and-jade-carved children, also resembling small dan performers. Behind these came four or five large carts laden with trunks, clothing bundles, tea stoves, wine boxes, portable kitchens, and other such articles. The drivers were all dressed in short silk jackets, brocade trousers, damask stockings, and satin shoes, striding along in magnificent, vigorous fashion. The procession passed for quite some time. Pincai thought: "What sort of person is this, with such a grand retinue?" Just then he heard his driver say: "Sir, do you know who this person is?" Pincai replied: "I have no idea. What person is this, so wealthy?" The driver said: "This is the magnificent young nobleman of Jinchun Garden. There is a saying in the capital that everyone repeats: 'In the city, one star; beyond the walls, one cloud. Two great young noblemen, more grand than all the world.' As for this young nobleman's family background, I don't know the full story. I only know his father is a duke and currently serves as Commander-General of the Western Frontier.`,`"His residence takes up three or four li in circumference. They have a Master of Stables called Master Lu, and I once heard him say they have a hundred-odd horses and seventy or eighty large mules. Would you say that's wealthy?" Pincai asked: "What is his surname?" The driver replied: "His surname is Hua, people all call him Young Master Hua." Pincai asked: "The men on horseback must be his household servants; but those children in the carriages, who look like opera performers, what are they?" The driver said:`,'"They are xiangong, opera boys."','"He keeps his own opera troupe at home. Whenever he is invited out to drink and watch a performance, he insists on bringing his own troupe to perform a couple of scenes. Even for performers outside, if one catches his eye, he spares no expense to buy them and take them home. I hear one of his personal attendants is also an opera boy, whom he bought last year for eight thousand taels of silver. Just think of that extravagance! Who could possibly match him?" Pincai said: "Truly magnificent. But do his parents just let him carry on like this without interfering?" The driver replied: "His father and mother are ten thousand li away! Besides, his family has boundless silver. So what if he spends a little more, what does it matter? Today he must be going out to a banquet, which is why he has his troupe with him." As they spoke, they entered the Eastern City and arrived at the Gold Pailou. Finding a large doorway opposite a tea shop, the carriage stopped. Pincai had Sier take in his calling card, waiting in the carriage himself. He saw two sealed notices on the wall: one for a former Vice-Minister of War, the other for the Jiangnan Bureau of the Ministry of Revenue. Someone in the gatehouse took the card and went inside; shortly after, he returned and said, "Please come in." Pincai stepped down and followed the gatekeeper inside. Passing through the second gate, they entered a courtyard leading to a covered passageway.',`Through the passageway was the main hall, flanked by six wing-rooms on either side. Fu San was already standing beneath the eaves of the main house, coming forward to welcome him. Pincai hurried up and clasped his hands in greeting. Fu San led him behind the main hall to sit in a pair of small study rooms, exchanging polite inquiries about each other's health. Pincai said: "The snow delayed me these past few days, otherwise I would have come to pay my respects the day before yesterday. I felt so restless at home, thinking of you constantly, Third Brother." Fu San replied: "The feeling is mutual." This place was Fu San's study, situated close to the inner chambers, separated only by a courtyard. Pincai briefly took in the room's furnishings: the space was divided by a floor-to-ceiling partition of nanmu wood carved with ice-crackle patterns. In the upper room was a wooden kang, adorned with a set of four small landscape screens, and a striking clock rested on the kang table. Nearby stood a square table with several chairs, and in the center sat a large copper coal-brazier. On the wall hung a silk-scroll couplet, and beside it an intricate Western painting. The kang cushions were of sapphire-blue satin. A servant entered, wearing a plain silk fur-lined jacket and a fur hat pulled down over his eyebrows, revealing half of a large braided queue at the back. Carrying a tea tray, he first handed a cup to Pincai. Pincai said: "Please convey my respects to the Madam." Before the servant could reply, Fu San said: "Your sister-in-law is not home today; she has gone to visit her parents. Stay and have a meal with me today, and we can chat." Pincai readily agreed, then asked: "Will Eldest Brother be coming today?" Fu San replied: "I'm not sure. I heard him say yesterday he had business and needed to go to Jinchun Garden to ask Young Master Hua to put in a good word. I imagine he has gone there by now." Hearing of Young Master Hua of Jinchun Garden, Pincai asked: "I was just going to ask about that Young Master Hua." He then recounted what he had seen on the road and what the driver had told him. Fu San said: "What does a driver know! This Young Master Hua's name is Guangsu, courtesy name Xingbei. His father is a hereditary first-class duke, currently serving as Commander-General of the Western Frontier. Because of the great merits of his ancestors, when he was eighteen and entered official service, he was immediately awarded the rank of a second-class idle grand minister. This year he is twenty-one, accomplished in horsemanship and archery, and quite capable in literary affairs as well. His intelligence goes without saying, even those young performers could not match it. The only fault is that he spends too lavishly. But in truth he is not arrogant or haughty at all; people who see his imposing retinue simply do not dare approach him. His family wealth is beyond reckoning. A few years back he married the daughter of Marquis Jingbian and Vice-Minister Su, whose dowry alone came to a million taels. His wife is truly beautiful as a heavenly immortal, her looks may be the finest in all the realm. She is supremely virtuous too, accomplished in calligraphy, painting, chess, and music. She also has ten maidservants called the Ten Pearl Maids, all bearing names with the character 'pearl', all as beautiful as flowers and jade, literate, and able to sing and play instruments. In his residence, Young Master Hua truly passes every day in delight. He is the foremost noble young man in the city, the supreme wealthy patron. I am distantly connected to him, for his wife's maternal great-uncle is a relation of my own sister-in-law.`,`"This year I hosted him for one meal and spent over a thousand strings of cash. He does not go to restaurants or theaters; when he comes to someone's home, this crowd of twenty or thirty horses and twenty or thirty people must be accommodated. If the house is small there is simply no room to fit them all. Besides, his tastes are demanding: he wants both quality and abundance. His own appetite may be limited, but when hosting him you must devise special preparations. You need thirty or forty excellent dishes, twenty or thirty varieties of fruit, and more than ten kinds of fine wine. When his spirits rise, one day is not sufficient; he stays until midnight. Naturally there must be a theatrical troupe, and he brings his own as well.`,`"Getting performers to serve as wine companions is also difficult. One moment he thinks of this one, the next moment that one, and you must have all the famous ones present in attendance. Even having performers is not enough: there must also be ensemble troupes, eight-beat drum performers, acrobats, and all manner of miscellaneous entertainments. All must be arranged beforehand to cater to his fancy. When pleased, his tips are several gold ingots. But if anything goes wrong, or someone's face displeases him, he rewards them all the same, and then has them flogged thirty or forty lashes and driven out. Can you imagine such standards? He does not care whether people find it tolerable or not, he simply follows his own whims. That day I was somewhat careless: I love the shibo xian variety performance, and there is a young performer named Xiaoshun'er who is the absolute master of that genre. I thought surely he would like him too. When Xiaoshun'er performed *Ten Non-Idle Acts* came out in full costume and walked on, Young Master Hua's face instantly turned furious. He let out a cold laugh, and his attendants at once drove Xiaoshun'er off, leaving me extremely mortified. After that he said not a word, gave not a smile. Barely ten dishes had been served when he grew eager to leave, and no amount of urging could detain him. I had to let him go. It was generous of him to save my face and not have the whip used on anyone. For that brief visit, counting the main courses, the middle courses, and the late courses, plus all the various tips and incidental costs, it came to over a thousand strings. Not only did I fail to please him, he went away declaring me vulgar and intolerable. Since then I have never dared invite him again. He has a personal attendant named Lin Shanji who genuinely cost eight thousand taels of silver." Pincai nodded with a slight smile and said: "Befriending this wealthy nobleman would indeed be no easy matter." Fu San said: "Difficult indeed, unless one possesses true abilities that earn his admiration. Otherwise, however assiduously one flatters and courts him, this is precisely the sort of person who most enjoys being flattered." Just as they were saying this, the meal arrived, a flask of wine, four side dishes, and a hot pot. Fu San said: "Today is quite simple, nothing worth eating." The two drank and chatted at ease. Then Pincai heard some women's voices from within, quite animated. After a moment it seemed as if two women had gotten into an argument, growing noisy and confused, mixed with the voices of maids and older maidservants trying to mediate, and scattered laughter.`,"Fu San tried to ignore it, but with Pincai present he felt embarrassed, and went inside.",`Pincai listened quietly and could make out Fu San's voice saying "There's a guest, there's a guest" once or twice. The women's voices dropped slightly, trailing off in sporadic murmuring. Fu San came back out and drank a cup of wine with Pincai; then the noise inside started up again.`,`Fu San could not sit still and ran back inside; this time the commotion was very loud. Even with Fu San there trying to suppress it, it only grew worse. They could also hear Fu San shouting: "Have some consideration for me, this is simply not done!" Then a woman's voice was heard, half-crying and half-shouting, her words too hurried and rapid to make out clearly from outside.`,'Pincai had no heart for drinking, and it would have been awkward to ask questions, so he simply asked for the meal to be served. Fu San came out again; Pincai could see that his composure was quite unsettled. He took his leave and expressed thanks for the meal. Fu San saw that Pincai had already eaten and the commotion inside was still so bad; he could hardly urge him to stay. He could only say: "Today things have been rather informal, please do not laugh at us. The moment my wife steps out of the house, the others lose all restraint and start quarreling." Pincai could say nothing in response, and went straight out. Fu San accompanied him to the main gate, watched until he had got into the carriage, then turned back inside.',"Pincai then called on Gui Daye's house; he was not at home, so Pincai left his card and departed. Riding in the carriage, Pincai reflected:",'"The other day at the theater, Rong Guan mentioned that the Green Concubine and the White Concubine had come to blows, throwing this, smashing that. I took it for a joke. Today it turns out to be quite true." The hour was still early to return home; he left the city, dismissed the carriage, strolled past the theater gates and various other places, then made his way home.',"Time passed quickly; after a few days, it was already the end of the year, and the Mei household was full of festive bustle.",`Master Li the tutor had finished teaching for the season and went out often, gathering with fellow provincials and examination classmates for conversation and amusement. On New Year's Eve, Pincai and Yuanmao sat idly in the study, feeling very much the melancholy of guests far from home. Presently Ziyu came out and said to both of them: "Yesterday I heard that Uncle Wang, on the day of the group-visit ceremony, will especially prepare two tables of wine to host us, and also the Sun brothers." Yuanmao said: "I will not go. I am not even from the same province." Ziyu said: "That does not matter. First, Uncle Wang has invited us specifically, and we will not be seated with the others; second, it is also Yong'an's wish. If you do not go, it will be awkward for everyone." Pincai smiled and said: "If that is the case, that day we should be able to watch Qin Guan perform in the opera!" Ziyu smiled and said: "There is still something else I must attend to." And he went back inside. That evening, when Li Xingquan returned home, entering the gate he already saw the whole hall illuminated with lanterns in brilliant splendor. Looking toward the main hall, he saw Scholar Mei with his wife and Ziyu, surrounded by a group of servants and their wives, offering sacrificial foods before the deity images.`,"He hurried to the study and found two pairs of red candles and four plain glass lanterns burning within. Yuanmao went forward to bow in greeting. Pincai also came to offer the New Year's salutation. Xingquan hastily returned the courtesies, then led them to pay their New Year's respects to their Teacher and Teacher's Wife. Scholar Mei stopped them from the full formality of bowing. Lady Yan immediately instructed Ziyu to go out and offer New Year's greetings to the Teacher. Scholar Mei then personally escorted Ziyu to the study; they exchanged congratulations, and wine and food were set out.","Scholar Mei with great reverence filled a cup of wine for Xingquan; Xingquan kept demurring that he could not accept such an honor. Mei then made to fill cups for Pincai and Yuanmao as well. Pincai quickly took the wine cup himself, set it in place, and they all took their seats in proper order.","Scholar Mei was a man of strict speech and upright conduct, and now paired with Li Xingquan, the conversation at the table was nothing but discourse on self-cultivation and moral rectitude, all words of exhortation to Ziyu. Li Yuanmao sat stiff and constrained, hardly daring to eat, in considerable discomfort. Only Pincai managed to pretend at being earnest, affecting pedantic speech to converse with them. After not too long, the banquet dissolved. Scholar Mei sat outside a while longer, speaking at length on various matters, then went inside together with Ziyu. Xingquan, Yuanmao, and the rest also retired for the night. But for what follows, please await the next chapter."]},y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations5:o},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),s={6:["As one year inevitably yields to the next, the passing of New Year's Eve heralds the first day of the new year, naturally inviting its own measure of celebration. After two days of bustling festivities, on the third day, an invitation notice and three sets of calling cards arrived from the household of Wang Wenhui. The notice detailed an assembly of twelve guests: Vice-Minister of Revenue Liu; Grand Academician Wu of the Imperial Cabinet; Hanlin Reader-in-Waiting Mei; Director of the Eastern Palace Zhuang; Left Senior Secretary Zheng; Tongzheng Commissioner Wang; Deputy Director of the Court of Imperial Entertainments Zhou; Imperial College Director Zhang; Censor Shi; Supervising Censor of the Shanxi Circuit Lu; Departmental Secretary of the Board of War Yang; and Assistant Department Director of the Board of Works Sun. Noting the gathering was even more intimate than the previous year, Scholar Mei instructed a young servant to convey two sets of cards to the study for the young gentlemen, Wei and Li.","On the fifth day, Lady Yan likewise prepared to host a gathering. She extended invitations to her cousin-in-law, Wang Wenhui's wife, Madam Lu, along with the young mistress of the Sun family and her two young nieces. She also invited the wife of Sun Lianggong, Madam Lu, together with her eldest daughter and two young mistresses—though the eldest Miss Sun politely declined.","These two Madam Lus—of the Wang and Sun households, respectively—were first cousins. The Madam Lu of the Wang family was the paternal cousin of Censor Lu Zongyuan; her own elder brother, Lu Zonghuai, currently served as Judicial Commissioner of Sichuan.","The Madam Lu of the Sun family was Lu Zongyuan's full sister. The Wang family's Madam Lu was forty-one, while the Sun family's Madam Lu was thirty-nine. Both women were second wives. Though entering middle age, they retained a remarkably youthful grace, appearing scarcely thirty. Sharing a fondness for opulent splendor, they adorned themselves with the vibrant elegance of much younger women. Wang Wenhui's wife possessed a lithe and graceful demeanor, complemented by a candid, spirited nature that harmonized perfectly with her husband. The Sun family's Madam Lu mirrored her elder sister's beauty but harbored a fierce, jealous temperament. She had always held her husband, Lianggong, in slight contempt, a disdain compounded by the presence of his three exceptionally gifted children from his first marriage—constant thorns in her side. In her idle moments, she would amuse herself by tormenting him. Lianggong's late first wife had been notably plain, yet had borne him a daughter and two sons in rapid succession. Having subsequently married this stunning beauty, he practically enshrined her like a revered bodhisattva. Raised in pampered indulgence, this Madam Lu, now approaching forty after over twenty years of marriage, had seen her fiery temper cool somewhat, leading to a much gentler treatment of Lianggong. Yet, his profound submission had long been cemented; despite her newfound grace, he remained bound to her in unwavering deference.","Madam Lu had borne but one child of her own—the young mistress married into the Wang family (Wang Xun's wife). Named Peiqiu, she was a paragon of virtue and beauty, cherished like a precious pearl, and had been married into the Wang household at eighteen. There was also the eldest daughter, her hair prematurely white, destined to remain unwed; this New Year marked her twenty-ninth year. Sihui, at twenty-six, and Siyuan, at twenty-four, were two thoroughly useless men who had nonetheless both taken wives. Sihui had married Miss Shen, named Yun'er, daughter of the Imperial College Director Shen Gong. Neat, clever, and endearing, she was driven to thoughts of despair the moment she arrived at her new home and laid eyes upon her husband. Only the desperate pleas of her maidservant stayed her hand, leaving her to resign herself to a bitter fate. She subsequently fled to her parents' home, refusing to return.","Her father, the Imperial College Director, was a rigidly orthodox Confucian. After delivering a stern lecture, he sent his daughter back. Miss Shen was truly driven to her wit's end. Adding to her misery was Sihui's boundless lust; he would constantly rub his red nose against her face, provoking a revulsion she could scarcely endure. Ultimately, she was forced to surrender her personal dowry maid to him. This maid, named Song'er, possessed feet a full eight inches long—resembling wooden boards—yet she was wildly flirtatious. Sihui, having already engaged in secret dalliances with her, found her an exceptionally accommodating partner. Treasuring her deeply, he granted her almost exclusive favor. How could Miss Shen harbor any jealousy? She only wished the pair might remain permanently entangled, like mating geckos, thus sparing her from her torment. Outwardly, she served her parents-in-law with perfect obedience, but in the quiet of her own chambers, she wept bitter tears.","Siyuan had married a woman of the Ba clan, named Laifeng. Her father, Ba Tianchong, hailed from Fengyang in northern Jiangsu, a man of clean and respectable lineage. A soldier since his youth, possessing a striking countenance and mastery in archery and horsemanship, his martial prowess had propelled him to the rank of Commander-in-Chief by his early forties. He now served as the coastal defender of Tianjin. Deceived by a matchmaker's honeyed lies, he had married his beloved daughter to Siyuan.","This Miss Ba was exquisitely beautiful, with rosy cheeks and an apricot-bright face. She possessed a slender waist and a tall, graceful figure; her willow-leaf brows were delicately arched, and her phoenix eyes radiated both fierce command and tender grace. Her temperament was fiery and fierce, nurtured by a lifetime of indulgence and adoration. Tragically, she was married off at the tender age of seventeen. She had envisioned the son of a civil official to be a paragon of romantic elegance and literary refinement. However, upon beholding Siyuan's monkey-like, dog-faced visage, accompanied by his stuttering, halting speech, she wept inconsolably in the bridal chamber for half the day. That evening, when Siyuan climbed into bed and attempted to undress her, she delivered a sharp slap across his face. Half of Siyuan's face swelled like a sun-ripened peach; howling like a wounded dog, he raised his fists to strike back. Seeing him approach, she stood up and planted a solid punch squarely on his chest, sending him tumbling head over heels. He scrambled up and fled to the door, where the attending matrons, servants, and maids frantically restrained him, spending hours mediating the dispute. Miss Ba, acting on a sudden surge of fury, soon regretted her rashness; realizing the unseemliness of a public scene and her family's inability to intervene from afar, she swallowed her pride in silence. Siyuan continued to babble until a matron covered his mouth. The attendants helped them undress, brokered a fragile peace, and only withdrew once both were lying down. Humbled by the blows, Siyuan dared not approach her again, keeping to the foot of the bed for the night. Days later, her wet nurse gently counseled her on propriety, arguing that an official's daughter must not strike her husband; regardless of his flaws, this was her predestined fate. Miss Ba, intelligent enough to recognize the finality of her situation, could only weep in private. Seeing her anger subside, Siyuan attempted to consummate the marriage days later. Waiting until she slept, he unfastened her clothes. Miss Ba considered resisting but, dreading a commotion, decided to see what the fool could manage. Astonishingly, Siyuan, who scorned his elder brother in all other matters, proved even less capable in this. Initially failing to find his way, when he finally did, he was already in tears, as limp as spun gold thread. Siyuan was consumed by anxiety and shame; Miss Ba, by resentment and fury. Thereafter, unless she was in a willing mood, she rarely permitted his approach. Thus, despite five or six years of marriage, Miss Ba remained unacquainted with true conjugal bliss. But we need not dwell further on this.","On that day, Wenhui's wife, accompanied by two daughters and one young daughter-in-law, arrived at the Mei residence in their fragrant carriages and embroidered sedan-chairs.",`Lady Yan, leading a retinue of female servants and maids, emerged to welcome them and guided them into the inner hall. Though past forty, Lady Yan possessed an almost immortal grace, her features closely resembling those of Ziyu. She found Miss Qionghua increasingly radiant—as pristine as newly fallen snow, as ethereal as one nourished by rosy dawn clouds, seemingly unburdened by mortal sustenance. Miss Ronghua, meanwhile, was luminous and bright, displaying an exquisite blend of outward beauty and inner refinement. The young mistress of the Sun household, Peiqiu, was charming, graceful, and gentle as spring. After the two noble ladies exchanged formal pleasantries, the young mistresses and the young lady offered a unified bow to Lady Yan, engaging in polite conversation. When Madam Lu inquired about Ziyu, Lady Yan explained he had gone out with his father, bringing a sense of relief to Miss Qionghua's heart. Suddenly, a servant announced, "The Madam and young mistresses of the Sun family have arrived." Lady Yan stepped forward to greet them. Madam Lu was a familiar presence, and while she had encountered the two young mistresses before, their elaborate attire today made them appear even more stunning. Fully aware of their unfortunate marriages, Lady Yan felt a profound compassion for them. Once the greetings concluded and casual chatter resumed, Wang Wenhui's wife lavishly praised Ziyu with obvious admiration. Lianggong's wife laughed and said, "Sister, your nephew is undoubtedly fine, but my niece is equally matched. Since you hold him in such high regard, why not pair my niece with your nephew, just as I matched my nephew with your niece? We would forge a closer bond, allowing me to share in the glory of this four-way family connection. Wouldn't that be splendid?" Initially puzzled, Lady Yan smiled after a moment's realization, "Sister, your tongue is so nimble; you had me completely lost! We have harbored this wish for a long time, but feared our child might not be worthy, hesitating to broach the subject lest we face a refusal. I worried my sister-in-law might not consent."`,`Wang Wenhui's wife replied, "Aunt, what kind of talk is that? We are close family; there is no need for such formality. The truth is, my children might not be worthy of your nephew. Aunt must be deferring the decision to her lord husband—how does he feel about it?" Lady Yan said, "My husband has long held this in his heart, often speaking of it at home. Last year, when our cousin came to act as a matchmaker, I was on the verge of suggesting it, but an interruption prevented me. It has lingered in my mind ever since." Lianggong's wife, bold and direct, declared, "Then let us settle it right here and now! In matters concerning children, a mother has the right to decide—must we always wait for the father?" Lady Yan answered, "Were it another family, I would not dare decide alone and would seek his father's consent. But regarding this niece, my husband and I have discussed it countless times and are of one mind. It depends entirely on whether my sister-in-law will grant us this honor." Wang Wenhui's wife said, "We feel exactly the same." Lianggong's wife then urged, "In that case, you two in-laws should exchange a bow—one word, and it is settled." Lady Yan bowed to Wang Wenhui's wife, who graciously returned the gesture. Remarkably decisive, Lianggong's wife examined Lady Yan's hair, removed a jade swallow hairpin, and walked over to pin it in Qionghua's hair. Qionghua's cheeks flushed a deep crimson; she raised her hand in a gentle attempt to stop it. Lianggong's wife laughed, "This is the matter of a lifetime—do not be shy." Mortified and unsure where to look, Qionghua could neither speak nor retreat, nor could she remove the hairpin. She bowed her head, her bright eyes brimming with tears, on the verge of weeping from sheer embarrassment. Her mother and Lady Yan offered gentle smiles, while the young mistresses shared knowing grins. Ronghua, seeing her younger sister in such distress, took her hand and led her outside the balustrade to admire the flowers, continuing their stroll through the other rooms. The young mistresses followed. Lianggong's wife remarked, "Did I not play the matchmaker perfectly? Both of you in-laws owe me your gratitude—a truly talented young man and a beautiful girl, a flawless match in every respect. Unlike my three useless ones: two inept sons have already ruined two fine young ladies, and there remains a useless daughter at home—could she ruin someone as well? Well, that is of no concern to us." Wang Wenhui's wife asked, "Do your two young mistresses get along well?" Lianggong's wife offered a cold laugh: "How could they possibly get along? Human nature is universal—can I favor my sons over my daughters-in-law? Even I feel pity when observing their plight.`,'"As for the first—she manages to endure it outwardly. She locks her resentment away, finding small, idle amusements at his expense when she has nothing else to do.',`"The second one's temper burns even hotter than mine. Our second son is worse than the first—constantly mumbling, stuttering, clumsy, and endlessly restless. I often hear her beating him, the sound echoing through the house as he scrambles from room to room; I can only pretend not to hear. She is as beautiful as a flower—how could I possibly fault her? Forced to share her days and nights with a monkey, she naturally suffers both anger and grief. I am the most clear-sighted of women, never one to shield my children's flaws or claim only my sons are worthy. Only your brother-in-law could raise children as magnificent as these." This prompted laughter from both ladies. Now, let us turn to the young mistresses and Miss Qionghua, who had wandered into a tranquil suite of three small, exquisitely refined rooms.`,`Famous calligraphy and ancient paintings adorned the walls, while Zhou-dynasty bronze cauldrons and Shang-dynasty sacrificial vessels were elegantly displayed. Inside, two small maids served tea. The young mistress, née Shen, inquired, "Whose room is this?" A maid replied, "It is the Young Master's room." Shen asked, "Is the Young Master absent?" The maid confirmed, "He is not here." At ease, the young mistresses began to explore freely. Entering the inner chamber, they discovered a small nanmu-wood bed, its brocade curtains suspended from gleaming silver hooks—a resplendent sight—while a delicate fragrance of orchid and musk drifted through the air.`,`Finding the room refined and elegant, the young mistresses took their seats. The young mistress, née Ba, who had never laid eyes on Ziyu, noticed a portrait of a beautiful young man on a mirror screen—a face of flawless, powder-white skin and crimson lips, radiating an elegance that dazzled the eye, unlike anyone she had ever seen. Pulling the young mistress, née Sun, closer, she said, "Sister-in-law, look at this painting—isn't it remarkably done?" The young mistress, née Sun, smiled and replied, "That is our future second brother-in-law—a truly remarkable likeness." Ronghua and the young mistress, née Shen, gathered to admire Ziyu's portrait, but Qionghua refused to approach, wandering alone to the writing desk. Idly flipping through a book, she discovered a leaf of floral-patterned stationery inscribed with several seven-character quatrains titled "The Person in the Carriage"—clearly verses born of longing for a fleetingly glimpsed beauty. Examining the final line of the third poem, she noticed it rhymed on the character 'qiong' (琼), invoking the immortal maiden Xu Feiqi; the closing line of the fourth poem rhymed on 'hua' (华), invoking the immortal maiden Ruan Linghua.`,`Qionghua's heart leaped in shock. She thought, "Is my cousin truly so frivolous? He has fractured my very name, embedding its characters within his rhymes. What if someone were to discover this?" Seizing the moment while the others were absorbed by the portrait, she used her fingernail to discreetly scrape away the two characters, her face flushing crimson, before quietly withdrawing from the room.`,`Soon after, the other young mistresses emerged. The young mistress, née Ba, could scarcely tear her gaze from Ziyu's portrait; as she left, she cast two more lingering glances backward, involuntarily exclaiming, "Now that is a truly magnificent young nobleman!"`,"The beauties smiled knowingly. Lady Yan dispatched a maid to invite them to the banquet, and the young women obliged. The gathering was divided into two tables: the three noble ladies at one, and the five young women at the other. The two Madam Lus proved exceptionally engaging conversationalists, while the young mistresses sparkled with lively spirits. Only Miss Qionghua remained uneasy throughout the day, sitting quietly without uttering a word. She harbored a deep resentment toward her aunt for pinning Lady Yan's hairpin in her hair, feeling as though her head now weighed a thousand catties, unable to be lifted.","The young mistresses sensed her distress; despite their efforts to comfort her with pleasantries, she kept her head bowed in silence, deeply regretting her attendance. The two noble ladies and the young women enjoyed each other's company throughout the day, finally parting ways only after the evening meal, when the first night-watch drum sounded.",`The following day brought the fellowship gathering at the Suzhou Guild Hall. Scholar Mei departed early in the morning. Pincai had borrowed a set of clothes from Ziyu the previous night—a perfect fit. Only Yuanmao remained dissatisfied with his attire, brooding in a foul mood; seeing Ziyu emerge in his splendid official hat and elegant garments, he felt acutely inferior and stubbornly tore off his clothes, reverting to his ordinary dress, declaring, "I will not go." Ziyu immediately dispatched Yun'er to inform the Mistress and request a set of his own clothes for Young Master Li. Yun'er promptly returned with a bundle. Yet, while Ziyu and Yuanmao shared a similar height, their builds were vastly different: Yuanmao possessed a thick neck and a broad waist. The collar refused to fasten, and once the waist was tied, the garment was a full three inches too short. Ziyu sighed, "This will not do—my clothes do not fit you. You had better wear our Master's." He sent Yun'er back to exchange them for Scholar Mei's garments. Scholar Mei was exceptionally tall, and both pieces were heavy, expansive fur coats. When Yuanmao donned them, the hems dragged along the floor. Pincai hoisted the fabric a few inches, tightly bound the waist, and smoothed the front and back—producing an effect that resembled a pigeon-breasted hunchback.`,"Next, he donned the outer lynx-fur coat, and Ziyu placed a massive sable fur hat upon his head—a colossal, fluffy sphere that barely fit within the carriage. Ziyu and Pincai burst into irrepressible laughter. Accompanied by four book-boys, they found two carriages already harnessed with four horses waiting outside. Ziyu rode alone in one, while Pincai and Yuanmao shared the other. They made straight for the Suzhou Guild Hall, where carriages were already packed tightly.",`The trio entered. The servants of the Mei household, catching sight of them, rushed forward, announcing, "Young Master Wang and Young Master Yan have been waiting; all the esteemed gentlemen arrived long ago." They were led directly to the main seating area, where the operatic performance was already underway. Among the distinguished elders, there were several Ziyu had yet to meet; Scholar Mei pointed them out, and Ziyu offered his respects to each, receiving lavish praise in return. Pincai and Yuanmao then stepped forward to greet Wang Wenhui. Pincai, nimble and composed, managed the introduction smoothly; Yuanmao, however, hindered by his nearsightedness and cumbersome attire, struggled to maneuver. As he rose from his bow, he accidentally bumped Wenhui's hat, knocking it askew. Wenhui quickly adjusted it, while Yuanmao flushed deeply, attempting an awkward retreat.`,`Just then, Imperial College Director Shen—elderly but famously talkative—seized Ziyu's arm. Struck by his extraordinary, almost ethereal beauty, and remembering his own daughter-in-law, he mused that the human realm held two extremes: celestial beings walking the earth, and monstrous demons. He inquired about the books Ziyu was currently studying and the compositions he was practicing; Ziyu answered each question thoughtfully. Still young, the relentless praise from these venerable elders left him profoundly embarrassed. When Director Shen finally released him, Ziyu and his companions excused themselves and ascended to the eastern upper floor. There, Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing hurried to greet them, bowing and saying, "We have waited endlessly—how is it you've only just arrived?" Ziyu replied, "I rose a little late today. Have Elder Brother Sun and Second Brother Sun not yet arrived?" Wang Xun answered, "They should be here any moment." Wang and Yan warmly welcomed Pincai and Yuanmao. Across the upper floor, another group had assembled, hosted by Liu Wenze, son of the Right Vice-Minister. His guests included Shi Nanxiang, son of Censor Shi; Zhang Zhongyu, nephew of Grand Academician Wu; the renowned Suzhou scholar Gao Pin; Shen Bocai, son of Imperial College Director Shen; and Ba Lin, son of Commander-in-Chief Ba, the coastal defender of Tianjin. The latter two were brothers-in-law to the Sun brothers. A native of the capital, Feng Zipei, son of the former Jiangsu county magistrate, had yet to arrive. Ziyu recognized no one in this group save for Nanxiang and Wenze. Liu Wenze, courtesy name Qianzhou, hailed from a prominent Zhongzhou family and held a second-rank hereditary title. Known for his generous spirit and approachability, he deeply valued friendship and was exceptionally close to Xu Ziyun and Hua Xingbei. Zhang Zhongyu, a quick-witted and handsome Yangzhou native, had traveled to the capital seeking an alternative path to officialdom, lodging with his uncle, Grand Academician Wu. Masterful in chess, qin, flute, and all manner of entertainments, he had purchased a ninth-rank position and was a vibrant fixture in social circles. Gao Pin, styled Zhuoran, a Suzhou native and supplementary tribute student, possessed extraordinary intelligence and a talent for witty banter; his remarks invariably threw the entire room into uproar. Connected to Director Shen through family ties, he delighted in teasing the Sun brothers. Shen Bocai, a provincial graduate past thirty, had recently been appointed a county magistrate and was preparing for his post—a shrewd and capable man. Ba Lin, visiting his elder sister from his father's station, matched her striking beauty. Only twenty, he excelled in both civil and martial arts. Disliking the Sun brothers, he chose to lodge at an inn and maintained a close friendship with Liu Wenze.`,`Wang Xun and Zhongqing guided Ziyu over, facilitating introductions. Bound by examination-year ties and family connections, they exchanged warm pleasantries. Liu Wenze remarked, "Yong'an, when arranging this banquet, why didn't you inform me? Even these two guest-friends of yours could have joined us perfectly well. Why must you sit separately over there?" Wang Xun smiled, "It was not my intention to isolate us. Yuxiang is, of course, beyond question, but Elder Brothers Li and Wei are also the most delightful company.`,'"Today, I also invited the Sun brothers—and they are an entirely different matter. Elder Brother Shen, though not close to them, can tolerate their presence. But I truly feared that the moment Old Third Ba saw them, a brawl would erupt." Laughter rippled through the group.',`Ba Lin chimed in, "Elder Brother Wang, this is where you erred. If you have three honored guests, you shouldn't have invited those two disagreeable ones—they make a man lose his appetite. It is only out of respect for family ties that I endure them." The room erupted in laughter. Gao Pin added, "Excellent! Excellent! Let us all sit together today—why should anyone lose their appetite? With the 'worm-eaten History of the Former Han and Thousand Character Classic' and the 'rhyme-and-alliteration compendium,' it is far more entertaining than drinking wine to the recitation of the Hanshu!" Shi Nanxiang said, "What is there to fear? Bring them over! Bring them over! With so many venerable elders at the main tables, Old Third Ba won't dare start anything." Left with no choice, Wang Xun ordered the two tables moved over, and everyone sat together. Nanxiang turned to Ziyu, "Today you will witness magnificent opera and exquisite performers—then you will believe I was not exaggerating." Ziyu smiled, "You named your foremost one; I have already had the pleasure." Nanxiang pressed, "And? Does your appreciation endure?" Ziyu merely smiled. Wang Xun asked, "When did you see her?" Ziyu replied, "Don't you remember? That day when you asked me to buy you a meal and I delayed you—have you truly forgotten?" Nanxiang leaned in, perplexed, preparing to ask for clarification. Wang Xun laughed loudly, "What a misunderstanding! That was not Yuan Baozhu at all—that was the darkest of the dark-class performers, whose name also happened to be Bao Zhu. Yuxiang heard the name and immediately assumed him to be your top choice. I was about to explain, but got caught up in the commotion and never had the chance." Nanxiang burst into hearty laughter, and Ziyu finally realized it had been a completely different 'Baozhu' than the one in the Selection of Flowers.`,`Just then, the Wang household servant announced, "The young Sun masters have arrived." Sihui and his brother first offered their respects at the main tables before ascending the stairs, welcomed by forced smiles from the crowd. Sweeping his gaze across the room, Sihui offered a general bow. Catching sight of Gao Pin and Shen Bocai, alarm gripped his heart; he thought, "What wretched luck today—running into these two arch-enemies." Siyuan, spotting Ba Lin, felt his stomach drop; he paid his respects, to which Ba Lin responded with a begrudging half-bow. The upper floor was arranged with four tables. Liu Wenze announced, "We are all friends here—no need for formalities; sit wherever you please." Eager to distance themselves from the Sun brothers, everyone scrambled for seats. Liu Wenze, Shen Bocai, Ba Lin, and Zhang Zhongyu claimed one table; Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, and Gao Pin pulled Ziyu to a second; Pincai and Yuanmao took a third; leaving Sihui and Siyuan isolated at the fourth, with Wang Xun moving between tables to act as host. Sun Sihui, adopting his pedantic "the Master says" persona, began interrogating Wei and Li about their names and native places. Dishes were served, and wine flowed. Gao Pin noted that the pimples on Sihui's face had visibly multiplied; after a few cups of wine, his red nose glowed like a frostbitten chili pepper—radiant and intensely red.`,`With a sly grin, Gao Pin turned to Shen Bocai, "In all the world, what thing is both red and shiny? You cannot offer an elegant answer—only the most vulgar thing you can imagine." Bocai instantly understood the allusion to Sihui's nose and smiled, "Give me an example first." Gao Pin replied, "I'll say: Red and shiny—the year ends, spring returns, and a dog's lust awakens." The group struggled to suppress their laughter. Sihui, catching the insult, glared at Gao Pin and retorted, "Why resort to such reproachable language? Even the crowing of cocks and the barking of dogs echo across the four quarters." Laughter erupted once more. Shen Bocai chimed in, "I have one: Red and shiny—in urgent need, an anal hemorrhoid prolapses." Terrified the sound would reach the main tables below, the men swallowed their laughter, though muffled sniggers swept through the room. Ba Lin added, "I have one as well—perhaps slightly more respectable than yours: Red and shiny—a wine-blossomed nose hanging dead center on the face." Gao Pin laughed, "No good—you've exposed the subject entirely. Now there's nothing left to say." Sihui interjected, "Absurd! What things are red? What things are shiny?" He paused, then stammered, "Red and shiny..." before falling entirely silent, unable to finish his thought.`,`Observing his flustered expression, the room erupted into even louder laughter. Siyuan burst into guffaws, tears streaming from his drooping eye, and stuttered, "I-I-I have one: Red-red-red and sh-sh-sh-shiny—a-a-a-a fireball f-f-f-flies up onto the b-b-bed." The crowd laughed uncontrollably, nearly shouting in amusement. Yan Zhongqing remarked, "With a fire like that, it truly becomes entirely red and shiny." Gao Pin added, "That fire has inspired Old Second Sun's Three-Character Classic." When the others looked puzzled, Gao Pin explained, "During a fire, people naturally yell: 'Come, come, come! Quick, quick, quick! Save it, save it, save it! Grab the valuables—grab, grab, grab! Run for your lives—run, run, run! The wind blows—whoosh, whoosh, whoosh! The fire roars—boom, boom, boom! Things ignite and burst—crack, crack, crack! Pop, pop, pop! People scream in chaos—heehee, heehee, heehee! Out, out, out!' Is that not a complete Three-Character Classic?" Ba Lin interjected, "Old Second Sun has two specialized Classics—do you know them?" Gao Pin laughed, "I had no idea." Ba Lin elaborated, "Firing a hand-gun, and emptying a chamber-pot—these are his two specialized Classics." Uncertain of the vicious implication, the group pressed for an explanation. Ba Lin clarified, "Also in three-character format: Firing the hand-gun is: pull-pull-pull. Emptying the chamber-pot is: lean-lean-lean." The room roared with laughter. Ziyu praised, "These two Classics are exceptionally fine—truly expressed with perfect naturalness." From that moment, Siyuan gained the moniker "the Unapproved Three-Character Classic." Enraged, Siyuan was ready to explode, but fearful of his father's presence downstairs and terrified of another beating from Ba Lin, he dared not retaliate. Forced to swallow his fury, he kept his mouth shut, his drooping eye wide and brimming with resentful tears.`,`A moment later, Pincai's attendant approached and whispered, "Master Ye has sent the opera program." Ziyu leaned over to review it with Pincai. The opening pieces—'Sweeping Flowers,' 'Three Times Drunk,' 'Discussing the Sword,' 'Paying Respects to the Teacher,' and 'Appreciating the Lotuses'—had already concluded. The remaining lineup included 'Merit Banquet,' 'The Jade Terrace,' 'Dancing on the Tray,' 'Stealing the Poem,' 'Inscribing the Song,' 'The Monastery Gate,' 'Going on the Hunt,' 'A Stroll in the Garden and Waking from a Dream,' and 'Knight-Errant in Hiding,' 'Returning from the Hunt,' 'The Peony Pavilion—Waking from a Dream,' and finally, 'The Hero in Concealment' from The Bright Pearl Record. Ziyu murmured to Pincai, "The program is fine, but I wonder if Qin Guan is in any of these." Before he finished speaking, a voice from below shouted, "Without Yuan Baozhu's performance, I absolutely refuse!"`,`Ziyu and the others peered over the balcony: Wang Wenhui was throwing a tantrum. A man stood beside him, bowing repeatedly and offering placating smiles. Wenhui snapped, "Even at Master Xu's house, what harm is there in singing a single piece before leaving? Besides, when this opera was arranged, what were you explicitly told?" The man explained, "In this 'Waking from a Dream,' there is a newly arrived performer, Qin Guan, who surpasses even Baozhu. If Your Lordship doubts this, let him sing one piece first—if he fails to meet your expectations, I will rush off immediately to fetch Baozhu, guaranteeing no delay." Vice-Minister Liu intervened, "Very well, then—after 'The Jade Terrace' concludes, 'Waking from a Dream' will do perfectly." The man eagerly agreed with a chorus of "Yes, yes, sirs!" and, seeing Wenhui mollified, hurried into the theater room. Pincai nudged Ziyu, "Did you hear that?" Ziyu nodded, feeling a profound surge of gratitude toward Wenhui.`,"As 'Merit Banquet' concluded, 'The Jade Terrace' commenced. Catching his first glimpse of the performer, Ziyu started; in a momentary daze, he mistook the figure for Qin Guan, before realizing this actor was slightly older.",`What he beheld was a vision akin to an auspicious moon framed by blessed clouds, a radiant morning mist kissed by celestial dew—fragrant, warm, and suffused with the essence of spring. He was like a blossom opening to eight-tenths of its glory, his beauty shining with absolute brilliance. Already transfixed, Ziyu asked Nanxiang, "Who is this? He possesses such exquisite bone structure." Nanxiang replied, "Do you find this good? I'm afraid he barely registers on the rankings." Knowing Nanxiang was intentionally baiting him, Ziyu turned to Zhongqing. Zhongqing explained, "This is the Selection of Flowers's number two—Su Huifang, the Jade Moon of the The Jade Terrace." Ziyu marveled, "All the spiritual essence of heaven and earth is concentrated here. I was truly a summer insect, unable to conceive of ice. No wonder Zhujun reproached me." Nanxiang laughed heartily, "I don't reproach you either—fortunately, you admit your own ignorance!" Wenze chimed in, "What? Yuxiang doesn't even recognize Su Meixiang?" Nanxiang added, "He is a scholar who never steps outside—how could he possibly know the ways of the world?" Shortly after, 'The Jade Terrace' concluded, and 'Waking from a Dream' began.`,`Anxious that Qin Guan might struggle to match Su Huifang's brilliance, Ziyu focused his entire attention on the stage. The entrance curtain was swept aside, revealing Qin Guan—the very same youth he had glimpsed twice before, whose delicate features were flawlessly engraved in his memory. At the resonant strike of the hand-gong, lotus steps glided forward, carrying a fragrant breeze. It was a vision akin to the full-moon night of the Mid-Autumn Festival, as if the Dragon Palace had unveiled its treasures, their jeweled radiance ascending to meet the moonlight. He seemed woven from clouds yet grounded in reality, a celestial canopy of vibrant, five-colored brilliance—a splendor and beauty beyond earthly comparison. This dazzling radiance shattered Ziyu's gaze into fragments, swirling breathlessly around every inch of Qin Guan, impossible to gather back. The closer he looked, the more the world blurred around him. Fortunately, the singing began—starting with the line, "Returning from a dream, the orioles warble"—and Ziyu listened, enraptured by every syllable. As Qin Guan sang, "All my life I have loved what is natural," and "What avail this fair season and lovely scene," Ziyu felt a thread of subtle fragrance drifting from the performer's lips, articulating sorrow and yearning with absolute clarity, laying bare the innermost depths of human emotion. It was truly the miracle of a celestial being taking human form. As the aria continued—"In one and the same heavenly bond, what blessed fate—that has cast this green spring so far away"—every word struck Ziyu's very soul; he nearly wept, barely reining in his tears. As Liu Mengmei took the stage, singing, "Endure for now and rest tenderly a while," Pincai asked, "Well, what do you think?" Ziyu heard nothing; his very spirit seemed tethered to the young leading man, vanishing behind the stage with Qin Guan. Suddenly, Li Yuanmao blundered over, clapping Ziyu on the shoulder: "This is Qin Guan—would you say he's good?" The sudden impact startled Ziyu, while the rest of the group remained locked in rapt attention.`,`The moment Qin Guan stepped onto the stage, he had instantly spotted Ziyu. Having encountered him once more in a dream—making this their fourth encounter in total—he felt a secret surge of joy: "What a fortunate coincidence—this gentleman is here today." During his second entrance, while singing the aria 'Fragrance of Rain and Drifting Clouds,' his eyes continually wandered to rest upon Ziyu, filling Ziyu's heart with a quiet, glowing warmth. Wenze murmured to Nanxiang, "This newly arrived young performer seems exceptionally intimate with Yuxiang—look at his expression, all his attention is directed entirely at him." Nanxiang turned to Ziyu, "What is this performer's name?" Ziyu replied, "He is called Qin Guan." Nanxiang pressed, "How many times have you two spent time together?" Ziyu answered honestly, "I don't actually know him." Wenze laughed, "Yuxiang, when speaking of a performer, one usually tries to keep it a secret—but with this kind of four-eyes-meeting, two-hearts-illuminating display, to claim you don't know him... what would it take to actually count as knowing?" The group smiled knowingly at Ziyu, who, devoid of any defense, felt his face flush deeply. Following this piece, they watched Lu Sulan in 'Dancing on the Tray,' Jin Shufang in 'Inscribing the Song,' and Li Yulin in 'Stealing the Poem'—all superlative performers of unmatched, fragrant brilliance. Ziyu could only bow his head to Nanxiang, acknowledging the magnitude of his earlier error.`,`At the tables, Zhang Zhongyu and Pincai discovered a shared family connection through marriage and struck up a fast friendship. Pincai dedicated his time to cultivating goodwill, offering compliments to everyone seated nearby. Ziyu watched with quiet amusement as the opera performers circulated among the main tables, pouring wine and engaging in lively banter. He also noted his uncle, Wang Wenhui, who appeared more exuberantly animated than anyone else in the room. Another operatic piece commenced. At the primary tables, Vice-Minister Liu, Scholar Mei, Grand Academician Wu, and Director Shen were the first to depart. Noting his father's absence and the late hour, Ziyu also prepared to leave. Just as he rose, a stunningly beautiful young man suddenly ascended the stairs. Wenze's servant announced, "Young Master Feng has arrived!" Feng Zipei stepped forward, offering his respects to the room. Ziyu observed that he was merely eighteen or nineteen, possessing a loveliness rivaling that of a beautiful young woman, thoroughly captivating in his presence. Liu Wenze asked, "Everyone is about to disperse—how is it you've only just arrived?" Feng Zipei explained, "This morning I went into the city to pay my New Year's respects to the Hua household at Jinchun Garden, intending a brief visit. However, Hua Xingbei absolutely insisted I stay for dinner and forced me to watch several more pieces of opera before letting me leave; I rushed here as quickly as I could." Ziyu, alongside Yuanmao and Pincai, bid their farewells; the entire group accompanied them to the staircase landing, with Wenze, Wang Xun, and Zhongqing escorting them all the way downstairs.`,`Wenze turned to Ziyu and said, "On the ninth, I am hosting a small gathering and hope Elder Brother will honor us with a visit—a refined discourse and elegant assembly. It will be an intimate group: just Zhujun, Jiantan, Yong'an, Zhuoran, and a few others. Elder Brother must absolutely not decline." Ziyu agreed, expressing his deep gratitude. Wang Xun, Pincai, and Yuanmao offered their thanks in turn before departing. The remaining guests lingered in conversation for a while longer before dispersing into the night. As for the events that followed, please await the unfolding of the next chapter.`]},f=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations6:s},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={7:["After returning from the guild hall, Ziyu spent two full days reflecting on Qin Guan's performance, convinced it was the pinnacle of worldly beauty. He also appraised Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, and Li Yulin, finding them all to be utterly superlative. Yet upon careful comparison, Huifang's spirit and bearing surpassed the rest, with Sulan as her only true equal. But when measured against Qin Guan, even by the finest margin, they all fell slightly short. He thought to himself:",`"If Qin Guan's beauty were not placed upon the stage, the world would have no chance to admire and praise him, leaving him buried in obscurity. Perhaps the Creator, in a playful mood of bestowing spiritual essence, deliberately cast him into the theater world to display the wondrous workings of fate. Thus, this king of flowers was born, followed by so many flower-companions to surround him, much like the hundred flowers attending the peony."`,`"But a beautiful flower offers only one season of delight, whereas a person's beauty can endure for a decade. Since a person surpasses a flower, the love one bears for a person should naturally be all the deeper. Who would have thought the eyes of the world could be so perfectly aligned? I had assumed that Shi Zhujun, Wang Yong'an, and the others were exaggerating, but now I see their descriptions actually fell short. Their claims about his poetic and painting talents must also be unequivocally true." He then thought: "Although I saw Qin Guan perform today, I had no chance to make his acquaintance. When might we meet again? Yet seeing his eyes repeatedly glance my way, it seemed he harbored some tender sentiment. Earlier, when he lifted the carriage curtain to gaze out, it felt as though he did not treat our roadside encounter as mere coincidence. What could this mean?"`,`The more he pondered, the less clarity he found. Desiring to review the 'The Person in the Carriage' verses he had composed earlier—and perhaps add two more—he brought them out. To his great surprise, he noticed that two characters had been scraped away from the third and fourth poems. He immediately asked a small maidservant, "Who came in here to look at my books over the past two days?" The maidservant replied, "The day before yesterday, the Mistress hosted some guests—a group of young ladies and the Wang family's Second Miss, who all came in to look around. The young ladies spent a long time admiring the Young Master's portrait, while the Second Miss looked at your books. No one else came in. When the Second Miss was reading, I saw her turn to a page, look at it, scrape a spot with her fingernail, and then place it back in the book." She then laughed and added, "The other day, I heard the Second Miss's maid, Xue'er, say that Madam Sun acted as a matchmaker and betrothed the Second Miss to you, Young Master—she even took one of the Mistress's hairpins with her." Half-believing, Ziyu asked, "I don't believe it—are you making this up?" The maidservant protested, "Would I dare lie? I was watching the room that day and didn't dare leave. That's what Xue'er said. I expect the whole household knows by now." Hearing this, Ziyu felt a surge of joy. He suddenly recalled his second cousin's appearance, which bore some resemblance to Qin Guan's, and began comparing the two in his mind, unsure of who was finer. Then, thinking of the scraped-away characters, he realized in a flash: "Who would have thought I inadvertently used her name—an unintentional coincidence, how extraordinary! She must think I was deliberately longing for her, and her heart surely reproaches me. What am I to do? I have no way to explain myself; this is truly an unjust grievance." He then reasoned, "Since the betrothal is settled, there's no harm even if she does blame me." Ziyu was then led by the two characters of 'Qionghua' to think once more of Qin Yan, becoming increasingly entangled in tender thoughts; his heart's yearning for beauty blossomed from this very moment.`,"On the ninth day, Liu Wenze sent someone to extend another invitation. After informing his mother, Ziyu changed his clothes and departed in his carriage.",`At Wenze's residence, the invited guests—Yan Zhongqing, Wang Xun, and Shi Nanxiang—had already arrived, followed shortly by Mei Ziyu and Gao Pin at the gate. A servant guided them past the main hall, through a flowered gate beside the reception hall, and onto a pebble-paved path. On either side, irregular artificial hills of Lake Tai rocks were artfully arranged, interspersed with sparse, graceful winter trees. In the distance, terraces and pavilions could be seen, the entire layout exceptionally secluded and elegant. After two or three turns and crossing a stone bridge, they found themselves on a path flanked uniformly by green bamboo, enclosing a stretch of red balustrade. Directly ahead stood a five-bay rolled-eave hall. Yan Zhongqing and the others were waiting in the corridor, while Liu Wenze had already stepped down to welcome them. Gao Pin and Ziyu went forward to greet their host, and then everyone exchanged pleasantries based on age. Shi Nanxiang and Gao Pin were both twenty-five, but Gao Pin, born in the second month, was the elder. Yan Zhongqing was twenty-four, Wang Xun twenty-three, and Ziyu eighteen. Though Wenze was also twenty-four, he presided as host. As everyone took their seats, they naturally exchanged a few words of greeting. Ziyu, visiting for the first time, observed his surroundings attentively. In the center hung a nanmu wood plaque with blue characters reading, 'Chamber of the Leaning Sword and Sleeping Lute.' The flanking couplets, carved on areca-palm wood, read:

'Tea smoke just rising, the crane's dream yet unawakened—here one finds a touch of rare delight;
Pine wind gently blowing, the mountain spring clearly heard—where else could a speck of dust alight?'`,`The inscription read, 'Composed by Daosheng, written by Qu Benli,' showcasing calligraphy of exceptional, archaic charm. Below sat a large table laden with numerous books. To the side were twelve pots of forced winter flowers, their fragrance wafting over and intoxicating the senses. Taking it all in, Ziyu was reminded of Qin Yan's performance—a dazzling, jewel-like radiance and a fragrance that pierced the bone—and he felt momentarily dazed. Suddenly, Wenze's voice broke his reverie: "This room is too drafty; let us sit inside." They proceeded east, where a book-boy lifted a curtain revealing three studies, divided centrally by a glass partition. Walking around it, they found two connecting rooms within. Through the south-facing window, the outside was clearly visible. Above hung Dong Qichang's calligraphy bearing the characters 'Xu Bai' (Empty Clarity), alongside Ni Yunlin's painting of withered wood, bamboo, and rocks. The flanking couplets read:

'Within moral teachings lies a realm of joy;
Beyond wind and moonlight, there is little to discuss.'

In the center of the room rested a long, rectangular basin of Han white marble, inscribed with numerous poems and filled with narcissus, their blooms half-opened. Beside it in another pot stood a large green-calyx white plum tree, over five feet tall. Its gnarled trunk was covered with buds, mostly closed, save for one or two branches blooming toward the window.`,"Because the room was warmed by a heated floor, the banquet was set up inside. The host served the wine, and everyone took their seats.",`Nanxiang remarked, "It's a pity we didn't invite any performers today." Wenze replied, "I had intended to, but I learned they are all attending the ninth-day Winter Gathering at Yiyuan. Not a single one went to the theatrical halls, so I refrained from calling them, fearing it would put them in a difficult position." Nanxiang continued:`,`"Today, amidst the red dust of the world, we can enjoy a fleeting moment of refined assembly." Zhongqing was seated just below Gao Pin. Gao Pin leaned close to Zhongqing's ear and whispered something, causing Zhongqing to break into a silent laugh. The others asked Zhongqing, "What did he say?" Zhongqing turned to Gao Pin, "Shall I tell them?" Gao Pin shook his head. Zhongqing said, "His match for the seventh character is exceptionally clever." The two exchanged a knowing smile. Nanxiang, impatient as always, urged, "Tell us, and I'll gladly drink a cup." Gao Pin countered, "Drink ten cups, and then we'll see." Knowing Nanxiang's low tolerance for wine, Wenze mediated, "Let's compromise—three cups." Gao Pin smiled, "Zhujun drinks three, and the rest of you drink one each to appreciate the remark." Zhongqing chimed in, "I've already been enlightened, so I'm exempt." Gao Pin laughed, "Since when?" Zhongqing retorted, "With a mouth like yours, a dog's jaw truly cannot produce ivory." Nanxiang insisted, "Quick, bring the wine so he can tell us." He drank three cups, and the others followed with one each. Gao Pin smiled at Zhongqing, "Since you've been enlightened, you tell them." Laughing, Zhongqing fined Gao Pin a cup of wine and revealed, "He said, 'In the Xu Bai room, three pairs of chicken... parts.'" At first, no one understood.`,`Wenze asked, "What is so amusing about that?" Suddenly catching on, Nanxiang clapped his hands and roared with laughter, "This mischievous devil is truly incorrigible, but one must admit it's a quick match!" Ziyu and the others also burst into laughter as realization dawned. "He took an elegant word and gave it a crude, literal twist—quite a precise match." Wenze proposed, "Zhuo Brother, I'll give you a line to match, but you cannot pause to think. If it's good, I'll drink three cups; if you fail, you drink ten; if it's mediocre, five." Gao Pin negotiated, "It's always easy to set a line, but hard to match it. How about three cups if I fail, and one if it's mediocre?" Nanxiang added, "It depends on how difficult the line is. Let's hear it." Wenze turned to Ziyu, "I must borrow your esteemed name: 'Ziyu is like jade' (Ziyu ren ru yu)." Zhongqing mused, "That won't be easy." Before he could finish, Gao Pin declared, "I've got it. Drink your three cups." Wenze challenged, "Let's hear it." Nanxiang chimed in, "If it's truly good, we must all drink a toast." Gao Pin smiled and recited, "'Maojin's face is gold' (Maojin mian shi jin). How is that?" Wang Xun observed, "'Maojin' (Liu) against 'jade' is an absolute match." Nanxiang critiqued, "It's just that 'face is gold' lacks a bit of classical elegance." Gao Pin defended, "It may not be classical, but it is highly apt. Haven't you seen the Rabbit God figurines sold at the Mid-Autumn Festival stalls? Their faces are all painted gold!" The room erupted in laughter. Wenze groaned, "You wicked spirit, you're even cursing your sworn brother now." Gao Pin quipped, "The arrow was on the bowstring; it had to be fired." The host gracefully accepted his penalty, and the entire table joined in with a cup. Nanxiang suggested, "Drinking and penalizing like this feels a bit dull. The poetry tile game we played the other day was delightful. Yaoqing composed two superb pieces, though it's a pity they are at Yiyuan today. Why don't we devise a new drinking game?" Liu Wenze proposed, "Let's play the poetry-matching game today." Ziyu asked, "How does one match poetry?" Zhongqing explained, "It's quite simple. The game-master splits a line of poetry and gives one character to each person to match. If the matched characters form a coherent line, you're spared; if even a single character fails to connect, you drink a cup. It often leads to hilarious results—it's incredibly fun." Gao Pin agreed, "Let it be poetry matching. The host must drink the starting cup." After drinking, Wenze had someone fetch a nanmu wood board. He wrote down the surnames following the seating order and announced, "I shall set the first character." He wrote a 'Zhong' (Middle). After a moment's thought: Yan matched 'Wai' (Outside), Gao matched 'Hou' (Behind), Mei matched 'Shang' (Above), Shi also matched 'Shang', and Wang matched 'Li' (Inside). Wenze then set a 'Feng' (Phoenix): Yan matched 'Hong' (Swan), Gao matched 'Ji' (Chicken), Mei matched 'Luan' (Mythical Bird), Shi matched 'Ya' (Crow), and Wang matched 'Wu' (Raven). Wenze offered 'Xia' (Descend/Down). Nanxiang declared, "I'm handing in my paper first—I match 'Gui' (Return)." Gao Pin quickly followed with 'Qian' (Front); Zhongqing and Ziyu simultaneously matched 'Lai' (Come); Wang Xun matched 'Hui' (Return). Wenze recorded them all. Next came 'Fu' (Support/Assist): Gao rushed to match 'Kao' (Lean), Shi matched 'Song' (Send), Yan matched 'Ji' (Send/Entrust), Wang matched 'Yu' (Drive/Control), and Mei matched 'Ting' (Listen).`,`Wenze presented 'Shuang' (Double/Pair): Zhongqing matched 'Gu' (Solitary), Gao Pin matched 'Ba' (Eight), Ziyu matched 'Jiu' (Nine), and Wang Xun lamented, "Oh no, I suppose I must follow the sequence with 'Shi' (Ten)." Nanxiang pondered, "That's right, my character is a bit tricky to place. Very well, I'll match 'San' (Three)." Finally, Wenze set 'Nian' (Imperial Carriage). Nanxiang exclaimed, "I know exactly which line of poetry this is!" Ziyu quickly matched 'Qin' (Lute), Wang Xun matched 'Che' (Carriage), and Nanxiang matched 'Chuan' (Boat). Only Gao Pin remained silent. Wenze urged, "Any slower and you'll be fined wine." Gao Pin offered a wry smile and matched 'Zhou' (Boat). The game-master rewrote everything. The original line was: 'Twin phoenixes in the clouds support the carriage descending.' Zhongqing's match read: 'A solitary swan beyond the heavens sends a letter coming.' Everyone praised it. Gao Pin's match read: 'Eight chickens behind the dew lean on the boat's front.' The group took one look and couldn't hold back their laughter. Wenze said, "This is so bizarrely incoherent, not a single word connects. Even he gets stumped sometimes. How many cups should we fine him?" Nanxiang judged, "Only 'boat's front' barely connects; the rest is utter nonsense. Five cups, at the very least." Gao Pin just kept laughing, neither arguing nor drinking. The host pressed, "What will it be?" Gao Pin leaned over and whispered something to Zhongqing, sending him out of the room into the outer hall, roaring with laughter. Puzzled, Nanxiang hurried out to ask Zhongqing, who repeated it to him. Shi Nanxiang slapped the table in a fit of hysterical laughter. When Ziyu and the others questioned Gao Pin, he only laughed, saying, "Wait until you've seen everyone's lines, there's plenty of time." Wenze insisted, "You still have to drink the penalty." Gao Pin agreed, "Naturally." Zhongqing pulled Nanxiang back in, as Wenze muttered, "Who knows what kind of devilry he's concocting now." Hearing this, Nanxiang and Zhongqing burst into fresh bouts of laughter, tears streaming down their faces. Only after a servant wrung out a hot towel for them to wipe their faces did the laughter subside.`,`Next, they examined Ziyu's match: 'Nine chariots in the heavens listen to the lute coming.' Everyone praised it: "This matches perfectly character by character, and it surpasses Jiantan's." Thus, they offered a congratulatory toast. Nanxiang's match read: 'Three crows on the water send the boat returning.' Wenze critiqued, "Zhujun's match is a bit rough around the edges." Nanxiang shot back, "You're just an examiner with little experience who marvels at the ordinary—you should have your eyes gouged out! This is a perfectly crafted match." Ziyu agreed, "It truly is an excellent match." Wenze conceded, "I must ask for enlightenment on this one." Ziyu explained, "'Three crows on the water, one returning man' is a poem by Han Wo." Wenze suddenly realized, "Is it the poem 'Seeing Lord Wang of Xiangyuan Home to His Villa'? My memory is dreadful—I deserve a beating!" Nanxiang teased, "Luckily you remembered your mother's maiden name, otherwise you'd be fined ten cups." Finally, they looked at Wang Xun's match: 'Ten ravens in the sun drive the carriage returning.' Wang Xun admitted, "My match is ruined." Wenze noted, "It's just that the 'ten ravens' don't connect." Gao Pin argued, "You were wrong about the boat before, too. The sun contains the golden raven; in Emperor Yao's time, ten suns appeared at once. Are those not ten ravens?" Wenze countered, "That's a bit of a stretch, quite a forced argument." The group's consensus placed Ziyu first, Nanxiang second, Zhongqing third, Wang Xun fourth, and Gao Pin last. They would rotate as examiners based on this ranking.`,`Wenze reminded, "Zhuoran still hasn't drank his penalty wine. What on earth did he say to make you laugh like that? If it's truly clever, we might waive the fine." Nanxiang replied, "If he tells you, not only will the fine stand, it will be doubled." Wenze narrowed his eyes, "Is he mocking me again?" Zhongqing nodded, "Precisely." Wenze reasoned, "As long as the mockery has merit, the fine can be reduced." Gao Pin chimed in, "I suppose five cups are unavoidable. If you add more, I absolutely refuse. Better I say nothing." Wenze relented, "No additions, then." Gao Pin proposed, "Read my matching line backward—how does it sound?" Ziyu, Wang Xun, and Wenze silently read it to themselves, and soon found themselves clapping and roaring with laughter. Ziyu laughed so hard he collapsed onto the table, while Wang Xun leaned heavily against Nanxiang, setting off another wave of laughter from Nanxiang and Zhongqing.`,`Wenze declared, "Zhuoran, when you die, you are certainly destined for the Transmission of the Lamp Record's Hell of Pulled-Out Tongues." A servant poured the wine. Gao Pin negotiated, "Drinking five cups in one breath will surely leave me drunk. Perhaps if I play finger-guessing games with everyone, there's some hope of reducing the toll." He proceeded to play thumb-guessing with each person; winning some and losing others. Once the rounds were settled and the wine drunk, Ziyu took his turn as game-master, offering four characters for all to match: 'fei ying shou chang' (expend, shadow, withdraw, sorrow). Nanxiang matched 'jing sheng fang dan' (startle, sound, release, courage); Wang Xun matched 'rong xiang huan ru' (melt, fragrance, wash, milk); Wenze matched 'fan me xiao she' (flip, tiny-note, small, tongue); Zhongqing matched 'duo xian san fa' (many, immortals, loosened, hair); while Gao Pin, as distinctive as ever, offered 'chu yi fang fen' (remove, Yi, release, excrement)—already bringing smiles to their faces. Ziyu then put forth the character 'tai' (stage/terrace). Nanxiang noted, "This line has a fine beginning." After a moment's thought, he matched 'guan' (hall/lodge); Wang Xun matched 'wu' (room/house); Wenze matched 'xie' (waterside pavilion); Zhongqing matched 'dao' (island). Gao Pin quipped, "I live in Hongji Monastery, so I match 'si' (temple)." Ziyu then offered 'luan' (mythical phoenix-bird). Nanxiang remarked, "This character is even more unusual." Wang Xun quickly seized 'yan' (swallow); Zhongqing matched 'he' (crane). Nanxiang grumbled, "Not good—beaten to the punch. I refuse to use flying birds; I'll match 'shu' (rat/mouse)." Wenze pondered, "Could it be 'shadow of a luan-bird'? Under my 'me' (tiny/diminutive) character, what fits? Well, 'me' and 'niao' (bird) connect." Gao Pin added, "You matched 'niao,' so I'll also match 'niao.'" Ziyu presented 'wu' (dance). Nanxiang concluded, "It must be 'dancing luan-bird'—I can only match 'she' (archery/shoot)." Wenze grabbed 'ge' (song); Wang Xun matched 'hua' (glorious); Zhongqing matched 'yao' (jasper). Gao Pin asked, "Is 'Ba' (surname Ba) matchable?" Everyone laughed in unison, "As long as you're willing to drink the forfeits, what can't you match?" Ziyu revealed the results. The opening line was: 'The dance-stage withdrawing shadows—the luan-bird's sorrow expended.' Nanxiang sighed, "Ah—these are such familiar poetic images, yet they escaped me." Zhongqing observed, "Is the game-master still lost in thought?" Ziyu, busy recording Nanxiang's match, simply smiled without answering. They read Nanxiang's match: 'The archery-hall releasing its sound—the rat's courage is startled.' Everyone agreed, "A very fine match." Gao Pin teased, "He was dreaming of swan meat and didn't want to be frightened off." Nanxiang shot back, "Keep that glib mouth shut—I'll settle the score later." Next was Wang Xun's: 'The splendid house washing its fragrance—the swallow's milk blends and melts.' Ziyu had already marked it with double circles. The group praised it, "This line is beautifully harmonious," and drank a congratulatory toast. Wenze admitted, "I've clearly failed this examination." They read his match: 'The singing-hall's tiny note contains a bird-tongue.' Nanxiang allowed, "It does make sense." Gao Pin critiqued, "A tiny note in the singing-hall is perfectly coherent—it's just that the little note suffers rather harshly." Nanxiang asked, "Why?" Gao Pin explained, "With both a bird and a tongue, the two become indistinguishable; no wonder it cannot contain it all. The author must be well-practiced at such containment." Wenze retorted, "Nonsense—your 'excrement' match is likely no masterpiece either." Zhongqing's match read: 'Jasper island scattering immortals—many crane-feathered heads.' Ziyu had already awarded it partial marks, and the group praised it unanimously. Nanxiang remarked to Wang Xun, "I fear he may have stolen first place from us." Ziyu observed, "The writing reflects the man—these two matching lines perfectly suit their authors." Gao Pin declared, "Just cross mine out—no need to expose my failings." Nanxiang recalled, "I remember his was 'Ba si fang yi chu niao fen'—damnable, truly damnable. Who knows what nonsense he was spouting." Wenze gloated, "Amitabha! You who excel at mocking others, look at you now! Tell us, is there a single connecting character?" Ziyu examined it again and said, "Brothers, don't be too quick to dismiss it. Zhuoran's match has its own logic." Nanxiang reviewed it, nodded, and said, "Not bad—this man is truly incorrigible." Wenze asked, "Is there really some coherence to it?" Nanxiang replied, "The outrage is that it's not entirely incoherent." Gao Pin simply laughed, saying nothing. Wang Xun approached Zhongqing, asking, "Where does 'Ba si' (Ba Monastery) originate?" Zhongqing replied, "I recall the poet Dai Shulun wrote the line 'gazing at the monastery, passing through Ba Temple.'" Wang Xun accepted, "As long as there's a source, it suffices." Wenze pressed, "And the final five characters?" Zhongqing asked, "Do you have the Record of the Transmission of the Lamp here?" Wenze sent a literate book-boy to fetch it from the outer shelves. Zhongqing found the passage: "Grand Secretary Cui entered a temple and saw a bird defecating on the head of a Buddha image. He asked the monk, 'Do birds and sparrows possess Buddha-nature?' The monk replied, 'They do.' Cui asked, 'Then why do they defecate on the Buddha's head?' The monk answered, 'That is precisely why they do not defecate on the sparrowhawk's head.'" Zhongqing concluded, "Judged by this, the line holds." Wenze conceded, "It's just that 'fang yi' (releasing it) is hard to parse. If the 'niao' (bird) character were changed to 'que' (sparrow), it would be perfect."`,`Gao Pin argued, "My 'niao' (bird) and your 'que' (sparrow) are essentially identical. If your 'niao' were changed to 'que,' wouldn't that also be an improvement?" Wenze considered this and conceded the point. Ultimately, Ziyu accepted only Zhongqing's and Wang Xun's matching lines as passing; Wenze and Gao Pin were penalized with wine.`,`The turn passed to Nanxiang to set the game. He presented the character 'chun' (spring): Wenze matched 'xia' (summer), and Gao Pin matched 'zheng' (upright/primary). Wang Xun questioned, "Can a level-tone character be matched with another level-tone?" The group agreed, "Certainly—it has been done before." Wang Xun matched 'chen' (morning/dawn), Zhongqing matched 'qiu' (autumn), and Ziyu matched 'dong' (winter). Nanxiang then offered 'yue' (moon). Gao Pin noted, "Zhujun's thinking is always unconventional—these two characters surely won't connect logically, so I'll match 'yang' (sun/sunlight)." Wang Xun matched 'shuang' (frost), Ziyu matched 'xue' (snow), and Zhongqing matched 'kong' (empty sky). Wenze declared, "Never mind if they connect or not—let's just match our own," and offered 'yun' (cloud). Nanxiang presented 'san' (three). Gao Pin reasoned, "You see—it's not 'third month' but 'three springs.' If we all match 'yi' (one), they'll all connect." The group agreed, except for Wenze, who insisted, "I'll be different—I match 'ban' (half)." Nanxiang then put forth 'gai' (change/revise). Ziyu mused, "This character is quite unusual—I match 'qiao' (knock)." Wenze offered 'dui' (heap/pile), Wang Xun matched 'feng' (lush/abundant), and Zhongqing matched 'pan' (a turn/dish). Gao Pin casually tossed out 'fu' (prostrate/conceal). Finally, Nanxiang announced:`,`"'Tu' (rabbit). Match it, all of you." Wang Xun offered 'he' (badger). Zhongqing reasoned, "The eagle masters the rabbit—I match 'ying' (eagle)." Ziyu pondered, "A donkey-rider flying a hawk is likely unheard of, but I'll borrow it for the match—'lü' (donkey)." Wenze declared:`,`"'Wu' (raven)." Gao Pin countered, "I match 'gui' (tortoise)." Wenze replied:`,`"Ah, I see—I was unaware. I beg your distinguished pardon." The room erupted in laughter. Nanxiang teased, "This is your own self-incriminating confession. From now on, shouldn't we change your honorable name to Gao Gui (High Tortoise)?" Realizing his slip and unable to retract it, Gao Pin deflected, "These two characters are somewhat contrived—better to bestow them upon our elder brother instead. The two characters 'Shi Gui' actually form an ancient personal name, most refined and classical." Wenze noted, "Listen to Zhuoran's silver tongue—caught in his own trap, he immediately shifts the blame to someone else." The group shared a laugh before quietly waiting for Nanxiang to reveal the assembled couplet.`,`Nanxiang simply continued eating, remaining silent. Wenze prompted, "Why haven't you announced the couplet?" Nanxiang chuckled, "The examination papers have all been submitted—do we need more prompts? I gave the five characters in sequence: 'Spring moon three change rabbit.' Among the submissions, Qianzhou's 'Summer cloud half-stacked raven' borrows the 'raven' character quite effectively. Yet in pure composition, it cannot rival Jiantan's 'Autumn sky one-wheel eagle'—so pure and unrestrained; it deserves first place. Yuxiang's 'Winter snow one-knock donkey' and Yong'an's 'Morning frost one-lush sable' are both skillfully matched. The worst is Zhuoran's 'Upright-sun one-prostrate tortoise.' How can 'upright sun' (zheng yang) possibly apply to a tortoise?" Gao Pin laughed and asked Wenze, "Where exactly is your esteemed place of origin?"`,`Wenze groaned, "You dog! You truly make a man want to die of frustration—are you trying to retract your confession?" When the group realized what Gao Pin meant, they laughed uncontrollably. It turned out Wenze was a native of Zhengyang County in Henan—perfectly aligning with Gao Pin's line. Was it not an extraordinary coincidence? Wenze poured him another large cup of wine to vent his indignation.`,"Next, Zhongqing served as game-master, presenting six characters for everyone to match: 'si fa bai ri ru xin' (silk, hair, white, day, as, new). Gao Pin assembled: 'qian mao zhu tian ru chang' (signature, feather, vermilion, sky, enter, long). Ziyu matched: 'jing yan hua nian dui hao' (mirror, complexion, glorious, year, face, good). Nanxiang matched: 'zhu chun zhu sheng chui man' (bamboo, lip, crimson, sound, blow, slow). Wang Xun matched: 'jian yi wu shi shi fu' (scissors, garment, raven, time, try, brush). Wenze matched: 'cao ma huang chao qi shi' (grass, hemp, yellow, morning, rise, gaze). Zhongqing wrote the opening line: 'White hair like silk threads—renewing day by day.'",`He ranked Wenze's match—'Yellow hemp-cap rises—the morning court gazes day by day'—as first; Ziyu's match—'Glorious face against the mirror—year after year beautiful'—as second; and Nanxiang's match—'Crimson lips blow bamboo—note after note (Sheng Sheng Man)'—received circled marks and placed third. The group murmured, "Both of these matches are excellent—it seems Zhujun's is actually superior. The judge's ranking doesn't seem entirely fair." Zhongqing explained, "That both these submissions are exceptional goes without saying.`,`"On the surface, Zhujun's 'bamboo' matching 'silk,' and 'crimson lips' matching 'white hair,' is exquisitely crafted; 'Sheng Sheng Man' ingeniously conceals a ci tune title, seemingly placing it above Yuxiang's—which is why I awarded it circled marks.`,`"However, since the opening line is split character by character, each must match precisely. Yuxiang's use of 'year' to match 'day' is highly fitting; Zhujun's use of 'sound' to match 'day'—well, if 'day' is not reduplicated, or simply means 'broad daylight,' then 'crimson sound' becomes impossible to parse. Ultimately, it lacks the flawless balance of Yuxiang's, whose phrasing remains broadly elegant without descending into excessive cleverness. Do you gentlemen agree?" These words convinced everyone. Nanxiang, who rarely conceded, nodded in agreement. Gao Pin interjected, "But my use of 'sky' to match 'day' is even better than Yuxiang's—why was my submission ignored?" Zhongqing replied, "Write it out and explain it." He first wrote Wang Xun's: 'Raven-dark garment scissors—the raven-colored robe is tried and brushed each time.' The group agreed, "This line also flows quite naturally." Zhongqing concluded, "In this round, aside from Zhuoran, everyone else passes." Gao Pin laughed, "Watch closely—my submission cannot be crossed out." Zhongqing wrote it out: 'Vermilion feathers entering the sedge-hat—growing taller day by day.' Zhongqing ruthlessly crossed it out, sending the onlookers into uproarious laughter. Suppressing a chuckle, Nanxiang explained, "I understand his classical allusion. In ancient times, the Red Feather Nation's king conquered the Giant People's Nation and seized their lands.`,`"In the Giant People's Nation stood Sedge Hat City, the capital where their king resided. After the Red Feather king occupied the city for two days, he began to feel swollen, growing larger day by day." Nanxiang claimed this was the intended allusion, bursting into laughter as he spoke. Wang Xun, half-believing, asked, "What happened next?"`,`Nanxiang waved it off, "The allusion is rather long—enough has been said to serve the match." Wenze asked, "In what book is this recorded?" Zhongqing replied, "The Shi Clan Supplement." Wang Xun and Wenze finally understood, breaking into fresh peals of laughter. Gao Pin retorted, "You're all spouting nonsense—haven't you memorized the Four Books? This comes directly from Mencius's description of Yang Zhu—'who would not pull out a single hair'—chasing a young pig into a sedge-hat. Hence, 'vermilion feathers entering the sedge-hat.' Now that is true classical transformation!" Zhongqing pressed, "And how do you explain 'growing taller day by day'?" Gao Pin scoffed, "Your honor as game-master is truly obtuse. Since he refuses to pull out a single feather, naturally it grows longer day by day." The ensuing laughter was so thunderous that, had the rooms not been deeply secluded, passersby on the street would surely have heard. The host fined Gao Pin three cups of wine.`,"Wang Xun then took the mantle of game-master, presenting the opening line: 'Fragrance exhausted, Southern person fades—the beautiful country.' Wenze matched: 'Many tunes, Eastern entertainers compose mountain-names.' Zhongqing matched: 'Rhapsody difficulty, Eastern scholars, refined, capital learning.' Gao Pin matched: 'Combat long, Western sages, conveyed, region, equal-level.'","The group scrutinized Gao Pin's match, finding each character level, regular, and coherent. Wenze noted:",`"This time, Zhuoran is likely taking first place—every character matches with immense stability." Ziyu matched: 'Deep feeling, Western dan-performer, moves Kunlun-mountain fame.' Nanxiang matched: 'Many paintings, Western scholars, paint famous gardens.' As they finished, Wang Xun revealed the opening line: 'Fragrance fades in the Southern kingdom—the beauties are all gone.' Wenze's assembled match: 'Tunes composed of Eastern Mountain—many famous courtesans.' Zhongqing's: 'Rhapsodies forged in the Eastern Capital—difficult for scholars.' Gao Pin's: 'Contest-drive the Western region—the sages in long seniority.' Ziyu's: 'Deep feeling moves the Western Kun—famous dan-performer of profound sentiment.' Nanxiang's: 'Paintings of the Western Garden—many renowned scholars.' Wang Xun declared, "First place undoubtedly goes to Zhujun. As for Yuxiang, 'famous dan-performer' lacks classical gravity and is less seamless than Jiantan's—so second place likely falls to him, followed by Qianzhou. As for Zhuoran's line, I genuinely don't understand it. If it contains a classical allusion, please explain, lest you suffer an unjust mark."`,`Gao Pin replied, "I've never heard of a chief examiner asking candidates to explain their allusions. There is indeed an allusion, but if I reveal it, you'll accuse me of manipulating the exam." Zhongqing interjected, "His allusion stems from the Eastern Land of the Great Tang." Gao Pin shot back, "Jiantan is a close kinsman of the chief examiner—he should recuse himself and remain silent." (It transpired that Wang Xun had never read *Journey to the West*, leaving him staring blankly at the whiteboard.) Nanxiang was mid-drink when he noticed Gao Pin shading his eyes with his hand like a sun-visor,`,`glancing toward Wang Xun. Unable to suppress his mirth, Nanxiang choked on his wine, coughing and spluttering, setting the entire table off. Catching his breath, Nanxiang told Wang Xun, "Let me finish laughing, and then I'll read *Journey to the West* to you." Wenze stepped in to clarify, "It's the allusion of the Great Sage Equal to Heaven escorting the Tang Monk to the Western Heaven to retrieve the scriptures." Wang Xun finally understood, "How can that be? Even so, 'contest-drive' and 'long/seniority' still don't connect." Nanxiang smiled, "You don't realize—when Sun Xingzhe soars on his somersault-cloud, a single flip covers a hundred and eight thousand li. Is that not a long journey?"`,"The host moved to fine Gao Pin more wine; Gao Pin pleaded earnestly and ultimately drank only a single cup.",`Finally, it was Gao Pin's turn to lead. He rattled off six characters in a single breath: 'qian li yan zhao he kou' (thousand, li, speech, summon, grain, mouth). Zhongqing mused, "That's only seven characters total, yet he delivers six at once. Does he really think no one will see through it? He must be employing the character-decomposition method to confound us." He challenged, "Could your six characters form the three characters 'chong zhao he' (Repeat-Decree-Harmony)? If you don't clarify, we'll refuse to sit for the exam." Caught out, Gao Pin could only smile and nod. Ziyu matched with three characters: 'Zhuo yan guan' (Zhuo-speech-penetrating); Nanxiang matched: 'Pin yang chang' (Pin-sun-long); Wang Xun matched: 'Yi ling qing' (One-year-celebrate); Wenze matched: 'Pin jian dong' (Pin-wicked-move); Zhongqing matched: 'Guan hao ding' (Tube-fine hair-determined). Gao Pin then fired off four more characters: 'jiu xi qi feng' (nine, joy, spirit, phoenix).`,`Zhongqing declared, "This one clearly isn't character-decomposition. I'll match 'yi gao biao tu' (one-high-mark-rabbit)." Wenze followed, "I'll match 'yi huan xin ji' (one-joy-heart-rooster)." Wang Xun added, "I'll match 'di chang nian gui' (rank-long-year-tortoise)." Ziyu matched: 'chao yuan jing ren' (surpassing-primal-refined-person). Nanxiang matched: 'yi jing shen long' (one-spirit-dragon). Gao Pin turned his back, wrote out his opening line, set down his brush, and inspected everyone's submissions. With a quiet snort of amusement, he took a wine-dampened paper and wiped the whiteboard clean. The group was astonished:`,`"How extraordinary! Was not a single submission acceptable?" Nanxiang theorized, "No, no—if they were unacceptable, he'd have displayed them to subject everyone to ridicule. Let me ponder the characters he provided... assembled, what line do they form?" Zhongqing recalled, "While he was writing, I caught a glimpse of 'feng zhao' (phoenix-decree) at the beginning." Ziyu thought for a moment, "Could it be 'The phoenix-decree from the nine heavens brings harmonious joyful air'?" Nanxiang confirmed, "Exactly right." Gao Pin denied it, "No, no." Zhongqing suggested, "Let us each recount our own matching line; then it will become clear." Ziyu offered, "My line—'A person of speech who surpasses Zhuo and penetrates to the primal essence'—isn't particularly impressive, but it's not incoherent." Nanxiang deduced, "He rejected it because of his own courtesy name, Zhuoran—'Zhuo, primal essence' was intolerable to him." Zhongqing added, "Mine was 'A single rabbit-hair brush sets the highest standard (gao biao)'—surely rejected because 'tu gao' (rabbit-high) sounds like his name." Wang Xun remembered, "Mine was 'Tortoise longevity, foremost of all, celebrating the long years.'" Nanxiang praised, "An excellent match, clearly first place—so why was it rejected?" Wenze pointed out, "Don't you see him sitting up there in lofty first position?" Nanxiang nodded, "My match pointed even more directly at him." When asked why, Nanxiang revealed, "'The Longyang passion, first quality, ever vigorous in spirit.'" Wenze added, "Mine was even more explicit: 'Male debauchery, first quality, stirring joyful hearts.' How extraordinary—why did all our matches manage to mock him by name?" Nanxiang concluded, "This is the clear working of Heaven's justice—retribution for a glib and mocking tongue." Gao Pin insisted, "You're all guessing wildly. My opening line was nothing of the sort. Since your submissions were incoherent, I chose to spare you the embarrassment. I'm certainly not obliged to humiliate you." The group countered, "We candidates do not fear embarrassment—speak up!" Gao Pin pointed at the clock, "Look at the time. Haven't we had dinner yet?" They checked the hour; it was already past the second quarter of the last night-watch. Wenze demanded, "Is it or isn't it? Tell us, and then we'll eat." Gao Pin yielded, "Suppose it is—can I not keep a slight advantage for myself?" With that, they sat down to dinner. Once the meal concluded and they had washed, noting the deepness of the night, they bid farewell and departed.`,`As Ziyu walked out, he complimented his host, "This garden is tastefully arranged—quite secluded and refined." Wenze replied, "This barely qualifies as a garden, not even a tenth of Xu Duxiang's Yiyuan. Someday I'll take you there for a stroll." As host and guest conversed, Gao Pin asked Zhongqing, "Did you know another exceptional individual has recently arrived in the capital?" Zhongqing smiled, "I suppose it must be one of your brothers." Gao Pin clarified, "He could indeed be my brother. I hear he is also from Nanjing and currently lodging at Hongji Monastery, though we haven't interacted. From my observations, he appears quite refined and elegant, and his circumstances seem affluent. Do you know who he might be?" Zhongqing found this amusing, "How strange—you live in the same monastery, yet you don't recognize each other, and you come asking me." As they spoke, they reached the gate, boarded their carriages, and went their separate ways. This gathering of celebrated gentlemen had drawn two rather vulgar outsiders seeking amusement where none awaited them—they sought to make merry but found themselves left wanting.`,"But please await the following chapter."]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations7:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={8:[`Ziyu returned from drinking at Liu Wenze's home long past the second watch of the night. After paying his respects to his parents and changing his garments, he went to seek out Pincai and Yuanmao for a chat. Yet he found their quarters perfectly silent, the door closed fast. The servant boy Hu'er approached and said, "After Young Master went out, the private secretary was invited away and will not return tonight. Masters Li and Wei departed right after breakfast."`,`"Where could they have gone? To still be out at this hour!" Ziyu remarked, turning back inside. Now, once Ziyu had left the house earlier, Pincai and Yuanmao found themselves quite bored. Ever since the sixth day of the month when Yuanmao laid eyes upon those beautiful performers, his heart had been set on merrymaking. However, his father kept him under strict discipline, and his own pockets were empty, leaving him unable to fulfill his desires. He complained to Pincai, "Today you really must treat me to an opera." Pincai replied, "If I had the silver, would I not have invited you before you even asked?" Frowning, Yuanmao folded his arms in his sleeves and paced aimlessly for a while before declaring, "For just the two of us to listen to an opera, three hundred large cash would be plenty." Pincai sighed, "If it were merely three hundred cash, I could scrape it together. But to sit there coldly listening to a few arias holds little charm. Have you not seen the others, reclining on fine cushions in the premium seats, surrounded by a lively circle of performers, laughing and chatting in high spirits? Then, after the show, they bring those same performers to a fine restaurant to drink. You have yet to experience such a banquet—the intoxicating scent of cosmetics and powder mingling with wine and flowers, their voices sweet as swallows and orioles, their playful rebukes and enchanting smiles. Such delights never fail to bewitch the soul. In truth, the expense is quite modest, barely seven or eight strings of capital cash, which comes to only three or four mace of silver. Down south, that would not even cover a single table of such entertainment. I simply do not have those few strings of cash, so I cannot play the host." Hearing this, Yuanmao's heart burned with impatience. He pressed, "I have nothing left to pawn, but you still possess a few decent garments. Why not pawn them to treat me?"`,`"If I pawn them, I shall have nothing to wear," Pincai replied. Yuanmao urged, "Then borrow from the accounts room! You are quite friendly with the bookkeeper." Pincai shook his head. "How could I show my face? We have only just arrived a few days ago. To borrow money just to watch operas would make us laughingstocks." Yuanmao sighed, "Then we are at a loss. You will neither pawn nor borrow, so we must abandon the idea. I have no other solutions." Pincai countered, "You do have a solution, you simply refuse to use your silver." "I have silver?" Yuanmao protested. "I ran short on the journey, and no one has given me a single coin since arriving in the capital. Where would I get silver?" Pincai smiled knowingly. "Your honorable father's trunk must surely hold some. Why not borrow a few taels for the time being? We can use it now, and once I find a way, I will repay you exactly what we spent. You need not breathe a word to him." Yuanmao hesitated, "That might not end well. What if he questions me?" "If he never asks, all the better," Pincai reasoned. "If he does, merely say we borrowed travel funds from Ye Maolin on the road, and when he came to collect today, it would have been too embarrassing not to repay him at once." "That sounds plausible," Yuanmao admitted, "but since it was never mentioned before, he might doubt the tale." "Why should he?" Pincai pressed. "Just say you always assumed I had settled the debt, which is why it was never brought up." After a moment's thought, Yuanmao marched straight into his father's room. He unlocked the trunk and reached inside, feeling a heavy bundle that contained several dozen taels. Opening it, he found a multitude of small pieces and selected five or six. As he was about to tie the bundle closed, Pincai interjected, "For both the opera and the wine, we will need about twenty strings of cash. You had best take out two more pieces." Yuanmao obliged, selecting two more to total about eight or nine taels. He slipped the silver into a pouch and tucked it securely into his waistband, carefully rewrapped the remainder, and locked the trunk. After finishing their meal, they hired a carriage and, with his servant Sier carrying a riding blanket, hurried off to the theater.`,'As they approached, Yuanmao asked, "Which troupe shall we patronize?" Pincai answered without hesitation, "The Lianjin Troupe, naturally." Checking the playbills on the wall, they confirmed the Lianjin Troupe was performing at the Taihe Garden. Having frequented the area the previous year, Pincai knew the way well and led Yuanmao straight inside. Pincai walked briskly ahead. Yuanmao, bedazzled by the colorful decorations adorning the theater entrance—painted clouds, fierce tigers, and vibrant floral displays—gazed around in wonder, letting his feet carry him blindly forward. He failed to notice a low wooden bench lying across the threshold. Tripping heavily over it, he fell forward like an uprooted plant. Just as Sier rushed to help him, a bystander stepped forward, lifted Yuanmao by both arms, and brushed the dust from his robes. With a broad grin, the man chided, "Mind your step! That was no light fall. Fortunate I caught you so swiftly. If you had injured your shoulder or cracked your skull, your pursuit of pleasure would have found nothing but sorrow."',"Deeply embarrassed, Yuanmao muttered his thanks and hurried inside to find Pincai. They settled at a small table upstairs. The performance was already underway, with two acts concluded; they were currently staging 'Finding Gold.' Noticing it was not a play featuring young female roles, Yuanmao lost interest. He let his gaze wander in every direction, searching the hall. Yet not only were Qin Guan and the others nowhere to be seen, even Ye Maolin was absent from the stage.",`As he sat there listlessly, a man approached and nodded in his direction. Yuanmao found the face familiar but could not immediately place him. The man stepped behind Pincai, patted him on the shoulder, and declared, "In high spirits, I see!" Pincai turned, his face instantly lighting up with a wide smile as he hastily offered his seat. "Second Brother!" he exclaimed. "Are you alone, or did you come with company?" Zhang Zhongyu replied, "When do I ever have the leisure to watch operas? Early this morning I visited the Hua residence at Jinchun Garden, then went to Master Xu's place at Yiyuan to discuss a matter. Afterward, I dined at Tianxiang Pavilion with Third Brother Pan of the Qisheng Bank. Yesterday, Monk Tang of the Hongji Monastery arranged to meet me here on business." As he spoke, he produced a jade snuff bottle and handed it to Pincai, who accepted it graciously. Yuanmao suddenly recalled having met Zhongyu on the sixth day of the month, and they exchanged brief pleasantries. Zhongyu then offered the snuff bottle to Yuanmao. Not recognizing it, Yuanmao mistook it for medicinal powder meant to clear the head and inhaled deeply. Instantly, he fell into a fit of seven or eight violent sneezes, his eyes watering and nose running, sending Zhongyu and Pincai into peals of laughter. Zhongyu inquired about life at the Mei residence, and Pincai offered a casual reply. "My young friend," Zhongyu offered, "should you ever find yourself in need, do not hesitate to seek out your elder brother." Pincai thanked him. Zhongyu paid no mind to the stage, preferring to chat with Pincai. When Pincai brought up Qin Guan, Zhongyu remarked, "I have met the young man. His features are striking enough, though his temperament runs rather cold. These days he is constantly at Xu Duxiang's Yiyuan. There is also Qi Guan, who is somewhat more amiable." Pincai noted, "This Qin Guan is the one our Mei Yuxiang treasures above all." Zhongyu raised an eyebrow. "He fancies Qin Guan as well? I rarely see him out and about." Yuanmao sat in a daze, listening quietly, when a young performer approached. The youth offered a respectful greeting to Zhang Zhongyu, acknowledged Pincai, and offered a slight bow to Yuanmao. Yuanmao rubbed his eyes, focused his gaze, and realized it was the same performer he had seen at the guild hall days earlier—the very one Sun Sihui had praised so extravagantly. The performer seated himself beside Zhang Zhongyu, though Zhongyu's demeanor remained icy. Pincai turned to Zhongyu and asked:`,'"What is his name?" Before Zhongyu could answer, the performer eagerly chimed in, "My name is Erxi." He then turned to Pincai, "And your honorable surname?" Pincai provided his name. Erxi then addressed Yuanmao, "The other day at the Suzhou Guild Hall, you were conversing with Young Master Sun. Are the two of you well acquainted?"',`Yuanmao thought to himself, "This performer is exceedingly attentive. To remember me after a single meeting is rare indeed." And so, with his amber eyes fixed intently upon the young man, he observed him closely. Erxi unabashedly shifted closer, sharing the same bench as Yuanmao. "Do you often attend the theater?" he asked. "Which troupe do you favor?" Yuanmao mumbled a vague, noncommittal reply. Erxi deftly prepared a pinch of tobacco in Yuanmao's short pipe, lit it, and handed it over. Yuanmao was utterly captivated; his eyes grew increasingly misty and dewy, his heart fluttering wildly with a joy so profound it left his throat itching with unspoken words. The performer remained seated by his side, making no move to leave. The next play, 'Visiting the Courtyard,' commenced, and another performer approached, seating himself beside Zhang Zhongyu and lingering there. Pincai inquired after his name and learned it was Baozhu. On stage, the scene shifted to 'Female Ballad-Singing.' The moment the actor made his entrance, Pincai recognized Qi Guan. His attire was exquisitely alluring, exuding the delicate grace of a blossom kissed by morning dew or the moon reflected in a tranquil stream. A thunderous ovation erupted from both galleries. "That is Qi Guan," Zhongyu noted. Pincai smiled and nodded, "He has grown even more refined than last year." Yuanmao, however, could not catch a clear glimpse. Entranced by his conversation with Erxi and stealing glances at Baozhu, he had no attention left to spare for the stage. Seeing that Yuanmao was pleased with him, Baozhu attempted to sidle closer, but Erxi firmly blocked his path, refusing to yield any ground. Undeterred, Baozhu circled around and took a seat on Yuanmao's other side.`,"Flanked by two dark-complexioned performers, the timid, country-bred patron found himself entirely overwhelmed, unable to attend to both at once. Baozhu and Erxi vied for his attention, competing to prepare his pipe and pour his tea. One pressed warmly against his shoulder, while the other squeezed intimately against his leg. Li Yuanmao was in seventh heaven, his heart blossoming with absolute delight.","When Qi Guan concluded his performance, he exited the stage, removed his makeup, and paused at the edge of the curtain. Catching sight of Pincai, he offered a faint, graceful smile. Pincai returned the nod. He noted that Qi Guan was now arrayed in splendid furs, complete with fashionable boots and a stylish hat—a stark contrast to his former appearance. Pincai assumed the young man would surely come over to pay his respects. Indeed, Qi Guan disappeared into the dressing room, but after waiting a while, Pincai glanced up to find him seated in the opposite gallery. There, alongside the performer who had sung 'Inscribing the Song' a few days prior, Qi Guan was attentively serving two lavishly dressed gentlemen. Before long, the two men departed, taking the performers with them. Pincai felt a profound sense of disappointment. Just then, he heard Erxi ask Yuanmao:",`"Where shall we go today?" Unsure of what was being asked, Yuanmao simply nodded. Then Baozhu chimed in, "Which restaurant shall we visit today? Allow me to wait upon you." Yuanmao stammered, unable to form a coherent reply. Erxi pressed, "Only two or three establishments have opened today. If we delay, there may not be any tables left." Yuanmao thought to himself, "Both are delightful company, and it seems they both wish to come. But I fear the silver I brought will not suffice." He reasoned further, "If I give them twelve strings of cash, and we spend five or six on food and wine, it should be enough." Resolving this, he turned to Pincai, "Shall we go?" Baozhu immediately caught Yuanmao's hand and asked, "Which restaurant?" Pincai cast a glance at the two performers, not entirely pleased, but since Yuanmao was footing the bill, he was content to join the revelry. He turned to Zhongyu and invited him, "Second Brother, come along! Let us share a few cups." Zhongyu demurred, "You go ahead; I still have someone to wait for." Pincai urged, "Come with us! If they have not arrived by now, they likely will not come at all." With that, he took Zhongyu by the arm and led him downstairs, entirely forgetting to pay for their theater seats. The attendant rushed forward, grabbing hold of Sier. "Hold a moment! You haven't paid for your seats." Hearing this, Pincai stopped and looked at Yuanmao. Zhongyu waved his hand dismissively, "Put it on my account. Just tell the manager." The attendant nodded in agreement.`,`No sooner had they exited the theater than their two young companions fell into step behind them. Pincai asked Zhongyu, "Which restaurant is best?" Zhongyu replied, "The Chunyang Restaurant just ahead is quite excellent." A few short steps brought them inside, where the manager immediately rose to his feet. "Master Zhang, a happy New Year to you! May you rise in rank and prosper!" he called out, bowing deeply. Zhongyu offered a few polite pleasantries in return. They selected an elegant private room, with Zhongyu taking the seat of honor, Yuanmao the second, and Pincai the third. Erxi and Baozhu squeezed onto a single stool together. A waiter served tea and presented the menu. Zhongyu offered the choice to Yuanmao and Pincai, who deferred back to him. Zhongyu ordered tile-shaped fish and braised duck kidneys, while Pincai requested fried gizzards and ham. Baozhu asked for white clams with tofu and stir-fried shrimp, and Erxi requested sliced fish, braised offal, and yellow-braised pork. Yuanmao announced, "I am fond of chicken, so I shall have the chicken." The waiter and Erxi both chuckled at this. Two pots of wine, several plates of fruit, and an array of small appetizers were brought in, and everyone enjoyed a few cups. The first four dishes—fried gizzards, duck kidneys, ham, and sliced fish—were served. Pincai proposed a game of finger-guessing. Zhongyu gestured to Erxi, "You set the rules." Erxi announced, "Joy within joy, bitterness within bitterness. The first loss requires a song; the second, a joke; and the third, a 'skin-cup' drink shared mouth-to-mouth." Yuanmao quickly protested, "I am useless at all three!" Pincai laughed, "That will not do. In that case, you must play the first round!" Erxi poured three brimming cups and placed them before him. "Master Li, let us begin!" Yuanmao squinted, "Keep an eye on me, my vision is poor and I might make a mistake." He extended his hand, played a round with Erxi, and instantly lost. Zhongyu laughed, "A song, if you please." Yuanmao pleaded, "I truly cannot sing. I would rather drink an extra cup." Baozhu insisted, "If you agreed to sing, you must sing." Yuanmao downed a cup of wine and begged Baozhu to sing in his stead. Erxi declared, "A substitute singer must drink a penalty of ten cups!" Intimidated, Baozhu dared not comply. Yuanmao offered him a desperate bow, "Good fellow, please sing a verse for me. I do not know a single word of these things." Seeing his genuine distress, Baozhu relented and sang a delicate rendition of 'Silver Button Silk'.`,`The second round was played, and this time Erxi lost. Erxi began his penalty: "There was once a man hosting a banquet, but he had no money for wine. He placed an empty cup before his guest. The host said, 'Please, drink,' but the guest did not move.`,`"The host repeated, 'Please!' The guest replied, 'The wine has not yet been poured, what is there to drink?' The host leaned in, inspected the cup, and said, 'Ah, it seems this wine is utterly bone-dry. You had best drink it just as it is.'" With that, Erxi raised his own cup to Yuanmao's lips. Overjoyed, Yuanmao drained it in a single gulp. Zhongyu and Pincai erupted in cheers of "Bravo!" Baozhu added, "That joke was truly delightful." He then poured a cup of wine, brought it to Pincai's lips, and called out affectionately, "Godfather, drink this cup." Pincai, highly amused, drank it down.`,'Baozhu poured another cup, brought it to Zhongyu, and again called out, "Godfather!" Zhongyu drank it as well.',"The third round saw Yuanmao victorious. Erxi took a mouthful of wine, cradled Yuanmao's face in both hands, and delivered the wine mouth-to-mouth. Yuanmao was in raptures, though his face flushed with embarrassment. Just as he swallowed half the mouthful, he suddenly lowered his head and chuckled. The remaining wine shot forcefully out of his nostrils, looking for all the world like two streams of yellow urine, splattering messily across the table. His forehead tingling and burning, Yuanmao buried his face in Erxi's shoulder, unable to lift his head. Baozhu laughed so hard he lost his balance, slipping off his stool and onto the floor. Zhongyu laughed until he spilled wine all over himself, while Pincai clutched his aching sides.",`Laughing, Erxi patted Yuanmao's chest until he finally raised his head. His eyes closed, his mouth agape, his nostrils still tickling, he let out several loud sneezes. After a long pause, he finally asked, "What is so funny?" Seeing his bewildered state, the others broke into fresh fits of laughter before settling down to enjoy the meal.`,`Erxi then poured wine and engaged Zhang Zhongyu in a round. Zhongyu lost, and Yuanmao eagerly demanded a song. "That is simple enough," Zhongyu replied. He downed a cup of wine and sang a verse of the 'Horse-Head Tune,' drawing cheers of approval from the group. Zhongyu lost the second round as well, requiring a joke. Glancing up, he noticed a small shrine affixed to the wall, housing an image of Zhao Gongming, the God of Wealth, riding a black tiger. He turned to Erxi and said, "When you performers encounter a wealthy patron, you rejoice, 'The God of Wealth has arrived, ready to spend!' But when a poor patron sees a dark-complexioned performer, he panics, 'A tiger is here, ready to devour anyone it meets!' Look up there—is that the God of Wealth riding a black tiger, or a destitute patron straddling a dark performer?" Pincai slammed the table in absolute delight, Yuanmao pinched his nose to stifle his laughter, while Baozhu tipped his head back to study the shrine. Erxi poured a cup of wine and presented it to Zhongyu, declaring, "A penalty for you! Your mockery is far too sharp." Zhongyu accepted the cup and drank, remarking, "Fortunately, there are no poor patrons terrified of performers here today." They played the third round, and Erxi lost, obliging him to offer Zhongyu a skin-cup. Zhongyu waved it off, "Let us skip that. Master Li did not properly enjoy his earlier cup; I ask you to pass this one to him instead." Erxi took a sip from the cup and offered it to Yuanmao, but Yuanmao shook his head vigorously, clamping his mouth shut in refusal. Undeterred, Erxi boldly straddled Yuanmao's lap. Sitting upright, he firmly grasped Yuanmao's head and tilted it back, forcing his face upward. Erxi pressed his delicate crimson lips tightly against Yuanmao's broad mouth, slowly and deliberately passing the wine. He did this three times in succession.`,"Yuanmao felt as though heavenly nectar was being poured over his crown; his joy was beyond words. The others listened as he audibly gulped three distinct times. Erxi poured more wine, and it was Pincai's turn. Erxi lost the first round and sang a verse of the 'Nine Linked Rings'.",`Pincai lost the second round. With a sly grin, he began, "There were once two sisters-in-law. With the older brother away from home, the younger sister shared a bed with the older sister-in-law. Missing her husband terribly, the sister-in-law could not sleep. She asked the younger girl to mimic her brother's actions and lay atop her for a while. The sister-in-law was thrilled, sighing, 'It is wonderful, but you are not quite skilled at it—you are leaking water.' The younger girl replied, 'It is my first time! I will be an expert the second time around. We ought to give this a name.' The sister-in-law pondered, 'There is already a name for this—it is called grinding mirrors.' The girl argued, 'That doesn't fit. Mirrors are round. Let's just call it offering a skin-cup instead!'" This joke provoked roars of laughter. Tears streaming from his eyes, Yuanmao scolded, "You wicked man! You will surely be struck dumb by tomorrow!" Baozhu laughed until he collapsed into Pincai's lap. Erxi marched over and slapped Pincai lightly on the arm. "Is there anyone more wicked than you? Your insults cut straight to the bone!" By some twist of fate, Erxi lost the third round. Holding the wine, he asked, "How shall I sing it? You command." Feigning sternness, Pincai replied, "You listened to Master Zhang, but you ignore my words. You look down on me, and I will not stand for it today." Alarmed, Erxi protested, "I have done nothing to offend you." Pincai countered, "You may not have offended me, but you must obey my instructions." "Speak then," Erxi yielded. Pincai declared, "I decree that this skin-cup must also go to Master Li." Erxi obediently aimed the wine at Yuanmao, who protested, "Enough! You have made me your plaything all day. I will not fall for it again." Zhongyu teased, "Eldest Brother Li, if you refuse this cup, I shall invent another joke to mock you." Pincai spat in mock disgust, "Bah! A silver spearhead made of wax—so useless! One word and he loses his nerve." Yuanmao reasoned internally, "I cannot out-talk them. Whatever, there is no hardship in the world so long as one possesses a thick skin. Taking the advantage is always best." He boldly proclaimed, "I am not like the rest of you, paralyzed by bashfulness. Come, come, come! Watch me drink it again." Cupping Erxi's face in his own hands, he drank the cup. No one could mock him for that. With Erxi's penalty complete, Baozhu played a round with Yuanmao, lost, and sang a rendition of 'The River Runs Red'.`,`Suddenly, Pincai noticed a monk entering the room. "My Second Master!" the monk exclaimed. "Here you are! I have scoured seven or eight theaters looking for you!" Seeing the monk, Erxi and Baozhu both offered respectful greetings, while Pincai and Yuanmao rose to acknowledge him. The monk returned their bows and took a seat beside Zhongyu. Pincai appraised the newcomer: a square face the color of dark sugar, two thick mustaches, perhaps forty years of age. He wore a velvet monk's cap and a sapphire-blue silk robe lined with fox fur, tied at the waist with a yellow silk sash. On his feet were cloud-patterned black satin boots lined with fur. He bore absolutely no resemblance to an ascetic; he was undoubtedly a monk well-acquainted with wine and meat.`,`Yet, his demeanor was remarkably pleasant and accommodating. After inquiring after Pincai and Yuanmao's names and lodgings, he turned to Zhongyu. "Second Master, when tomorrow's affairs are concluded, we shall gather at either the Suzhou Guild Hall or the Tianqing Hall. Bring your two esteemed friends and these two young performers, and we shall enjoy a grand day of revelry. I truly cannot linger today; everyone has already assembled and is merely waiting on you to finalize the matter." Zhongyu replied evenly, "No need to rush. Have a cup, and we shall depart together." The monk stroked his mustache, grinning broadly as he downed a cup of wine and helped himself to a slice of ham.`,`Baozhu smiled playfully. "Master Tang, did you not bring your young son with you?" Monk Tang barked a laugh. "Preposterous! A monk doesn't even have a wife, where would I get a son?" Erxi retorted, "Your 'son' looks just like a wife anyway." Tang reached out to pinch Erxi's cheek. Erxi dodged, laughing, "I only said he looks like a wife, I wasn't wrong!" Noting Pincai and Yuanmao's presence, the monk feigned scholarly dignity, withdrawing his hand. "To mock a disciple of the Buddha is a grave sin," he chided. Zhongyu and Pincai burst into laughter. Tang again urged Zhongyu to leave. "A brief delay will do no harm," Zhongyu replied. Erxi pointed to a gourd hanging on the wall and asked Tang, "What does that resemble?" Tang chuckled, "It looks just like your mouth." Erxi shook his head. "Illogical! Why my mouth? It clearly resembles your bald pate—smooth and shiny, without a single hair." The monk laughed heartily. "It is naturally smooth. Have you not heard? Heaven has three lights, but the mortal realm has four: a monk's shining pate, a bride's smooth thighs, a patron's emptied silver pouch, and a performer's ravenous mouth. A monk's head is shaved bare, a bride's thighs are worn bare, a patron's pouch is spent bare, and a performer's mouth eats everything bare!" Roaring with laughter, he hauled Zhongyu to his feet. Offering a deep bow to Pincai, he smiled, "I speak nonsense; please do not take offense." Zhongyu said, "Let me settle the bill before we go." Pincai insisted, "Since you have business, please go ahead. I am the host today." Zhongyu replied, "Enjoy a few more cups, both of you. I will return shortly." With that, he took his leave, and the two departed.`,`Pincai and Yuanmao played another round of games with Baozhu, who offered two more skin-cups. Erxi ordered several more dishes, and the revelry continued for some time, well past the lighting of the candles, deep into the first watch of the night. The two performers were growing weary, and the two servants stood waiting by the wind-break door. Utterly disoriented, having consumed vast quantities of hot soup and wine, Li Yuanmao removed his hat. Steam rose thickly from his head, as if he were a bamboo steamer. "It is time we dispersed," Pincai suggested. He leaned close to Yuanmao's ear and whispered, "Take out the silver and settle the bill at the counter." Yuanmao patted his waist, a look of sudden panic crossing his face. "How strange!" he muttered. He stood up, lifted the back of his robes, and turned to Pincai. "Do you see it?" Pincai asked, "See what?" "The pouch." "It is not there." Yuanmao's face instantly went blank. "This is bizarre. Where could it have gone?" Baozhu asked, "What have you lost?" Ignoring him, Yuanmao frantically searched his inner robes, finding nothing. The blood began to drain from his face in waves. He undid his sash and shook his clothes, but the pouch was gone. Pincai grew anxious, "Do not tell me you forgot it." Yuanmao snapped, "Nonsense! You saw me wearing it!" He lifted his robe again, desperately feeling around his trouser waistband, but found nothing. Pincai pulled Yuanmao to the window; noting the servants were still present, they stepped out into the courtyard. "This is disastrous!" Pincai whispered fiercely. "Think—where could you have lost it?" The question jolted Yuanmao's memory. "Ah! Now I know. When I entered the theater, I tripped and fell. Someone helped me up and brushed the dust from my clothes. That man must have stolen it." Pincai frowned, "I didn't see you fall. When was this?" Yuanmao explained, "There was a low bench lying across the entrance. I wasn't looking and fell the moment I stepped inside." As quick-witted as Pincai was, he could think of no solution.`,`Erxi stepped out and asked, "What are you two discussing out here?" They returned inside and took their seats. "It is growing late," Erxi noted. He leaned intimately into Yuanmao's ear and whispered, "Come back to my place; I will take good care of you." Hearing this, Yuanmao felt a surge of joy mixed with intense panic, his face burning hot. He could only stare blankly at Pincai. Baozhu and Erxi were utterly bewildered by his behavior. Left with no alternative, Pincai finally spoke up, "Now that the silver has been lost, what are we to do?" Yuanmao added miserably, "There were other things in that pouch—it was all taken together." "What happened?" Baozhu demanded. Yuanmao explained, "The silver... at the theater entrance, a cutpurse made off with it." Erxi frowned. "I walked out with you. I saw no cutpurse." "It happened on the way in," Yuanmao clarified. Erxi's eyes narrowed. "It happened on the way in, yet you sat through the entire opera and drank with us for hours without noticing? And only now, when it is time to leave, do you mention it? Is it not more likely you simply forgot to bring it, and it remains at home?" Yuanmao bristled, "That is an outrage! Do you think I am making excuses?" Erxi offered a cold laugh. Pincai interceded hastily, "That is not what we mean. It is not as if we came here penniless, plotting to cheat you. Master Li was careless and lost his silver; naturally, it was difficult to admit at the time. Trust us—tomorrow, when we come to the theater, we will bring everything we owe, including Baozhu's share." He turned pleadingly to Baozhu, "Do you believe us?"`,'Baozhu replied, "I have no particular reason to doubt you. After all, this is our first meeting; you would hardly set out to deliberately cheat us. But when we return, we must present our accounts to our master. And during the New Year, returning empty-handed is especially difficult. Not only would we struggle to face our master, but it would be deeply unfair to the boys who attended us all day."',`"We only ask that you find some way to spare us this hardship," Baozhu pleaded. He turned to Erxi, "Brother Xi, is that not fair?" Yuanmao insisted, "I am telling you the truth, yet you refuse to believe me! I had eight pieces of silver today, well over ten taels, loose in a pouch, and the thief took the pouch and all. To ask us to borrow money at this hour—where would we even go? You are putting us in an impossible position." Erxi scoffed, "It is not that late. Why not send your servant back to fetch more? We can wait here a while." He firmly pressed Yuanmao back into his seat. Yuanmao shook his head vehemently. "That is absolutely out of the question." Erxi's voice hardened. "If it is out of the question, then this was your plan from the start. We have spent the entire day in the company of strangers, serving wine and offering skin-cups. If you consider that a sufficient display of friendship, Master Li, then fine. But we, too, are driven by necessity—we must please our patrons, earn a few strings of cash, and maintain our standing before our master. If things always went this way, we would starve. Master Li, since you refuse to send someone home, I have one request. Please be so kind as to accompany me back and explain the situation to my master yourself. Whether you give us anything is irrelevant." Baozhu agreed, "What he says is perfectly reasonable. As long as our master hears the truth from you, that is all we ask. We do not need the money." Li Yuanmao was reduced to absolute helplessness. His face flushed a deep crimson, and he could not utter a single word. Pincai had no choice but to intervene. "We have only just made each other's acquaintance. Is this to be our first and last meeting? You ask him to face your master—your master would likely accept the explanation, but I have never heard of a performer dragging a patron before his master to settle an account." Baozhu replied coolly, "That is only because we are strangers. Once you have been our patron for years and we are well-acquainted, such measures would be unnecessary." The two sides were locked in a tense impasse. Suddenly, Sier rushed in and announced, "Young Master Sun is here! He just stepped out." Pincai saw an immediate lifeline. Knowing Sihui was familiar with these performers, he ordered, "Go quickly and invite him in!" Sier hurried out. Sihui was still at the counter, chatting with a performer who had just boarded a carriage to leave. Though he barely recognized Sier, hearing someone call for him, he returned inside, recognizing Yuanmao and Pincai. Spotting Erxi and Baozhu, he beamed, "My two gentlemen! What joyous merrymaking is this?" Yuanmao and Pincai offered respectful bows, while Erxi and Baozhu paid their respects. Once everyone was seated, Pincai quickly explained the predicament—how Yuanmao had lost his silver, had no means to pay, promised to settle the debt tomorrow, and how the performers stubbornly refused the offer. Sihui pushed back his hat, rubbed his reddened nose, pointed directly at Li Yuanmao, and launched into a grandiose, improvised recitation stringing together obscure phrases from the *Thousand Character Classic*. He proclaimed how Yuanmao's pouch of 'gold born of beautiful waters' had become like 'fallen leaves adrift in the wind.' Instead of venturing forth to 'execute thieves and robbers,' he sat here idly 'dispersing his cares in ease.' Assuming Sihui to be an 'old acquaintance,' he urgently summoned him 'to requite gladness and joy.' And seeing the two performers in their 'vigorous and resplendent' state, something deep within Sihui had suddenly 'pushed forth new shoots.' He prattled on through a bizarre parade of classical idioms, culminating in scandalous suggestions of what Yuanmao might do with the performers to ensure everyone's profound satisfaction. Wei Pincai and Li Yuanmao were already doubled over in hysterics. The two performers could scarcely follow the convoluted classical allusions, assuming he was merely reciting lines from a play, yet they, too, laughed uncontrollably. Yuanmao sputtered incoherently, unable to form a defense. Pincai, left with no alternative, finally stated he would personally assume the debt and settle with them the following day.`,`Hearing this, Sihui feigned profound shock and quoted dramatically, "'One whose strength cannot lift a hundred jun cannot bear a heavy burden on a long road'—I fear I am unequal to the task!" Pincai pleaded with him at length until Sihui finally, reluctantly, agreed. "What can be given tomorrow shall be given," he intoned solemnly. "'A man without integrity, who knows what becomes of him?'" He then turned to Erxi and Baozhu. "Come, let me speak plainly with you. Why not take your leave now? Tomorrow I shall personally deliver the funds to your door. Do not press people too harshly." Finding his classical rhetoric incomprehensible, Sihui was forced to repeat his guarantee in simple vernacular. Seeing that Sihui had formally assumed responsibility, Baozhu and Erxi were left with no further grounds for complaint. They made a reluctant show of gratitude and departed. Terrified they might also ask him to settle the restaurant bill, Sun Sihui practically sprinted out the door.`,'The waiter entered and read out the bill item by item, which, including the staff gratuity, totaled fifty-six strings and seven hundred eighty copper cash. Hearing the sum, Yuanmao stuck out his tongue in shock. "What discount can you offer?" he asked. "Full price, sir. No discounts," the waiter replied crisply. Counting on his fingers, Li Yuanmao calculated, "At a strict exchange rate, that is five thousand six hundred seventy-eight capital coins—exactly two thousand eight hundred thirty-nine hard-cash coins. Exceedingly fair! I shall certainly patronize your establishment frequently." The waiter chuckled, "We do not offer discounts. The total is fifty-six strings and seven hundred eighty copper cash." "How can it be so much?" Yuanmao protested. "We dare not overcharge," the waiter replied simply. Pincai nudged Yuanmao, "You are drunk. Stop talking nonsense. Let us go settle the bill at the counter." They approached the front desk, where the waiter recited the total once more. The manager clicked his abacus beads, then looked sharply at Pincai and Yuanmao. "You two arrived with Master Zhang, yet he has already departed. Are you his fellow provincials, or something else?" Pincai replied smoothly, "We are relatives. He had pressing business and left early." The manager pressed, "May I ask your honorable names? Where are you lodging? What business brings you to the capital?" Pincai provided brief answers, then requested a credit slip. The manager hesitated visibly. "Money is exceptionally tight during the New Year. Today is our very first day of business, and custom dictates we hope for a few cash payments in the first month. Furthermore, today is the very first time we have had the pleasure of your company."',`"However, since Master Wei is a relative of Master Zhang, it would be deeply awkward for me to refuse you a credit account. But I beg of you—do not let this drag on too long." He handed a slip to Pincai. Seething with inward indignation, Pincai wrote out the full amount, added two strings for the wine service, and signed it: 'Mingke Alley, Mei Residence, Wei surname.'`,"The manager glanced at the slip and filed it away. A waiter brought out a lit lantern, which Sier accepted, and they exited the restaurant. The two men trudged home step by step, their heads bowed in heavy silence. It could truly be said: they arrived riding the crest of high spirits, only to depart mired in bitter deflation. But as for what unfolded next, please await the following chapter."]},w=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations8:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={9:["When Wei Pincai and Li Yuanmao arrived home, it was past the third night-watch. The Mei household had already barred and bolted its gates. Fourth Boy knocked for a long while before anyone came to open them. The two entered their rooms, and Pincai could not help voicing a few reproaches at Yuanmao for carelessly losing the silver. Yuanmao had no answer. They each went to sleep. The next day, they had no choice but to prevail upon Xu Shun to borrow a ten-string promissory note, which they divided into two drafts. Wrapping them with a letter, they had Fourth Boy deliver the money to Ye Maolin to distribute between Erxi and Baozhu. Later, when Ziyu inquired, Pincai and Yuanmao simply claimed that Zhang Zhongyu had treated them to opera and a restaurant, keeping the truth concealed.",`Two days later came the Lantern Festival. Li Xingquan took Yuanmao to the year-end banquet at the guild hall, while Pincai had gone out to view the lanterns and had not yet returned. Ziyu sat alone with nothing to do when Mei Jinjin came in and announced: "Young Master Liu, Young Master Yan, and Young Master Wang invite you to go out and view the lanterns. They are all waiting at the gate." Ziyu reported to his parents, Mei Jinjin had the carriage harnessed, and Yun'er followed along. Zhongqing and the others were waiting in the carriage, stepping down when they saw Ziyu emerge. Liu Wenze said: "On such a fine night, worth a thousand gold pieces, let us take advantage of the lantern-lit moon and walk. That would be much better. We can have the carriage follow behind so we can ride back." Ziyu agreed. The four strolled slowly, chatting casually along the way, and in no time arrived at the lantern market.`,"The moment they entered the lantern arbors, they were engulfed in a sea of people, with carriages and horses jostling among the crowds. Ziyu and the others strolled slowly along the shop verandahs. Every shop was hung with festive lanterns and colored streamers. There were glass lanterns, painted gauze lanterns, elaborate screens displayed inside, and lantern towers erected outside the doors, while vendors set out their wares in dense, layered rows. Fortunately the streets were broad, for otherwise one could not have moved a single step. At the doorways, people set off firecrackers and rockets: shooting stars chased the moon, nine dragons played with a pearl, and fire trees bloomed with silver flowers. Mingled with gongs, drums, silk strings, and wind instruments, it was a scene of perfect peace and abundance. Everyone was in high spirits celebrating the Lantern Festival. A procession of fine carriages and ornate sedans passed by with their curtains open. Maids and servants sat on the carriage edges, burning superior incense, while the ladies inside gazed left and right through large glass windows. Wenze, Wang Xun, and the others watched attentively. Though they varied in beauty, the women's festive dress and jewels under the lamplight added radiant layers of color. As the four friends walked, they could not stay abreast, with some falling ahead and others lingering behind. After perhaps seven or eight carriages had passed, a few more pulled up to join the front of the procession, jamming the thoroughfare so it could not move.","At that moment Ziyu was in the lead and had just been blocked by a carriage axle, unable to pass. Wenze noticed a young woman inside the carriage, quite handsome and exquisitely dressed. Ziyu happened to be squeezed against the front of the carriage. Wenze saw the young woman's eyes fixed unblinkingly on Ziyu, while Ziyu had lowered his head with nowhere to retreat. He watched as the young woman placed one hand on the carriage door and leaned forward, extending one foot. It was a three-inch lotus hook, slender and delicate enough to fit in a palm. She first arranged her opposite leg, then drew the lotus foot back in and sat properly, lowering her slender hand. Turning toward Ziyu, she offered a sweet, gentle smile.",`Wenze tugged at Wang Xun's sleeve and murmured softly: "Look, it seems she is taken with Yuanxiang and wishes to show off her lotus feet." Wang Xun nodded. Zhongqing, standing behind Wenze, added: "How do you know it is not you she fancies?" Wenze laughed: "I do not think so." Then he called quietly: "Yuanxiang, what is there to look at in 'Cases of Magistrate Shi'? You keep staring at those pairs of lanterns." Ziyu turned his face around, only to meet the young woman's gaze directly. Seeing she was still watching him from inside the glass window, he felt quite awkward.`,"After a moment the carriage moved on. Wenze noticed that hanging from the carriage side-panel was a small foreign-style lantern painted with two ruyi scepters. Four small characters on its face read: Qisheng Hao Pan, marking the Pan family of the Qisheng trading house. Behind it followed another carriage.",`In this carriage too was a young woman, yet she was strikingly ugly. Her face was a mass of dark, fleshy features, plastered with powder that showed a purplish, greasy sheen even in the night. Her dress, however, featured every fashionable variety, and she was making affected gestures inside the carriage, fluttering her silken sleeve-cloth. Wenze noticed that a character on her lantern read 'Hua', meaning Flower. The carriage moved and passed on. The four wandered to a few more spots, where the streets began to narrow again. Wenze said to Ziyu: "Just now that young woman was so attentive in her glancing at you, yet you gave her no response and kept looking at that old gauze lantern. What is the meaning of this? Could a young woman like that not merit even one glance in return?"`,'Ziyu laughed: "I paid no attention to her, nor did she look at me. She was sizing you gentlemen up." The four friends bantered and laughed as they moved on to admire a few more lantern displays.',"Just then a crowd of women came rushing toward them on foot in a disorderly cluster. The four men counted some ten or more of them, some in silk robes and some in plain cotton. With oiled hair and powdered faces, they giggled and chatted, their sleeves fluttering like frantic butterflies threading among blossoms, their figures darting like startled snakes from the grass. They showed no concern for whether others might give way and came barging straight through. One of them, apparently with natural feet, planted a foot squarely on Wang Xun's boot.",`Wang Xun's new black boot had its side smudged, and his foot was trodden on with considerable force. He reckoned the blow carried at least thirty catties of weight, but he could say nothing and quickly stepped aside. Then he saw a woman of some thirty years, of considerable height, wearing high-platform shoes and looking up at the lanterns. Her foot caught on a brick, and she lurched to one side, nearly toppling over. She happened to grab onto Ziyu's chest, clutching it tight to steady herself. Ziyu nearly fell as well, his heart leaping in fright, utterly bewildered as to what was happening. The woman let go and giggled as the whole crowd squeezed past. He heard one of the women say: "These gentlemen are truly infuriating. They keep their eyes wide open staring at people. Can it be there are no women at home, that they block the road on purpose and will not let anyone pass?" Zhongqing and the others heard this and burst out laughing. Wang Xun said: "What rotten luck. That woman's foot hurt me badly, and she still says we blocked the road to stare at her." Ziyu had just recovered his composure and said: "Just now when she grabbed me I was genuinely frightened. I thought she had mistaken me for someone and was about to start a brawl. Is that not bad luck? Who would have imagined that among women there could be such uncouth creatures? Comparing them to the lady we saw in the carriage just now, there is truly a difference of heaven and earth." Wenze laughed loudly: "Without climbing a high mountain, you do not see flat ground. So you have been harboring private thoughts all along, silently taking the measure of things. That young woman in the carriage earned this praise from you, which is not a bad return for all her attentive glancing." Ziyu smiled faintly and said: "These lanterns have nothing much to see, and the road is hard to walk. Why not take the carriages back?" Wang Xun replied: "It is too early, and going back serves no purpose either." Wenze said: "Let us go to Yiyuan to see the lanterns there. I hear they have fine lantern riddles too, and going to guess a few would be amusing." Ziyu said: "I do not know the host, and since it is evening and we are in plain clothes, how can we go?" Zhongqing said: "That is no obstacle. Xu Duxiang is one of our sort. He places no importance on formalities or appearances. Moreover, his garden also has Xiao Jingyi, who is an even more upright and free-spirited person. Going for a stroll would be perfectly fine." All three wished to go, and Ziyu agreed to join them. So everyone got into their carriages, with the pages riding on the footboards, and headed for Yiyuan.`,"After about two li they passed Nanheng Street and alighted at the gate of Yiyuan garden. A long wall of tiger-stripe stone, made of rough yellow fragments cemented together, enclosed the estate. The garden gate was framed by a five-colored ceremonial arch built of bolts of silk, with only a square opening of some five feet displaying the two large characters 'Yiyuan'. Below hung four consecutive rows of eight five-colored glass lanterns painted with flowers. Through the garden gate was a set of eight oil-green gold-speckled screen doors. Beside the gate stood a table surrounded by six or seven people writing out lantern riddle strips. Beside it was a long bench with sachets, firecrackers, and the four treasures of the study arranged on it, prepared as prizes for correct answers. In the very center of the overhead canopy hung a five-colored brocade pleated cloud-pavilion, from which was suspended a gourd-shaped glass lantern.",`Further inside was a square pavilion built against the wall, surrounded by railings on three sides. A flat rectangular glass lantern hung above the pond, covered with strips of paper, while below it a cluster of some twenty people had gathered. Climbing the pavilion steps, they found eight lantern riddles written out facing them. Just as Zhongqing was about to read them, a Yiyuan servant came up to greet them and said: "Why not come inside to look around?" Wenze asked about the host, and the man replied: "Our master is out at a dinner and has not returned, but Master Xiao is at home." Wang Xun said: "Let us guess a few lantern riddles first and then go in. There is no hurry." So they all looked at the first riddle: 'Roosting together, stable and undisturbed, recognizing the Lu family's tortoiseshell rafters.' Below was a notation indicating a line from the Liji. Ziyu was pondering it when he heard Wang Xun ask Zhongqing: "Could this be: 'knowing that one can dwell in peace, like the swallow who is settled and orderly'?" Zhongqing replied: "I believe so." They looked at the second: 'Though ten thousand waters and a thousand mountains separate us, the wild goose's letter and the fish's message always arrive.' The notation indicated a line from the Book of Changes. Zhongqing said: "This must truly be: 'proceeding through danger yet not losing one's integrity.'" Ziyu said: "That fourth one, 'fallen blossoms, one person standing alone; fine rain, swallows flying in pairs', must be the character for 'liang', meaning both or together." Wenze said: "This seventh one: 'In the desolate village, rain and dew, sleep comes early; at the roadside inn, wind and frost, rising must be late.' These two lines on an ancient person's name must be 'Xi Fugong'." Ziyu agreed. Wang Xun said: "Let us go submit our answers." Zhongqing said: "Let us simply solve all four remaining ones too, then submit. There is no hurry. That second one, 'A crow's back lit by the setting sun', is a line from the Liji; it must be: 'the sun is in the Wing constellation.'" Ziyu said: "That seven-character regulated verse on eight titles of ancient Music Bureau songs. The first couplet: 'I remember how at your home, morning and evening, the Qinhuai River wound its fragrant banks in several bends.' The answer must be 'Midnight Song' and 'Jinling Song.'" Zhongqing said: "The lower line of the second couplet, 'the moon's shadow particularly dislikes the dark and dusty world,' is 'Night Yellow'. But what of the upper line, 'Let not the thread of rain urge the flower-petals along'?"`,`Wenze said: "Perhaps it is 'Do Not Wash the Red'. The third couplet, 'Through the long night I hear the broken water-clock dripping; in middle age I pour out my heart and yet labor in vain', must be 'Fifth-Watch Bell' and 'Mochou's Joy'." Wang Xun said:`,`"The seventh line, 'Thirty-six pairs of crows fly in steady formation,' is unmistakably 'The Crow Has Eight or Nine Young'." Zhongqing said: "The last line, 'She should send the distant traveler on the road to Zhangtai,' must be 'Breaking the Willow'. As for the fifth entry, 'Born in the years of chen and si', based on a line from the Classic of Poetry, and the eighth entry, 'Not a word written, yet the style is perfect', on a Tang poetry line, I cannot solve those." Just then someone was heard asking: "'Born in the years of chen and si', could that be 'Where there are vipers and serpents'?" The man at the garden gate replied it was not. Wenze said: "Do not let others take it from us. Let us go submit." Everyone descended the pavilion steps. Ziyu said: "As for that Classic of Poetry one, I have thought of it. It must be 'not belonging to the fur-bearing creatures.'" Zhongqing said: "Precisely right. That answer was truly remarkable to have thought of."`,`Wang Xun said: "The one on the Tang poetry line, could it not be 'Yin Zi writing in the air'?" Wenze replied: "Let us go and try submitting it." Everyone went to the garden gate and reported their answers one by one. From inside came "Correct" for each one, until the very last, which no one had solved. Wang Xun said: "'Certainly poetry has no rival.'" From inside came "Correct" for that as well. A person came out with a new lantern and swapped it for the one already hanging. From behind the screen door someone came walking out. Ziyu saw a man of about thirty, with clear brows and fine eyes and an air of lofty elegance, dressed in understated, refined clothing. He strolled over in a leisurely, graceful fashion.`,"Wenze, Zhongqing, and Wang Xun all stepped forward at once to greet him, addressing him as Master Jingyi. The man exchanged courtesies with the three, then bowed to Ziyu, who hastened to return the salutation.",`Wenze thereupon said to Xiao Cixian: "This gentleman is Mei Yuanxiang, the peerless scholar of this age. Has Master Jingyi not had occasion to meet him?" Cixian replied: "Meeting such a distinguished personage today is truly my great good fortune." He then invited the four men inside. Ziyu said: "Being in plain clothes this evening, I fear it is somewhat discourteous. Please allow me to pay a proper formal call another day." Cixian laughed: "Master Yuanxiang is the foremost distinguished scholar of the age, and such trifles should not matter. Moreover, the host is not at home, and we are simply gathering to chat informally. Please do not stand on ceremony." Ziyu found it hard to persist in declining, and had no choice but to follow him out of the pavilion. On either side, at ten-pace intervals, stood ground lanterns that illuminated a flat open space. Not far ahead rose a sheer cliff face. At the foot of the cliff was a five-bay veranda-room with carved windows and fine lattice-work. Beneath its eaves hung over twenty foreign glass lanterns of uniform style, all of the western fragrant-lotus type. Cixian invited them inside, and guest and host took their seats. He was acquainted with Wenze, Wang Xun, and Zhongqing, and had only an exchange of heartfelt admiration to make with Ziyu. Ziyu, seeing his speech proper and his bearing extraordinary, knew this was a man of distinction, and the two found themselves thoroughly congenial. After some conversation and tea, a page-boy came from the inner room and brought out prize gifts arranged on the table. There were fine Hu brushes, Hui ink-sticks, Duan inkstones, and elegant fans. Only the prize for Ziyu's solved riddle, "fallen blossoms, one person standing alone; fine rain, swallows flying in pairs", was the heaviest: a jade qin zither in an old brocade case. Ziyu saw the qin and felt a sudden vague sense of memory. Finding the prize too generous, he repeatedly declined alongside Zhongqing and the others. Cixian asked:`,`"Was it you, Master Yuanxiang, who solved the qin riddle?" Ziyu replied: "I merely guessed at random. I could not possibly presume to accept such a generous prize." Cixian replied: "This was set by the garden's master for Du Yunong, and carries a particular meaning, so pray do not decline. An inscription still needs to be engraved on the qin. I will send it once that is done." With this he had a page-boy carry the jade qin back inside, and the remaining prizes were given to each guest's attendants. The fact was that because Qin Yan loved to recite the couplet about "fallen blossoms" when Duxiang was composing the riddle, Duxiang had asked Cixian to make this a riddle with the qin as the prize, embedding within the playful game the intention of making a match. Not only did Ziyu not know who Du Yunong was, but even Zhongqing, Wenze, and the others had not been fully informed. When everyone asked, Cixian did not immediately explain, replying only that all would be known in due time.`,"They conversed idly for a time. Zhongqing remarked that it was a pity that the capital, despite being in the very season of lantern-viewing, had no southern-style novelty lanterns, a considerable diminishment of the festival's splendor.",'Cixian replied: "You gentlemen wish to see lanterns? That can easily be arranged. Though these are not from the south, they are by no means unrefined." He had a page-boy lead the way. Skirting along the sheer cliff face, they walked the length of an arrow-shot, then mounted a succession of stone steps. Climbing over thirty tiers, they turned a corner of the cliff and found themselves on a white-stone terrace on the other side.',"At the center stood a round pavilion, all of whose windows were inlaid with whole panes of glass that were concave on the inside and convex on the outside.",`Entering the pavilion, they found the floor laid with a velvet carpet. In the center stood a large round table surrounded by fan-shaped stools, fitted together in a perfect circle that precisely encircled the table. Because the night air was mild, Zhongqing and the others sat on the outer gallery railing. Page-boys brought round tea-tables, one before each guest, and tea was served. Looking upward, a hazy moon shed diffuse light, and sparse stars lay scattered. Looking downward, drifting mist hung light and vast. The void and water were both clear and fresh. One felt the spirit broadened and the heart refreshed. Looking far into the distance, one could just make out overlapping peaks and towering pavilions. They were dimly visible and unclear, and not a single lantern light was yet to be seen. Suddenly, from the Lake Tai rock cave in front of the pavilion, a pair of bright lanterns lit the way for two jade-like figures. Coming closer, one proved to be Yuan Baozhu, and the other Jin Shufang. Zhongqing asked: "Where have you been hiding?" Baozhu replied: "We were playing chess in the little boat room at the front." Wenze said: "Have you performers been keeping your eyes open?" Baozhu and Shufang both smiled. Of those present, only Ziyu did not know them. Though he had seen Shufang's 'Inscribing the Song' that day, that had been in full makeup and costume. Now he saw them in plain dress. Their flesh was fragrant and smooth, of jade-pure clarity and crystalline freshness. Baozhu stood slender as jade, seemingly too delicate to bear the weight of clothing. Ziyu thought to himself: "These two in beauty seem comparable to Qin Yan. What their characters are like, I do not know." Just as he was thinking this, he heard a cloud-gong strike once below the terrace. In the far tree-groves across the way, several shooting-star rockets rose. Immediately a succession of clay-cylinders followed, one after another, near and far, some two hundred in all. Orchid-flower bamboo-arrows shot across the garden. The green bamboo groves and winter trees were set aglow as if painted in firelight. The clay-cylinders burned for a while, and several large cannon shots were heard. Yellow smoke rose from the groves in every direction. Then a thousand firecrackers rang out in unison, and countless fireworks lit up all around. On one side were nine-linked lanterns, and on another, the Myriad Years of Joy. On one side was a cannon battering Xiangyang City, and on another, the burning of Red Lotus Temple. On one side was the Great Fire of Efang Palace, and on another, the burning of troops at the Red Cliff. In the distance, golden drums beat in a furious gallop, evoking the charge of ten thousand horses. Fire-birds and fire-mice flew like a hundred flashes of lightning, darting and weaving through the entire garden. Ziyu and the others gazed in dazzled astonishment.`,`Wenze thought to himself: "What a pity there is no wine, for we are squandering this magnificent evening of flowers and lanterns." Just then he heard Cixian say: "On such a splendid night, why do you gentlemen not drink a few cups?" He at once ordered wine to be brought. In a short while the page-boys brought four jars of wine and handed them to Baozhu and Shufang, who went before each guest, removed the teacups, and lifted a layer off the tea-tables. This revealed inlaid tiered boxes within, each containing twelve plates of fruits and dishes, with silver cups and ivory chopsticks fitted inside, all of exquisite ingenuity. Baozhu and Shufang poured the wine. Cixian said "Please!" and everyone began drinking in slow, relaxed sips. After several rounds, the cloud-gong below the terrace struck once. The fireworks in all directions died away. Then on every treetop, countless colored lanterns trembled into view. There were birds and flowers, scattered and layered, growing more and more numerous. In a short while the entire surrounding area held some several thousands, and all the lanterns in the trees had been lit. On the ground, several hundred cloud lanterns began to dance, their five colors swirling and folding, circling and spiraling. At the sound of the gong, a golden dragon emerged dancing. It was over ten zhang long, soaring and twisting like a real dragon. Presently, from within the Immortal's Grotto came a green dragon dancing, followed by a white dragon. From the groves came a black dragon, and in the light of the fireworks came a fire dragon. Each was over ten zhang long. They tumbled together while dozens of gong-beats roared like crashing waves. Their shifting smoke-clouds were truly magnificent to see. Then several dozen large and small ball-lanterns rolled out among the cloud-dragons, spinning and turning. This drew the five dragons to spread their claws and toss their horns, twisting and grasping in a frenzy. Everyone in the party was completely transfixed.`,'Suddenly a Yiyuan servant came forward announcing: "Young Master Shi has arrived!" Everyone rose to look. They saw two attendants supporting Shi Nanxiang, who came stumbling and staggering, step by halting step, up the stone stairs. Just as he reached the terrace, he suddenly heaved in a great surge of vomiting. After retching for a while, he shook his head and stood panting at the edge of the terrace, pointing at the assembled company and saying:',`"You are having such a fine time, such a fine time..." He could say no more. A servant first brought a bowl of warm water for him to rinse his mouth, then he managed: "What a fine time you are all having!" Zhongqing said: "Sit down first. Rest and then tell us." They helped him up into the pavilion, and he sat on the floor. Baozhu and the others went up to greet him, and he nodded to them. Wenze asked: "Where have you been drinking to reach this state?" Nanxiang shook his head again. Baozhu whispered a few words to Cixian. Cixian instructed a page-boy to bring a small gold box, from which he took out a pill, placed it in a bowl, dissolved it in boiling water, and gave it to Baozhu. Baozhu carried it over to Nanxiang and bent down to offer it to him. Nanxiang shook his head, refusing. Baozhu said: "This is a sobering draught. Drink it and you will be well." Nanxiang's mind was clear enough; he drank the draught, closed his eyes and said: "I am drunk and wish to sleep, so my friend may go." He lay back as if to sleep. Cixian, fearing he might catch cold, had servants carry him to a kang in the small inner room, helped Nanxiang inside, and pulled the door behind him. Ziyu asked Cixian what the pill was. Cixian replied: "It is something Duxiang himself makes. No matter how dead-drunk one may be, one pill and all the stored wine is consumed, with the vital energy additionally restored. It is called the Immortal Peach Life-Prolonging Pill."`,`Before long, they saw Nanxiang already open the door and walk out, saying: "Delightful! Delightful! I nearly became Liu Xuanshi who slept a drunken sleep of three years. I was nearly buried alive." Zhongqing said: "Your head has cleared, so why are you still talking drunken nonsense?" Shufang had wrung out a damp cloth. Nanxiang wiped his face and asked: "Where are we?" Everyone laughed. Cixian smiled and said: "Zhujun, this is Yellow Crane Tower. How can you not recognize it?" Nanxiang looked around and burst into wild laughter, saying: "So Jingyi is here too. When exactly did all of you arrive?" Everyone laughed again. Baozhu and Shufang drew him outside the pavilion and showed him around a while. Only then did Nanxiang understand they were in Yiyuan, and thinking it through carefully, he laughed loudly again. Just as he was about to ask something, the entire garden erupted. Golden drums filled the sky, explosions burst in continuous succession, and the wind was swift and fire fast. The sounds and spectacle were overwhelming. From all eight directions, a hundred beasts converged, all fashioned from five-colored silk gauze with their fur realistically painted. On one side was a cavalcade of elephant lanterns, and on another, a cavalcade of tiger lanterns. On one side was a charge of rhinoceros, and on another, a charge of lions, plus black bears, white bulls, spotted leopards, and yellow bears. Strange and wondrous, they numbered in the hundreds. Each had four small wheels beneath and was pulled by people at a run, emitting smoke from their nostrils and belching fire from their mouths. It was as thunderous and electrifying as if the earth had collapsed and the mountains fallen. Ziyu and the others were so astonished their skin rose in gooseflesh. On this side a hundred beasts, on that side five dragons, merging together with black fog rising to the sky and firelight covering the ground. It truly evoked the ferocity of the Red Cliff battle. After a long commotion, suddenly a single crack was heard, and in mid-air a nine-burst cannon was set off. In an instant the firelight below scattered like shuttles, and in a moment the whole garden fell utterly silent, with not a single lantern to be seen. The distinguished scholars all cried out in praise together: "Truly the artistry of heaven and earth, the strategy of Sun and Wu, we have never seen the like!" Zhongqing said: "Counting this one display, I calculate there must have been at least a thousand people. Where did the garden find so many people?" Cixian replied: "If one uses people for everything, of course there are many. These five dragon lanterns use people throughout. The hundred beasts and cloud lanterns use wheels to move, so one person can operate several. The beasts leading beasts require advance and retreat, fast and slow in coordination, which too requires rehearsal. Today's actual number was probably still fewer than two hundred." All the distinguished scholars were full of admiration. Cixian invited his guests down the hill to a spacious place to rest a while, and the company, reluctant to disperse so soon, followed him down the hill.`,`Threading through several grottoes fashioned to resemble immortal caverns, they followed a path lined with tree-screens and bamboo until they reached a pear-blossom garden. Cixian ushered the guests inside. Passing through several more sets of gates, they entered a five-bay Western-style building, three rooms wide and two rooms deep, facing east. The interior was appointed with extraordinary elegance. There were lacquered beds, painted writing-tables, golden tripods, and bronze vases, all pleasingly beautiful. In the center hung a plaque inscribed with three characters, 'Yichun Pavilion' or Lodge of Welcoming Spring, in the calligraphy of Qu Benli. On one side hung several panels of small-script calligraphy by Sulan, and on the other side, several panels of ink-painted orchids by Yuan Baozhu. In the middle of the floor stood a lamp imitating an ancient chicken-foot design in silver, some four feet tall. It held a nine-petal lotus lamp-basin above, burning nine wicks, which filled the whole room with bright light. Everyone sat down. Cixian said: "Why not drink a few more cups?" Everyone replied: "We have already drunk too much in the pavilion. We can do without wine, so let us have a quiet, simple gathering." Nanxiang said: "I have no idea how my drunken state cleared. What happened?" Baozhu replied:`,`"We who were sober today watched others in their cups, and that also had its charms." Nanxiang said: "Yaoqing, I recall that you poured a great bowl of wine down my throat." Everyone laughed and said: "This fellow is still befuddled from drink. How much did you actually consume in the end?" Nanxiang said: "Today I went with Gao Zhuoran and Zhang Zhongyu, bringing Wang Jingfang and Li Peixian, and spent the whole day drinking at the winehouse. I do not know how much it was; they all got drunk and left before me. I saw Jingfang home, then dropped by Yong'an's place on the way. Learning he had gone out to see the lanterns, I went too. I do not know what the carriage-driver was thinking, but he just pulled me here. The people at the garden gate said you were inside viewing the lanterns, so they helped me in." As he spoke, he pulled from his lapel a bundle of lantern-riddle strips. Everyone looked. One read 'A spring breeze song for which the embroidered silk wrapping costs a fortune', and another read 'Little horse, run quickly along'. Both were theater-title riddles, one being 'Appreciating Autumn' and the other 'Driving the Cart'. Baozhu said to Shufang with a laugh: "One is yours, one is mine, and he solved them both." Nanxiang laughed: "So you two made them!" He then turned to Ziyu and said: "Yuanxiang, what do you make of these two? Judging from their looks and talents, am I telling the truth or not? Now look at this," he pointed at the calligraphy and paintings on the walls, "this is painted by Yaoqing, and that is written by Xiangwan. Compare the outside crowd of pseudo-scholars. Can any of them match these real performers?",`,`Ziyu laughed: "I long ago made my acknowledgment, yet my elder brother still dwells on it at every moment." Nanxiang declared: "Henceforth, among your company, when you meet us you must not bow in salutation. You may only address us by our courtesy names. Violators will be penalized." Baozhu said: "This is exactly the same disposition as Duxiang and Jingyi, so it shall be." Wang Xun said: "Yuanxiang, look at this Yaoqing. How does he compare to what you saw at the theater last year? Could the real and the fake possibly be confused?" Ziyu laughed: "Can rubble and stone presume to the name of pearl and jade? That person's name should be changed." Baozhu was puzzled and asked Wang Xun. Wang Xun told the story of having seen the fake Baozhu the previous year and of Ziyu's mishearing. Baozhu gave a sweet smile.`,`Thereupon Shufang drew Wang Xun into a game of chess while Wenze looked on. Ziyu and Baozhu admired the ink-painted orchid, praising it without end. Nanxiang, Zhongqing, and Cixian sat together on the Drunken-Scholar's Couch in easy conversation. Nanxiang said: "Brother Jingyi, do you still recall those fine lines: 'Only the poet-drunkard is famed among the scholars; drunk and chanting, he may ascend Yueyang Tower'?"`,`Cixian replied: "That cannot compare to the celebrated lines: 'I only grieve that the immortals' elixir is scarce, preventing wine from filling the whole of Dongting Lake.'" Zhongqing said: "If wine filled Dongting Lake, I fear Shi Zhujun would long since have drunk himself to death. Master Jingyi, why not paint him a 'Drunken Bamboo' picture tomorrow." Cixian nodded and smiled faintly. Taking advantage of the others' conversation, Ziyu quietly asked Baozhu: "These past two days, have you seen Qin Yan from your troupe?" Baozhu, hearing this, looked Ziyu over carefully and asked: "Are you and Qin Yan close friends?" This question rather caught Ziyu off guard. Quite embarrassed, he could only answer: "We have never met. I merely admire his name." Baozhu replied: "He is no longer called Qin Guan; his name has been changed to Qin Yan. What a pity you were just a step too late today, for Duxiang took him to a dinner engagement." Ziyu thought to himself: "It is as if fate is sparing in its dealings with us. I do not even have the fortune of a proper conversation with him." As he thought, he stared absently at Baozhu, and Baozhu too was staring absently at Ziyu. Four eyes met and both fell into a trance. Liu Wenze, turning his head and seeing the scene, gave Ziyu a light tap on the shoulder and said quietly: "Is Yaoqing fine?" Ziyu, thinking he was asking about Qin Yan, replied: "His performance of 'Waking from a Dream' is straight from heaven among immortals." Baozhu gave a languid smile. Ziyu came back to himself and realized the question and answer had been mismatched, but luckily what he had said was not wrong. He followed Wenze over to Nanxiang's side. Zhongqing asked Cixian whether any fine lantern riddles had been solved by others. Cixian replied: "Yesterday there were two love-letters that a young man solved. I happened to have a matter to attend to and stepped away, and did not get his name and address." Zhongqing asked Cixian for the rough drafts of the two love-letters, and everyone read them together. One contained herb names concealed within it, the other flower names. They read:`,`"Last autumn, in the eighth month, we gathered in joy at Jin Gate. Huang first smiled and beckoned, fragrant as the oil-walled carriage that came to meet me. Though my talent is but a peck and a bushel, I am privileged to follow in your perfumed train. The road ahead stretches ten thousand li, regretting this search for rank and title. A thin shadow dwelling alone, still hoping to continue my life. Asking who governs the heart of the grass, fearing that the flower-tidings come too often to urge me on. Though powdered like a fair young gentleman, the black silk not yet old, yet the fragrant attendant's jade bones have already grown cold. Only by maintaining propriety can one protect oneself. Alone through the remaining years, enduring the winter. Counting on fingers, the gourd-harvest time is nearly upon us. This heart's bitterness is entirely dissolved. This letter arrives before you, and I hope for a brief reply. The fifteenth of the fifth month, respectfully bowing before your jade presence."`,`Ziyu exclaimed: "What a fine puzzle in the guise of a Lantern Festival riddle!" Baozhu said: "I love most the couplet about the 'powder-faced young gentleman.'" Nanxiang said: "Among us here, only Yuanxiang qualifies as a powder-faced young gentleman. Do you admire him?" Baozhu gave a little smile, and Ziyu's cheeks turned thoroughly red in embarrassment. Then they read the reply letter:`,`"A foot away, reluctant to convey its fragrance, a fragrant letter suffused with crimson. Bowels twisting as if turning back upon themselves, the year cycling round, now renewed. Remembering the joyful meeting of that former night, the farewell banquet, the parting flight. The jade maiden playing pitch-pot, faintly opening fragrant cheeks in a smile. Golden lotus steps on the ground, small steps soft in the dust. Ever since the distant search for Chang'an, only grieving over shyness and timidity. Lingering by the Luo River bank, suddenly light meets light. For you, with rouge and powder cases, your beautiful form is naturally fine. For me, already with snow-thread and frost-temples, all worldly habits forgotten. Past ninety days of spring light, a few fallen petals. Praying that through all the great Dharma-realm, lotus-pairs bloom for three lifetimes. Reckoning the jade ladle at the conjunction of yin and mao, may the jeweled box divine the starry meeting. Cut into frosty silk, wishing to send but repeatedly delayed, hovering back and forth. The sixteenth of the second month, at the yin hour, another respectful salutation is enclosed."`,`Zhongqing said: "These two love-letters, even setting aside the riddle element, are supremely fragrant and beautiful in their own right. Moreover the concealed herb names and flower names fit each context perfectly without the slightest strain. The person clever enough to solve them must truly be a peerless wit. What a pity we do not know who it was." Wenze said: "Are both these letters written in Master Jingyi's hand?" Cixian replied: "The original letter is in Duxiang's hand." As he spoke, Wang Xun had already finished his chess game, having lost three stones to Shufang. Ziyu, the night being already deep, took his leave together with Nanxiang and the others. Ziyu also conveyed that when Duxiang returned to the garden he would first pay his respects, and that he would come again in earnest on another day. Cixian replied affirmatively and saw each guest to their carriage. So they scattered. Hear what follows in the next chapter.`]},b=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations9:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{b as _,w as a,p as b,f as c,y as d,m as e,c as f,g,u as h,l as i};
